INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_61_m_61_2_46',
  'A company stores highly sensitive customer data in an Amazon S3 bucket. To comply with data protection regulations and internal security policies, they must ensure that this data is encrypted at rest. Furthermore, the encryption keys used to protect this data must be securely managed and auditable, separate from the data itself, to prevent unauthorized access even if the storage is compromised.',
  'mcq',
  '["Use AWS Key Management Service (KMS) to generate and manage encryption keys, then configure Amazon S3 to use these KMS keys for server-side encryption of objects.","Implement client-side encryption where the application encrypts data before uploading it to Amazon S3, and stores the encryption keys directly within the application''s configuration files.","Configure Amazon S3 bucket policies to restrict access to the data, relying solely on access controls instead of encryption at rest.","Store the encryption keys in the same Amazon S3 bucket alongside the encrypted data, making them easily accessible for decryption when needed."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Using AWS Key Management Service (KMS) to manage encryption keys and configuring Amazon S3 to use them for server-side encryption (Option 0) is the recommended and most secure approach for data at rest. KMS provides centralized, auditable key management with strong access controls, ensuring keys are separate from the data. Option 1 (client-side encryption with keys in config files) is an anti-pattern (ap-2.2-003) as it co-locates keys with the application, risking exposure. Option 2 (relying solely on access controls) does not protect data at rest if the bucket itself is compromised. Option 3 (storing keys alongside data in S3) is a severe anti-pattern (ap-2.2-003) that defeats the purpose of encryption.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain61_13',
  NOW(),
  50,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_61_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_61_m_61_2_47',
  'A large enterprise uses AWS Organizations to manage several development, testing, and production AWS accounts. The security team wants to enforce a strict policy across all accounts that prevents any user or role from launching specific services, such as Amazon EC2 instances in unsupported regions, regardless of the IAM permissions defined within individual accounts. They need a way to establish guardrails at the organizational level.',
  'mcq',
  '["Manually configure IAM policies in each individual AWS account to restrict the launch of unsupported services and regions.","Use AWS CloudTrail to monitor for attempts to launch prohibited services and then manually intervene to stop them.","Implement Service Control Policies (SCPs) within AWS Organizations to specify the maximum permissions available to any user or role in the affected accounts.","Deploy Amazon GuardDuty in each account to detect and alert on the launch of unauthorized services or resources."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Service Control Policies (SCPs) within AWS Organizations (Option 2) are designed to establish guardrails and define the maximum available permissions for all accounts in an organization. SCPs can prevent any user or role from performing prohibited actions, even if their IAM policies would otherwise allow it (ap-2.2-004). Option 0 (manual IAM policy configuration) is labor-intensive, prone to errors, and does not provide a centralized, enforced guardrail. Option 1 (CloudTrail monitoring and manual intervention) is reactive and does not prevent the action. Option 3 (Amazon GuardDuty) is for threat detection, not for enforcing preventive controls on service usage.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain61_13',
  NOW(),
  51,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_61_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_61_m_61_2_48',
  'A software company is preparing for a compliance audit, specifically for PCI DSS (Payment Card Industry Data Security Standard). The auditors have requested documentation proving that AWS''s infrastructure meets certain security and compliance standards. The company needs to access official AWS compliance reports, certifications, and attestations to provide to the auditors as evidence of AWS''s security posture.',
  'mcq',
  '["Search the AWS Management Console for a general security overview document that summarizes AWS''s compliance programs.","Contact AWS Support directly to request copies of specific compliance reports and certifications for their auditors.","Browse the AWS Security Blog for articles and announcements related to AWS compliance initiatives and certifications.","Access AWS Artifact, a self-service portal for on-demand access to AWS security and compliance reports and agreements."]'::json,
  '{3}',
  'AWS Artifact (Option 3) is the dedicated self-service portal for on-demand access to AWS security and compliance reports, such as SOC reports, PCI DSS attestations, and ISO certifications, as well as online agreements. This is the authoritative source for providing auditors with evidence of AWS''s compliance (ap-2.2-001 is about customer''s responsibility, but accessing AWS''s reports is the first step). Option 0 (searching the console) is too vague and unlikely to yield official reports. Option 1 (contacting AWS Support) is unnecessary as Artifact is self-service. Option 2 (AWS Security Blog) provides general information but not the formal reports required for an audit.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain61_13',
  NOW(),
  52,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_61_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────
-- Module: m_61_1
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_61_m_61_1_26',
  'A company is using Amazon EC2 instances to host its application. Under the AWS shared responsibility model, which security task is explicitly the responsibility of the CUSTOMER?',
  'mcq',
  '["Managing the physical security of the data centers where EC2 instances run.","Patching the underlying hypervisor software that hosts the EC2 instances.","Configuring the operating system, applications, and security patches on the EC2 instances.","Maintaining the global network infrastructure that connects AWS Regions."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'For IaaS services like Amazon EC2, the customer is responsible for ''security IN the cloud.'' This includes configuring the guest operating system, installing application software, and applying security patches to the operating system and applications. This directly addresses anti-pattern [ap-2.1-002]. AWS is responsible for ''security OF the cloud,'' which includes physical security of data centers (Option 0), patching the underlying hypervisor (Option 1), and maintaining the global network infrastructure (Option 3).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain61_13',
  NOW(),
  53,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_61_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_61_m_61_1_27',
  'A development team is building a serverless application using AWS Lambda functions. They are focusing on writing secure code and managing access permissions for their functions. The team needs to understand AWS''s role in securing the underlying compute environment where their Lambda functions execute, specifically concerning the physical infrastructure and runtime environment.',
  'mcq',
  '["The customer is responsible for securing the underlying operating system and physical servers that host AWS Lambda functions.","AWS is responsible for the security of the underlying infrastructure, including the physical servers, network, and the Lambda runtime environment, while the customer is responsible for their code and data.","The customer and AWS share responsibility equally for all aspects of the Lambda environment, including patching the underlying host.","AWS Lambda functions do not run on physical servers, so there is no underlying infrastructure to secure."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'For serverless services like AWS Lambda, the shared responsibility model shifts significantly. AWS is responsible for the security OF the cloud, which includes the underlying infrastructure (physical servers, network, hypervisor) and the Lambda runtime environment. The customer is responsible for security IN the cloud, which includes their function code, data, and IAM permissions (ap-2.1-003). Option 0 incorrectly places infrastructure responsibility on the customer. Option 2 misrepresents the division of responsibility for serverless. Option 3 is factually incorrect; Lambda functions run on AWS-managed compute infrastructure.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain61_13',
  NOW(),
  54,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_61_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_61_m_61_1_28',
  'A company is migrating its sensitive customer data to Amazon S3. They understand that while AWS provides robust physical security for its data centers, they have a critical role in protecting their data within the cloud. The primary concern is ensuring that the data stored in Amazon S3 is encrypted and that only authorized personnel can access it.',
  'mcq',
  '["Assume that AWS automatically encrypts all data stored in Amazon S3 and manages access control by default.","Rely on AWS to handle all security aspects, including data encryption and access control, as part of the shared responsibility model.","The customer is responsible for implementing data encryption for objects stored in Amazon S3 and configuring appropriate IAM policies and bucket policies for access control.","Delegate all data security responsibilities to a third-party security vendor, as AWS does not allow customers to manage their own data encryption."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Under the AWS shared responsibility model, the customer is responsible for security IN the cloud. This includes customer data (e.g., in Amazon S3), which means the customer is responsible for implementing data encryption and configuring access controls using IAM policies and Amazon S3 bucket policies (ap-2.1-001). While Amazon S3 offers default encryption options, it is the customer''s explicit responsibility to enable and manage them. Options 0 and 1 represent the anti-pattern of assuming AWS handles all security, including customer data encryption and access control. Option 3 is incorrect; customers are fully empowered to manage their data security.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain61_13',
  NOW(),
  55,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_61_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_61_m_61_1_29',
  'A company has deployed several Amazon EC2 instances within a Virtual Private Cloud (VPC) to host their internal applications. They need to control network traffic flow to and from these individual instances, ensuring that only necessary ports are open and specific IP addresses are allowed to connect. The security team needs to identify the AWS-provided mechanism that operates at the instance level to manage this traffic.',
  'mcq',
  '["Configure Network Access Control Lists (NACLs) at the subnet level to filter traffic for all instances within that subnet.","Implement AWS WAF to protect the web applications running on the EC2 instances from common web exploits.","Use AWS Firewall Manager to centrally manage firewall rules across multiple AWS accounts and VPCs.","Configure security groups for each Amazon EC2 instance to act as a virtual firewall, controlling inbound and outbound traffic at the instance level."]'::json,
  '{3}',
  'Security groups (Option 3) act as a virtual firewall for Amazon EC2 instances, controlling inbound and outbound traffic at the instance level. They are stateful and allow customers to specify rules for allowed protocols, ports, and source/destination IP addresses, which is a key customer responsibility under the shared responsibility model (ap-2.1-004). Option 0 (NACLs) operate at the subnet level and are stateless, providing a different layer of defense. Option 1 (AWS WAF) is a web application firewall that protects against web exploits, not a general instance-level network control. Option 2 (AWS Firewall Manager) is for centralizing management of various firewall rules, not the specific mechanism for instance-level traffic control.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain61_13',
  NOW(),
  56,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_61_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

