) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_60_m_60_4_33',
  'A development team is creating a new application that consists of several independent microservices. They want to ensure that these services can be developed, deployed, and scaled independently without affecting each other. Which AWS design principle is crucial for achieving this modularity and preventing cascading failures?',
  'mcq',
  '["Implementing elasticity to scale each microservice based on its individual demand.","Designing for failure to ensure each microservice is resilient to internal issues.","Decoupling components to allow independent operation and fault isolation.","Thinking parallel to process requests concurrently within each microservice."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Decoupling components is a fundamental design principle for microservices architectures. It ensures that services are loosely coupled, allowing them to be developed, deployed, and scaled independently. This also isolates failures, preventing an issue in one service from impacting others. Implementing elasticity (Option 0) is for scaling. Designing for failure (Option 1) is for resilience within a component. Thinking parallel (Option 3) is for concurrency. Decoupling directly addresses the modularity and fault isolation between microservices.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain60_13',
  NOW(),
  26,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_60_4',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_60_m_60_4_34',
  'A company is modernizing its IT operations and wants to adopt a highly automated approach to infrastructure management. They aim to reduce manual configuration, ensure consistency across environments, and enable rapid, repeatable deployments. Which AWS design principle is most central to achieving these operational goals?',
  'mcq',
  '["Designing for failure, to build resilient systems that can withstand outages.","Implementing elasticity, to automatically scale resources based on demand.","The importance of automation, enabling infrastructure as code and continuous delivery.","Decoupling components, to allow independent scaling and fault isolation."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'The importance of automation is a core principle of operational excellence in the cloud. It involves using infrastructure as code, continuous integration/continuous delivery (CI/CD), and automated monitoring to reduce manual effort, ensure consistency, and enable rapid, repeatable deployments. Designing for failure (Option 0) focuses on reliability. Implementing elasticity (Option 1) focuses on performance efficiency/cost optimization. Decoupling components (Option 3) focuses on reliability/performance. Automation is the most central principle for achieving the stated operational goals.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain60_13',
  NOW(),
  27,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_60_4',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_60_m_60_4_35',
  'A media streaming company needs to ensure its video delivery service remains available even if an entire data center experiences an outage. Their current architecture relies on a single physical location, which poses a significant risk to their service continuity. They want to adopt an AWS design principle to enhance resilience and minimize service disruption.',
  'mcq',
  '["Deploying the application across multiple AWS Regions to achieve the highest level of geographic redundancy.","Utilizing a single Availability Zone within an AWS Region and relying on automated backups for recovery.","Consolidating all application components onto a single, powerful Amazon EC2 instance to simplify management and reduce potential points of failure.","Designing the application to distribute resources and data across multiple Availability Zones within an AWS Region."]'::json,
  '{3}',
  'The correct answer is designing the application to distribute resources and data across multiple Availability Zones within an AWS Region. This aligns with the AWS Well-Architected Framework''s reliability pillar and the ''design for failure'' principle, ensuring that if one Availability Zone experiences an outage, the application remains available in another. Option A, deploying across multiple AWS Regions, provides even higher geographic redundancy but is typically for disaster recovery from a regional outage, not solely a data center outage within a region. Option B, utilizing a single Availability Zone, creates a single point of failure and contradicts the principle of designing for failure (Anti-pattern: ap-1.2-004). Option C, consolidating components onto a single Amazon EC2 instance, also creates a single point of failure and reduces resilience (Anti-pattern: ap-1.2-004).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain60_13',
  NOW(),
  28,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_60_4',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_60_m_60_4_36',
  'An online retail company experiences predictable spikes in traffic during holiday sales events, with demand increasing tenfold for several weeks. Outside of these periods, traffic is much lower. They need an infrastructure that can automatically scale resources up and down to match demand, avoiding over-provisioning during low periods or under-provisioning during peak times.',
  'mcq',
  '["Designing the system to automatically acquire and release compute capacity as needed, scaling resources up during peak times and down during low periods.","Manually adding and removing Amazon EC2 instances based on daily traffic forecasts.","Implementing a fixed infrastructure size that can handle the maximum expected peak load at all times.","Using a single, large database instance to store all customer data, ensuring all data is centrally managed for simplicity."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'The correct answer is designing the system to automatically acquire and release compute capacity as needed, scaling resources up during peak times and down during low periods. This is the definition of elasticity, a core design principle of the AWS Cloud, which optimizes resource utilization and cost. Option B, manually adding and removing instances, is inefficient and prone to human error, contradicting the benefits of automation and elasticity (Anti-pattern: ap-1.2-001). Option C, implementing a fixed infrastructure for peak load, leads to significant over-provisioning and wasted costs during off-peak times (Anti-pattern: ap-1.1-004). Option D, using a single large database, addresses data storage but not the compute capacity elasticity required for fluctuating application traffic.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain60_13',
  NOW(),
  29,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_60_4',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_60_m_60_4_37',
  'A software development team is building a new microservices-based application where different services need to communicate without direct dependencies that could cause cascading failures. They want to ensure that a failure in one service does not impact the availability or performance of other services, maintaining overall system stability.',
  'mcq',
  '["Using a centralized monolithic application architecture where all components run on a single server for simplified deployment.","Designing components to interact asynchronously through message queues or event streams, allowing them to operate independently.","Implementing direct API calls between all microservices to ensure immediate communication and data consistency.","Deploying each microservice to a separate AWS Region to isolate them geographically and reduce the risk of regional outages."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'The correct answer is designing components to interact asynchronously through message queues or event streams, allowing them to operate independently. This is a key design principle of the AWS Well-Architected Framework, promoting loose coupling between components. Loose coupling prevents a failure in one service from directly impacting others, enhancing overall system reliability. Option A, using a centralized monolithic architecture, inherently creates tight coupling and a single point of failure (Anti-pattern: ap-1.2-002). Option C, implementing direct API calls between all microservices, can lead to tight coupling and cascading failures if not managed carefully (Anti-pattern: ap-1.2-002). Option D, deploying each microservice to a separate AWS Region, provides geographic isolation but does not directly address the architectural principle of decoupling components within the application itself.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain60_13',
  NOW(),
  30,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_60_4',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────
-- Module: m_60_3
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_60_m_60_3_26',
  'A traditional enterprise is considering migrating its entire data center to AWS. Currently, they invest heavily in purchasing physical servers, storage arrays, and networking gear every few years, along with significant ongoing expenses for data center space, power, cooling, and hardware maintenance. The finance department wants to understand how AWS Cloud adoption could fundamentally alter their expenditure model and potentially reduce the overall financial burden over time. Which core economic benefit of AWS Cloud computing would be most relevant to their financial strategy?',
  'mcq',
  '["Eliminating all data transfer costs by keeping resources within a single region.","Guaranteeing a fixed monthly infrastructure cost regardless of resource usage.","Trading capital expenditure (CapEx) for variable expenditure (OpEx) with a pay-as-you-go model.","Increasing the need for dedicated IT staff to manage physical hardware and infrastructure."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'A fundamental economic benefit of AWS Cloud computing is the ability to trade capital expenditure (CapEx) for variable expenditure (OpEx). Instead of large upfront investments in hardware, companies pay only for the computing resources they consume, on a pay-as-you-go basis, which aligns costs directly with usage and can significantly reduce Total Cost of Ownership (TCO). Option 0 (Eliminating all data transfer costs) is incorrect; data transfer costs exist, especially for egress traffic, and are a factor in cloud economics. Option 1 (Guaranteeing a fixed monthly infrastructure cost) is incorrect and represents an anti-pattern (ap-1.1-004) as cloud costs are variable based on usage, though Reserved Instances or Savings Plans can provide predictability for consistent workloads. Option 3 (Increasing the need for dedicated IT staff) is generally incorrect; cloud adoption often shifts IT staff focus from infrastructure maintenance to higher-value activities, potentially reducing the need for hardware-focused roles.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain60_13',
  NOW(),
  31,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_60_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_60_m_60_3_27',
  'A mid-sized media company currently manages its video rendering farm on-premises. This involves significant upfront capital expenditure (CapEx) for high-performance servers, which are often idle during off-peak hours or between projects. The finance department wants to reduce large initial investments and instead align infrastructure costs more closely with actual production demand, transforming fixed costs into variable operational expenses. They also seek to avoid the long procurement cycles associated with hardware refreshes. Which economic benefit of the AWS Cloud directly supports this financial strategy?',
  'mcq',
  '["Trading fixed expense for variable expense by paying only for the compute resources consumed.","Benefiting from massive economies of scale, which ensures that cloud resources are always the cheapest option available.","Eliminating the need for any IT staff, as AWS manages all operational aspects of the infrastructure.","The ability to deploy infrastructure globally in minutes, enabling rapid expansion into new markets."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option 0 (Trading fixed expense for variable expense) is the correct answer. This core economic benefit of cloud computing allows companies to convert large upfront capital expenditures (CapEx) for hardware into flexible operational expenses (OpEx) that scale with actual usage, directly addressing the company''s goal. Option 1 (Economies of scale) is a benefit, but it does not guarantee that cloud resources are *always* the cheapest option (anti-pattern ap-1.1-001), and it''s not the primary mechanism for shifting CapEx to OpEx. Option 2 (Eliminating the need for IT staff) is an overstatement and a misconception; while AWS reduces operational burden, IT staff are still needed for cloud architecture, management, and optimization. Option 3 (Global deployment) is a benefit of AWS but does not directly address the financial strategy of converting CapEx to OpEx.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain60_13',
  NOW(),
  32,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_60_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_60_m_60_3_28',
  'A startup is launching a new application and needs to keep initial infrastructure costs as low as possible while having the ability to scale rapidly if the application becomes popular. They want to avoid large upfront investments in hardware. Which aspect of cloud economics is most beneficial for this scenario?',
  'mcq',
  '["The ability to benefit from massive economies of scale, reducing the cost per unit.","The trade of capital expenditure (CapEx) for variable expenditure (OpEx).","The high availability and fault tolerance of the AWS global infrastructure.","Increased speed and agility in provisioning new resources for development teams."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Trading capital expenditure (CapEx) for variable expenditure (OpEx) means paying for compute resources only when you consume them, instead of large upfront investments. This is highly beneficial for startups as it minimizes initial costs and allows them to scale without significant financial risk. Economies of scale (Option 0) contribute to lower prices but don''t describe the shift from CapEx to OpEx. High availability (Option 2) is a reliability benefit. Speed and agility (Option 3) is an operational benefit. The CapEx to OpEx shift directly addresses the financial objective of avoiding large upfront investments.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain60_13',
  NOW(),
  33,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_60_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_60_m_60_3_29',
  'A company is migrating its on-premises data warehouse to AWS. Historically, they''ve purchased servers with excess capacity to handle peak loads, leading to significant underutilization during off-peak times. They want to optimize costs in the cloud by ensuring they only pay for the compute and storage resources they actually use. Which cloud economics principle should they leverage?',
  'mcq',
  '["The benefit of massive economies of scale, reducing the cost of services over time.","The ability to deploy globally in minutes, enhancing data accessibility.","The pay-as-you-go pricing model, paying only for consumed resources.","Increased speed and agility, allowing rapid provisioning of new data environments."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'The pay-as-you-go pricing model is a fundamental principle of cloud economics where customers pay only for the individual services they use, for as long as they use them, without long-term contracts or complex licensing. This directly addresses the company''s goal of avoiding over-provisioning and only paying for actual consumption. Economies of scale (Option 0) explain why AWS prices are low but not the consumption-based model itself. Deploying globally (Option 1) is a geographic benefit. Speed and agility (Option 3) is an operational benefit.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain60_13',
  NOW(),
  34,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_60_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_60_m_60_3_30',
  'An organization is evaluating the total cost of ownership (TCO) for their current on-premises infrastructure compared to a potential migration to AWS. They need to account for all direct and indirect costs, including hardware, software, data center space, power, cooling, and IT labor. Which AWS tool is specifically designed to help estimate and compare these costs?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Well-Architected Tool, for reviewing architectural best practices.","AWS Cloud Adoption Framework (AWS CAF), for guiding cloud transformation.","AWS Pricing Calculator, for estimating the costs of AWS services.","AWS Migration Hub, for tracking the progress of migration projects."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'The AWS Pricing Calculator is the dedicated tool for estimating the costs of using AWS services and performing TCO comparisons between on-premises and AWS environments. It allows users to input their current infrastructure details and receive an estimated cost breakdown for AWS. The AWS Well-Architected Tool (Option 0) is for architectural reviews. AWS Cloud Adoption Framework (Option 1) is for strategic guidance. AWS Migration Hub (Option 3) is for migration tracking.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain60_13',
  NOW(),
  35,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_60_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_60_m_60_3_31',
  'A company is running several development and testing environments on AWS that are only needed during business hours (9 AM to 5 PM, Monday to Friday). However, these environments are currently running 24/7. The finance department wants to reduce unnecessary cloud spending. Which cloud economics principle should be applied to optimize costs in this scenario?',
  'mcq',
  '["Leveraging massive economies of scale to benefit from lower infrastructure costs.","Designing for high availability across multiple Availability Zones to ensure uptime.","Implementing the pay-as-you-go model by shutting down unused resources.","Increasing speed and agility to provision new resources quickly for testing."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Implementing the pay-as-you-go model effectively means paying only for what you use. By shutting down development and testing environments when they are not in use, the company can avoid unnecessary charges, directly applying the pay-as-you-go principle to optimize costs. This addresses anti-pattern [ap-1.1-004] (Overlooking pay-as-you-go pricing by keeping resources always on). Economies of scale (Option 0) are a general cost benefit. High availability (Option 1) is a reliability principle. Speed and agility (Option 3) is an operational benefit.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain60_13',
  NOW(),
  36,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_60_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_60_m_60_3_32',
  'An enterprise is migrating its applications to AWS. They are particularly interested in reducing their overall IT spending by leveraging the lower infrastructure costs that a large cloud provider can offer due to its purchasing power and efficient data center operations. Which cloud economics concept best describes this cost advantage?',
  'mcq',
  '["Elasticity, allowing resources to scale up and down with demand.","High availability, ensuring applications remain operational during failures.","Economies of scale, where costs per unit decrease as usage increases.","The pay-as-you-go model, paying only for consumed resources."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Economies of scale refer to the cost advantages that large-scale cloud providers like AWS can achieve due to their massive infrastructure, purchasing power, and operational efficiencies. These savings are passed on to customers, resulting in lower prices for services. Elasticity (Option 0) is about dynamic scaling. High availability (Option 1) is about resilience. The pay-as-you-go model (Option 3) describes the billing method, but economies of scale explain the underlying reason for the lower unit costs. This directly addresses the cost advantage from a large cloud provider.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain60_13',
  NOW(),
  37,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_60_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_60_m_60_3_33',
  'A company is considering moving its on-premises virtual machines to AWS. They want to understand the potential cost savings and financial benefits, including how the cloud model can reduce their need for upfront hardware purchases and ongoing maintenance. Which AWS service or tool can help them analyze these financial aspects?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Well-Architected Tool, to improve the security and reliability of their architecture.","AWS Cloud Adoption Framework (AWS CAF), to guide their organizational cloud journey.","AWS Pricing Calculator, to estimate the costs of running their VMs on AWS and compare TCO.","AWS Migration Hub, to orchestrate and track the migration of their applications."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'The AWS Pricing Calculator is designed to help users estimate the costs of AWS services and perform Total Cost of Ownership (TCO) comparisons. It allows the company to model the cost of running their virtual machines on AWS, including compute, storage, and networking, and compare it against their current on-premises expenses, addressing their need to analyze financial benefits. AWS Well-Architected Tool (Option 0) is for architectural reviews. AWS Cloud Adoption Framework (Option 1) is for strategic guidance. AWS Migration Hub (Option 3) is for migration tracking.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain60_13',
  NOW(),
  38,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_60_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_60_m_60_3_34',
  'A traditional IT department is struggling with the overhead of managing physical servers, including procurement, installation, patching, and decommissioning. They are considering migrating to AWS to offload these undifferentiated heavy lifting tasks. Which cloud economics benefit will allow their IT staff to focus on more strategic business initiatives?',
  'mcq',
  '["The ability to benefit from massive economies of scale, reducing the cost of services.","The high availability and fault tolerance of the AWS global infrastructure.","Reduced operational overhead by leveraging managed services and automation.","The elasticity to automatically scale resources up or down based on demand."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Reduced operational overhead is a significant cloud economics benefit. By moving to AWS and utilizing managed services, the responsibility for managing physical infrastructure, patching, and other undifferentiated heavy lifting tasks shifts to AWS. This frees up the IT staff to focus on higher-value, strategic business initiatives. Economies of scale (Option 0) are a general cost benefit. High availability (Option 1) is a reliability benefit. Elasticity (Option 3) is a scalability benefit. Reduced operational overhead directly addresses the goal of offloading infrastructure management.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain60_13',
  NOW(),
  39,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_60_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_60_m_60_3_35',
  'A company is planning to migrate its entire data center to AWS. They want to calculate the potential cost savings by comparing their current expenses (hardware, software, power, cooling, facilities, IT staff) with the estimated costs of running the same workloads on AWS. Which AWS tool should they use for this comprehensive financial analysis?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Well-Architected Tool, to ensure their cloud architecture is optimized.","AWS Cloud Adoption Framework (AWS CAF), to define their migration strategy.","AWS Pricing Calculator, to perform a Total Cost of Ownership (TCO) analysis.","AWS Migration Hub, to track the progress of their migration project."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'The AWS Pricing Calculator is the appropriate tool for performing a Total Cost of Ownership (TCO) analysis. It allows organizations to input their current on-premises costs and compare them with estimated AWS costs, providing a comprehensive financial comparison that includes all relevant expense categories. AWS Well-Architected Tool (Option 0) is for architectural reviews. AWS Cloud Adoption Framework (Option 1) is for strategic guidance. AWS Migration Hub (Option 3) is for migration tracking.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain60_13',
  NOW(),
  40,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_60_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_60_m_60_3_36',
  'A growing startup needs to deploy new application environments frequently for development and testing. They want to avoid the capital expenditure of purchasing new servers each time and instead prefer a model where they pay only for the compute resources they consume, without long-term commitments. Which cloud economics principle is most relevant to this requirement?',
  'mcq',
  '["The ability to benefit from massive economies of scale, leading to lower unit costs.","The high availability and fault tolerance provided by AWS global infrastructure.","The pay-as-you-go pricing model, eliminating upfront costs and long-term contracts.","Increased speed and agility in provisioning new resources for development."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'The pay-as-you-go pricing model allows the startup to avoid upfront capital expenditures and long-term commitments, paying only for the compute resources they actually consume. This directly addresses their need to deploy new environments frequently without large initial investments. Economies of scale (Option 0) contribute to lower prices but don''t define the payment model. High availability (Option 1) is a reliability benefit. Speed and agility (Option 3) is an operational benefit. The pay-as-you-go model is the most direct answer to the financial requirement.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain60_13',
  NOW(),
  41,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_60_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_60_m_60_3_37',
  'A company is running an application that experiences predictable spikes in demand, such as during end-of-month reporting. They currently over-provision their on-premises servers to handle these peaks, leading to significant idle capacity during other times. They want to migrate to AWS to pay only for the resources they need, when they need them. Which cloud economics principle directly supports this goal?',
  'mcq',
  '["The ability to benefit from massive economies of scale, reducing the cost per unit.","The high availability and fault tolerance of the AWS global infrastructure.","The elasticity of resources, allowing dynamic scaling to match demand and optimize costs.","The shift from capital expenditure (CapEx) to operational expenditure (OpEx)."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Elasticity, combined with the pay-as-you-go model, allows the company to dynamically scale resources up during peak demand and scale them down during off-peak times. This ensures they only pay for the resources consumed, directly addressing the goal of avoiding over-provisioning and optimizing costs. Economies of scale (Option 0) contribute to lower prices. High availability (Option 1) is a reliability benefit. The CapEx to OpEx shift (Option 3) is a financial model change, but elasticity is the mechanism for dynamic cost optimization based on usage.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain60_13',
  NOW(),
  42,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_60_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_60_m_60_3_38',
  'A small business is launching its first online service and needs to minimize upfront infrastructure costs. They also want the flexibility to easily scale their resources up or down as their customer base grows or shrinks, without being locked into long-term contracts. Which core principle of cloud economics is most advantageous for this business?',
  'mcq',
  '["The benefit of massive economies of scale, leading to lower pricing for all services.","The ability to deploy globally in minutes, reaching a wider audience instantly.","The pay-as-you-go pricing model, avoiding upfront costs and offering flexible scaling.","Increased speed and agility in provisioning new resources for rapid development."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'The pay-as-you-go pricing model is a fundamental principle of cloud economics that allows businesses to pay only for the resources they consume, without upfront costs or long-term commitments. This provides the flexibility to scale resources as needed, making it ideal for a small business looking to minimize initial expenses and adapt to changing demand. Economies of scale (Option 0) explain lower prices. Deploying globally (Option 1) is a geographic benefit. Speed and agility (Option 3) is an operational benefit. The pay-as-you-go model directly addresses the financial and flexibility needs.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain60_13',
  NOW(),
  43,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_60_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_60_m_60_3_39',
  'An organization is migrating its data center to AWS and wants to ensure they are making informed decisions about resource allocation to avoid unnecessary costs. They need to analyze their current resource utilization and match it with appropriate AWS services and instance types. Which cloud economics principle is crucial for achieving this cost efficiency?',
  'mcq',
  '["The benefit of massive economies of scale, reducing the cost per unit of infrastructure.","The high availability and fault tolerance of the AWS global infrastructure.","Right-sizing resources, by matching capacity with actual demand.","The shift from capital expenditure (CapEx) to operational expenditure (OpEx)."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Right-sizing resources is a key cloud economics principle focused on optimizing costs by matching the capacity of AWS services and instance types to the actual performance and utilization requirements of workloads. This prevents over-provisioning and ensures the organization only pays for what is truly needed, directly supporting cost efficiency. Economies of scale (Option 0) are a general cost benefit. High availability (Option 1) is a reliability benefit. The CapEx to OpEx shift (Option 3) is a financial model change. Right-sizing directly addresses efficient resource allocation.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain60_13',
  NOW(),
