INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_62_m_62_1_19',
  'A small startup is developing a new web application that needs to be highly available and scalable, but they have a limited budget and minimal operational staff. They want to deploy their application quickly without spending time on server provisioning, patching, or operating system management. The application''s traffic is expected to fluctuate significantly, requiring automatic scaling capabilities. Which AWS service model and corresponding deployment option would provide the LEAST operational overhead for this scenario?',
  'mcq',
  '["Infrastructure as a Service (IaaS) using Amazon EC2 instances with Auto Scaling.","Platform as a Service (PaaS) using AWS Elastic Beanstalk.","Software as a Service (SaaS) using Amazon WorkMail.","Infrastructure as Code (IaC) using AWS CloudFormation templates for EC2."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'AWS Elastic Beanstalk is a Platform as a Service (PaaS) offering that handles the deployment, provisioning, load balancing, auto-scaling, and health monitoring of applications, significantly reducing operational overhead. The user only needs to upload their application code. Option A (IaaS with Amazon EC2) requires the user to manage the operating system, patching, and more, which increases operational overhead (anti-pattern ap-3.1-002). Option C (SaaS with Amazon WorkMail) is a completely different service (email) and not suitable for deploying a custom web application. Option D (IaC with AWS CloudFormation) is a method for deploying infrastructure, but it doesn''t reduce the operational overhead of managing the underlying servers if they are EC2 instances, it just automates their provisioning (anti-pattern ap-3.1-003).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain62_13',
  NOW(),
  1,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_62_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_62_m_62_1_20',
  'A company is planning to migrate its existing on-premises application to the AWS Cloud. The application requires a dedicated, private network connection to AWS with consistent network performance and high bandwidth, due to large data transfers and strict latency requirements for real-time processing. They want to avoid sending sensitive data over the public internet. Which AWS connectivity option should they choose to meet these requirements MOST effectively?',
  'mcq',
  '["Establish a site-to-site VPN connection over the internet.","Utilize the public internet connection with increased bandwidth.","Implement AWS Direct Connect to create a dedicated network connection.","Configure a VPC peering connection between their on-premises network and AWS."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'AWS Direct Connect provides a dedicated, private network connection from an on-premises data center to AWS, offering consistent network performance, reduced bandwidth costs, and enhanced security compared to internet-based connections. This is ideal for high-throughput, latency-sensitive workloads. Option A (VPN connection) traverses the public internet, which can introduce variable latency and bandwidth limitations, making it less suitable for strict performance requirements (anti-pattern ap-3.5-003). Option B (public internet) does not provide a private connection and offers no guarantees on performance or security. Option D (VPC peering) is used to connect two VPCs within AWS, not an on-premises network to AWS.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain62_13',
  NOW(),
  2,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_62_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_62_m_62_1_21',
  'A development team frequently deploys new versions of their application and infrastructure components. They want to ensure that deployments are consistent, repeatable, and version-controlled, minimizing the risk of human error. The team needs a programmatic way to define and provision all the necessary AWS resources, such as Amazon EC2 instances, Amazon RDS databases, and networking configurations, as a single unit. Which AWS service would best support this requirement for automated and consistent infrastructure deployments?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Management Console for manual resource creation.","AWS CloudFormation to define infrastructure as code.","AWS CLI for scripting individual resource commands.","AWS SDKs for application-level interactions with AWS services."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'AWS CloudFormation allows users to define their AWS infrastructure as code in templates, enabling consistent, repeatable, and version-controlled deployments. It provisions and updates resources in an orderly and predictable manner. Option A (AWS Management Console) involves manual configuration, which is error-prone, not repeatable, and not auditable for production environments (anti-pattern ap-3.1-001). Option C (AWS CLI) allows scripting, but it''s typically for individual commands and doesn''t manage the entire stack''s lifecycle or dependencies as comprehensively as CloudFormation. Option D (AWS SDKs) are primarily for application code to interact with AWS services, not for defining and provisioning infrastructure.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain62_13',
  NOW(),
  3,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_62_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_62_m_62_1_22',
  'A company is considering extending its on-premises data center capabilities to include AWS services, while keeping certain applications and data physically located on-premises due to regulatory compliance and ultra-low latency requirements. They need to run AWS compute and storage services locally, with seamless integration to the broader AWS Cloud. Which AWS offering is specifically designed to provide AWS infrastructure and services on-premises?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Regions for global infrastructure deployment.","AWS Local Zones for extending AWS services to metropolitan areas.","AWS Outposts for running AWS infrastructure on-premises.","AWS Wavelength for mobile edge computing applications."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'AWS Outposts brings native AWS services, infrastructure, and operating models to virtually any on-premises facility. This allows companies to run AWS compute and storage locally while seamlessly connecting to the broader AWS Cloud, addressing requirements for local data processing and ultra-low latency (anti-pattern ap-3.1-004). Option A (AWS Regions) are fully isolated geographic areas for deploying cloud resources, not on-premises. Option B (AWS Local Zones) extend AWS infrastructure to metropolitan areas but are still AWS-managed data centers, not customer on-premises. Option D (AWS Wavelength) embeds AWS compute and storage services within 5G networks for mobile edge applications, which is different from a customer''s private data center.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain62_13',
  NOW(),
  4,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_62_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_62_m_62_1_23',
  'A small development team needs to deploy a simple web application quickly. They want to focus entirely on their application code and minimize any server or infrastructure management tasks. The application is expected to have predictable, moderate traffic. They are looking for a service that provides an easy-to-use platform for deploying and scaling web applications and services, abstracting away the underlying infrastructure. Which AWS service would best fit this requirement for simplicity and reduced management overhead?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon EC2, manually configuring instances and web servers.","AWS Elastic Beanstalk, providing a platform for application deployment.","AWS CloudFormation, defining infrastructure as code for granular control.","AWS Lambda, for event-driven, serverless function execution."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'AWS Elastic Beanstalk is a Platform as a Service (PaaS) that simplifies the deployment and management of applications by handling the underlying infrastructure, including provisioning EC2 instances, load balancing, and auto-scaling. This allows the team to focus on their code. Option A (Amazon EC2) requires significant manual server management, which is the opposite of the stated goal of minimizing management tasks (anti-pattern ap-3.1-002). Option C (AWS CloudFormation) is an Infrastructure as Code service for provisioning resources, not a platform for direct application deployment and management. Option D (AWS Lambda) is a serverless compute service for event-driven functions, not typically used for hosting an entire traditional web application with predictable, moderate traffic without additional services.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain62_13',
  NOW(),
  5,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_62_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────
-- Module: m_62_2
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_62_m_62_2_16',
  'A company is designing a new application that requires high availability and fault tolerance. They want to ensure that if a natural disaster or power outage affects a single data center, their application remains operational. The application needs to distribute its resources across isolated locations within a single geographic region to achieve this resilience. Which AWS global infrastructure component is designed to provide this level of isolation and redundancy within an AWS Region?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Edge Locations for content delivery.","AWS Regions for geographic isolation.","AWS Availability Zones for fault isolation.","AWS Local Zones for extending services to metropolitan areas."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'AWS Availability Zones (AZs) are distinct, isolated locations within an AWS Region, designed to be independent of failures in other AZs. Deploying resources across multiple AZs ensures high availability and fault tolerance, as an outage in one AZ will not affect resources in another. Option A (AWS Edge Locations) are used for content delivery and caching, not for hosting primary application resources for high availability (anti-pattern ap-3.2-003). Option B (AWS Regions) provide geographic isolation, but AZs provide fault isolation within a single region. Option D (AWS Local Zones) extend AWS services to metropolitan areas but are not the primary mechanism for fault isolation within a standard Region.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain62_13',
  NOW(),
  6,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_62_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_62_m_62_2_17',
  'A global e-commerce company wants to improve the performance and reduce the latency for its customers accessing their website from various parts of the world. They host their main application servers in a single AWS Region. To deliver static content, such as images and videos, faster to users, they need a service that caches content closer to the end-users. Which AWS global infrastructure component and service combination would best achieve this goal?',
  'mcq',
  '["Deploying additional application servers in multiple AWS Regions.","Utilizing AWS Local Zones to extend compute closer to users.","Leveraging Amazon CloudFront with its global network of Edge Locations.","Configuring AWS Outposts to run content delivery services on-premises."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Amazon CloudFront is a content delivery network (CDN) that uses a global network of Edge Locations to cache content closer to end-users, significantly reducing latency and improving performance for static and dynamic content. Option A (deploying more servers in multiple Regions) would reduce latency for dynamic content but is more complex and expensive than caching static content. Option B (AWS Local Zones) extends AWS services to metropolitan areas but is not primarily designed for global content caching. Option D (AWS Outposts) brings AWS infrastructure on-premises and is not suitable for global content delivery (anti-pattern ap-3.2-003).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain62_13',
  NOW(),
  7,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_62_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_62_m_62_2_18',
  'A company is expanding its operations and needs to choose a new AWS Region for deploying a critical application. The application processes sensitive customer data and must comply with specific data residency regulations that mandate data to remain within a particular country. Additionally, the company wants to ensure low latency for its primary user base located in that country. Which factor should be the PRIMARY consideration when selecting the AWS Region in this scenario?',
  'mcq',
  '["The Region with the lowest overall cost for services.","The Region that offers the widest range of AWS services.","Compliance with data residency regulations and latency to users.","The Region with the most Availability Zones for maximum fault tolerance."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'When choosing an AWS Region, compliance with data residency regulations and latency to the primary user base are critical factors, especially for sensitive data and performance-sensitive applications. Regulatory requirements often dictate where data can be stored. Option A (lowest cost) is a valid consideration but should not override compliance or latency for critical applications (anti-pattern ap-3.2-002). Option B (widest range of services) is generally good but secondary to compliance and latency. Option D (most Availability Zones) is important for high availability within a region, but Region choice itself is about geographic and regulatory alignment.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain62_13',
  NOW(),
  8,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_62_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_62_m_62_2_19',
  'A financial services company is migrating its applications to AWS. Due to strict regulatory requirements, certain data and applications must remain on-premises, but they want to leverage AWS for bursting capacity and disaster recovery. They need a way to seamlessly extend their on-premises environment with AWS infrastructure, allowing applications to run across both environments with consistent APIs and tools. Which AWS offering is best suited for this hybrid cloud strategy?',
  'mcq',
  '["Deploying all applications to a single AWS Region and connecting via VPN.","Using AWS Outposts to run AWS infrastructure in their on-premises data center.","Utilizing AWS Local Zones to extend AWS services to nearby metropolitan areas.","Setting up a multi-Region architecture for disaster recovery without on-premises integration."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'AWS Outposts allows customers to run native AWS services on-premises, providing a truly consistent hybrid experience. This enables seamless integration between on-premises and AWS cloud environments, crucial for meeting regulatory requirements while leveraging cloud benefits. Option A (single AWS Region with VPN) does not bring AWS infrastructure on-premises, limiting the hybrid capability. Option C (AWS Local Zones) extends AWS to metropolitan areas, but the infrastructure is still managed by AWS, not within the customer''s data center. Option D (multi-Region architecture) is for cloud-to-cloud disaster recovery and doesn''t address the need to keep components on-premises.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain62_13',
  NOW(),
  9,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_62_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_62_m_62_2_20',
  'A media company hosts a popular streaming service on AWS. They are experiencing high traffic during peak hours, leading to increased latency for users in distant geographic locations. The company wants to ensure that content, especially frequently accessed videos, is delivered with the lowest possible latency to their global audience. They need a solution that caches content close to users worldwide. Which AWS service would effectively address this challenge?',
  'mcq',
  '["Deploying Amazon EC2 instances in every AWS Region globally.","Using Amazon S3 for storing all video content.","Implementing Amazon CloudFront to distribute content via Edge Locations.","Configuring Amazon Route 53 with latency-based routing policies."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Amazon CloudFront is a content delivery network (CDN) that caches content at Edge Locations around the world, reducing latency by serving content closer to the end-users. This is ideal for a global streaming service. Option A (deploying EC2 in every Region) would be very expensive and complex for content delivery, and not primarily a caching solution. Option B (Amazon S3) is an object storage service, but by itself, it does not provide global caching at edge locations to reduce latency for distant users (anti-pattern ap-3.5-005). Option D (Amazon Route 53) provides DNS services and can route traffic based on latency, but it doesn''t cache content itself; it directs users to the nearest server, which could still be far without a CDN.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain62_13',
  NOW(),
  10,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_62_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────
-- Module: m_62_7
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_62_m_62_7_16',
  'A marketing company frequently processes large batches of customer feedback documents to extract sentiment and key phrases. This process is sporadic, running only a few times a week, and involves a large volume of text data. The company wants to automate this analysis without needing to provision or manage any servers, and they prefer a pay-per-use model. Which AWS service would be the MOST cost-effective and operationally efficient solution for this task?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon Rekognition for image and video analysis.","Amazon Comprehend for natural language processing.","Amazon Polly for text-to-speech conversion.","Amazon Lex for building conversational interfaces."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Amazon Comprehend is an AI service that uses natural language processing (NLP) to uncover insights and relationships in text, such as sentiment analysis and key phrase extraction. It is a fully managed service, meaning there are no servers to provision or manage, and it operates on a pay-per-use model, making it cost-effective for sporadic batch processing. Option A (Amazon Rekognition) is for image and video analysis, not text (anti-pattern ap-3.7-001). Option C (Amazon Polly) converts text to speech. Option D (Amazon Lex) is for building conversational interfaces (chatbots).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain62_13',
  NOW(),
  11,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_62_7',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_62_m_62_7_17',
  'A company wants to build an internal dashboard to visualize key business metrics from various AWS data sources, including Amazon RDS and Amazon S3. The dashboard needs to be interactive, provide insights, and be accessible to business users without requiring extensive technical knowledge. They are looking for a fully managed, serverless business intelligence (BI) service to minimize development and maintenance effort. Which AWS service is designed for this purpose?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon Kinesis for real-time data streaming.","Amazon QuickSight for business intelligence dashboards.","Amazon SageMaker for machine learning model development.","AWS Glue for data integration and ETL jobs."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Amazon QuickSight is a scalable, serverless, cloud-powered business intelligence (BI) service that makes it easy to create and publish interactive dashboards with ML-powered insights. It integrates with various AWS data sources and minimizes development and maintenance effort (anti-pattern ap-3.7-004). Option A (Amazon Kinesis) is for real-time data streaming. Option C (Amazon SageMaker) is for building, training, and deploying machine learning models. Option D (AWS Glue) is an ETL service for preparing data for analytics.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain62_13',
  NOW(),
  12,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_62_7',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_62_m_62_7_18',
  'A manufacturing plant uses IoT sensors to collect real-time data from machinery. This data stream needs to be ingested, processed, and analyzed continuously to monitor equipment health and predict potential failures. The volume of data is high and continuous, requiring a solution capable of handling real-time streaming data with low latency. Which AWS service is purpose-built for collecting, processing, and analyzing real-time streaming data?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon S3 for object storage of sensor data.","Amazon Kinesis for real-time data streaming.","Amazon Redshift for batch data warehousing.","AWS Glue for batch ETL processing."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Amazon Kinesis is a fully managed service for real-time processing of large streams of data. It is designed for ingesting, processing, and analyzing streaming data with low latency, making it ideal for IoT sensor data. Option A (Amazon S3) is an object storage service and not optimized for real-time streaming ingestion. Option C (Amazon Redshift) is a data warehouse for analytical queries on large datasets, typically used for batch processing, not real-time streaming (anti-pattern ap-3.7-003). Option D (AWS Glue) is an ETL service, primarily for batch processing, although it can work with streaming sources, Kinesis is the core streaming ingestion service.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain62_13',
  NOW(),
  13,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_62_7',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_62_m_62_7_19',
  'A startup is developing a new mobile application that requires image recognition capabilities to identify objects within user-uploaded photos. The development team has limited machine learning expertise and wants to integrate this functionality quickly and cost-effectively, without building and training custom ML models from scratch. Which AWS AI service would allow them to add image analysis features with minimal effort?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon SageMaker for custom ML model development.","Amazon Rekognition for pre-trained image and video analysis.","Amazon Comprehend for natural language processing.","Amazon Textract for document text and data extraction."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Amazon Rekognition is a fully managed AI service that provides pre-trained computer vision capabilities, including object and scene detection in images and videos, via API calls. This allows the startup to integrate image recognition without deep ML expertise or custom model training (anti-pattern ap-3.7-001). Option A (Amazon SageMaker) is for building, training, and deploying custom ML models, which requires ML expertise. Option C (Amazon Comprehend) is for natural language processing. Option D (Amazon Textract) is for extracting text and data from documents.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain62_13',
  NOW(),
  14,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_62_7',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────
-- Module: m_62_3
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_62_m_62_3_16',
  'A development team is building a new microservices-based application using containers. They want to deploy and manage these containers without having to provision, patch, or manage the underlying Amazon EC2 instances that host the containers. The team prioritizes operational simplicity and a serverless-like experience for their containerized workloads. Which AWS compute service should they choose to run their containers with the LEAST operational overhead?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon EC2 instances with Docker installed manually.","Amazon Elastic Kubernetes Service (Amazon EKS) on self-managed EC2 instances.","AWS Fargate, a serverless compute engine for containers.","Amazon Elastic Container Service (Amazon ECS) on EC2 instances managed by the user."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'AWS Fargate is a serverless compute engine for Amazon ECS and Amazon EKS that allows you to run containers without having to provision, manage, or scale the underlying EC2 instances. This significantly reduces operational overhead and provides a serverless experience for containers (anti-pattern ap-3.3-005). Option A (manual Docker on EC2) requires full server management. Options B (EKS on self-managed EC2) and D (ECS on user-managed EC2) still require managing the EC2 instances that serve as container hosts.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain62_13',
  NOW(),
  15,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_62_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_62_m_62_3_17',
  'A company has a batch processing job that runs nightly. This job can tolerate interruptions and needs to be completed within a flexible time window. The primary goal is to minimize compute costs as much as possible, even if it means the job might occasionally restart. Which Amazon EC2 pricing option would be the MOST cost-effective for this type of workload?',
  'mcq',
  '["On-Demand Instances for predictable pricing.","Reserved Instances for long-term, steady-state workloads.","Spot Instances for fault-tolerant, flexible workloads.","Savings Plans for a flexible commitment to compute usage."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Amazon EC2 Spot Instances allow you to bid for unused EC2 capacity, offering significant cost savings (up to 90% off On-Demand prices) compared to On-Demand instances. They are ideal for fault-tolerant, flexible, and interruption-tolerant workloads like batch processing. Option A (On-Demand Instances) is the most expensive option. Option B (Reserved Instances) are for steady-state, predictable workloads over 1-3 years, not flexible batch jobs. Option D (Savings Plans) offer discounts based on a commitment to spend a certain amount per hour, but Spot Instances still provide the deepest discounts for interruptible workloads (anti-pattern ap-3.3-002).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain62_13',
  NOW(),
  16,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_62_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_62_m_62_3_18',
  'A small business is launching a new marketing campaign that is expected to generate a massive, but short-lived, spike in traffic to their website. The website is hosted on a fleet of Amazon EC2 instances. To ensure the website remains responsive and available during this event, traffic needs to be distributed efficiently across all healthy instances, and unhealthy instances should be automatically removed from service. Which AWS service is designed to distribute incoming application traffic across multiple targets, such as EC2 instances?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon Route 53 for DNS management.","Amazon CloudFront for content delivery.","Elastic Load Balancing (ELB) for traffic distribution.","Amazon S3 for static website hosting."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Elastic Load Balancing (ELB) automatically distributes incoming application traffic across multiple targets, such as Amazon EC2 instances, in multiple Availability Zones. It also monitors the health of its registered targets and routes traffic only to healthy instances, ensuring high availability and fault tolerance. Option A (Amazon Route 53) is a DNS service. Option B (Amazon CloudFront) is a content delivery network. Option D (Amazon S3) is an object storage service, which can host static websites but doesn''t distribute traffic to dynamic EC2 instances.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain62_13',
  NOW(),
  17,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_62_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────
-- Module: m_62_4
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_62_m_62_4_12',
  'A rapidly growing e-commerce company needs a database solution for its product catalog. The catalog contains millions of items, each with a flexible and evolving schema (e.g., different attributes for clothing vs. electronics). The application requires very low-latency access (single-digit milliseconds) to individual items and needs to scale to handle millions of requests per second. Which AWS database service is purpose-built for highly variable, key-value, and document data with massive scale and performance requirements?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon RDS for a managed relational database.","Amazon DynamoDB for a managed NoSQL database.","Amazon Aurora for a high-performance relational database.","Amazon Redshift for a data warehousing solution."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Amazon DynamoDB is a fully managed NoSQL database service that provides fast and flexible single-digit millisecond performance at any scale. It is ideal for applications requiring a flexible schema, high throughput, and low latency, such as product catalogs with varying attributes (anti-pattern ap-3.4-001). Option A (Amazon RDS) and Option C (Amazon Aurora) are relational databases that enforce rigid schemas and are less suitable for highly variable data. Option D (Amazon Redshift) is a data warehouse optimized for analytical queries, not for transactional key-value lookups.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain62_13',
  NOW(),
  18,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_62_4',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_62_m_62_4_13',
  'A company is migrating its legacy on-premises MySQL database to AWS. They need a fully managed relational database service that automates administrative tasks like backups, patching, and replication. The solution must also provide high availability with automatic failover and the ability to easily create read replicas for scaling read-heavy workloads. Which AWS database service would best meet these requirements while minimizing operational overhead?',
  'mcq',
  '["Deploying MySQL on an Amazon EC2 instance and self-managing it.","Using Amazon RDS for a managed relational database.","Implementing Amazon DynamoDB for a NoSQL database.","Configuring Amazon Redshift for data warehousing."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Amazon RDS (Relational Database Service) is a fully managed service that makes it easy to set up, operate, and scale a relational database in the cloud. It automates time-consuming administration tasks like hardware provisioning, database setup, patching, and backups, and supports high availability with Multi-AZ deployments and read replicas. Option A (self-managing MySQL on EC2) would require significant operational effort for backups, patching, and replication (anti-pattern ap-3.4-002). Option C (Amazon DynamoDB) is a NoSQL database, not suitable for a MySQL migration. Option D (Amazon Redshift) is a data warehouse, not a transactional relational database.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain62_13',
  NOW(),
  19,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_62_4',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_62_m_62_4_14',
  'A web application experiences intermittent spikes in read traffic, leading to increased load on its primary Amazon RDS database and occasional performance bottlenecks. The company wants to improve the application''s responsiveness for read-heavy operations without scaling up the primary database instance. They need a solution that can cache frequently accessed data in-memory to provide microsecond-latency responses. Which AWS service would effectively offload read traffic from the database and improve read performance?',
  'mcq',
  '["Adding more storage to the Amazon RDS primary instance.","Implementing Amazon ElastiCache for in-memory caching.","Migrating the database to Amazon DynamoDB for better read performance.","Using Amazon Redshift to handle read-heavy analytical queries."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Amazon ElastiCache is a fully managed in-memory caching service that supports Redis and Memcached. It is designed to provide microsecond-latency responses for frequently accessed data, significantly offloading read traffic from the primary database and improving application responsiveness (anti-pattern ap-3.4-004). Option A (adding more storage) would not directly address read performance bottlenecks caused by high query load. Option C (migrating to DynamoDB) is a significant architectural change to a NoSQL database, not a direct solution for offloading reads from an existing relational database. Option D (Amazon Redshift) is for data warehousing and analytics, not for transactional caching.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain62_13',
  NOW(),
  20,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_62_4',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_62_m_62_4_15',
  'A large enterprise needs to analyze petabytes of historical sales data to identify long-term trends, perform complex aggregations, and generate quarterly business intelligence reports. The data is stored in various formats and requires a highly scalable, columnar database optimized for analytical queries. They are looking for a fully managed service that can handle massive datasets and complex queries efficiently. Which AWS database service is purpose-built for large-scale data warehousing and analytics?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon RDS for transactional relational workloads.","Amazon DynamoDB for NoSQL key-value data.","Amazon Redshift for data warehousing and analytics.","Amazon ElastiCache for in-memory caching."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Amazon Redshift is a fully managed, petabyte-scale data warehousing service optimized for analytical workloads. It uses columnar storage and parallel processing to perform complex queries on large datasets efficiently, making it ideal for business intelligence and reporting. Option A (Amazon RDS) and Option B (Amazon DynamoDB) are designed for online transaction processing (OLTP) and operational data, not for large-scale analytical queries (anti-pattern ap-3.4-003). Option D (Amazon ElastiCache) is an in-memory caching service, not a data warehouse.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain62_13',
  NOW(),
  21,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_62_4',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_62_m_62_4_16',
  'A company is building a new application that requires a MongoDB-compatible database. They want a fully managed, highly available, and scalable document database service that is compatible with their existing MongoDB drivers and tools. The solution needs to automate backups, patching, and replication to minimize operational overhead. Which AWS database service would be the most appropriate choice for this requirement?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL.","Amazon DynamoDB.","Amazon DocumentDB (with MongoDB compatibility).","Amazon Aurora MySQL-compatible edition."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Amazon DocumentDB (with MongoDB compatibility) is a fast, scalable, highly available, and fully managed document database service that supports MongoDB workloads. It allows users to run MongoDB applications on AWS without managing the underlying infrastructure. Option A (Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL) and Option D (Amazon Aurora MySQL-compatible edition) are relational databases. Option B (Amazon DynamoDB) is a NoSQL key-value and document database but is not MongoDB-compatible.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain62_13',
  NOW(),
  22,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_62_4',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────
-- Module: m_62_5
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_62_m_62_5_16',
  'A company is deploying a multi-tier web application in an Amazon VPC. The application consists of web servers that need to be accessible from the internet and database servers that should only be accessible by the web servers and not directly from the internet. The database servers also need to initiate outbound connections to download software updates. Which networking components should be used to securely configure the database subnet and allow outbound internet access?',
  'mcq',
  '["Place database servers in a public subnet with an Internet Gateway.","Place database servers in a private subnet with a NAT Gateway.","Place database servers in a public subnet with a VPN connection.","Place database servers in a private subnet with a VPC Endpoint."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'To secure database servers, they should be placed in a private subnet, which means they do not have direct internet access. A NAT Gateway in a public subnet allows instances in private subnets to initiate outbound connections to the internet while preventing inbound connections from the internet (anti-pattern ap-3.5-001). Option A (public subnet with Internet Gateway) would expose the database servers directly to the internet, which is a security risk. Option C (public subnet with VPN) is incorrect as VPNs are for connecting to on-premises networks, and public subnets are still exposed. Option D (VPC Endpoint) is for privately connecting to AWS services, not for general outbound internet access.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain62_13',
  NOW(),
  23,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_62_5',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_62_m_62_5_17',
  'A security team is reviewing the network configuration for an application running on Amazon EC2 instances within a VPC. They need to ensure that only specific inbound and outbound traffic is allowed to individual EC2 instances, and that these rules are stateful (meaning return traffic is automatically allowed). They also want to apply these rules at the instance level. Which AWS networking security feature is best suited for this granular, stateful, instance-level traffic filtering?',
  'mcq',
  '["Network Access Control Lists (Network ACLs) at the subnet level.","Security Groups at the instance level.","VPC Flow Logs for monitoring network traffic.","AWS WAF for web application firewall protection."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Security Groups act as a virtual firewall for your EC2 instances, controlling inbound and outbound traffic at the instance level. They are stateful, meaning if you allow an inbound request, the outbound response is automatically allowed, and vice versa. Option A (Network ACLs) are stateless and operate at the subnet level, requiring explicit rules for both inbound and outbound traffic (anti-pattern ap-3.5-002). Option C (VPC Flow Logs) are for monitoring traffic, not controlling it. Option D (AWS WAF) is a web application firewall for HTTP/S traffic, not for instance-level network filtering.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain62_13',
  NOW(),
  24,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_62_5',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_62_m_62_5_18',
  'A company is hosting a public-facing web application that serves customers globally. They want to ensure high availability and low latency for their users by routing traffic to the closest healthy application endpoint. If an endpoint becomes unhealthy, traffic should automatically be directed to a healthy alternative. Which AWS service provides robust DNS management and health checks to achieve this global routing and failover capability?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon CloudFront for content delivery.","Elastic Load Balancing (ELB) for distributing traffic within a Region.","Amazon Route 53 for DNS with health checks and routing policies.","AWS VPN for secure connections to on-premises networks."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Amazon Route 53 is a highly available and scalable cloud Domain Name System (DNS) web service. It can perform health checks on registered resources and route traffic to healthy endpoints based on various routing policies (e.g., latency-based, failover, weighted), ensuring high availability and low latency for global applications (anti-pattern ap-3.5-004). Option A (Amazon CloudFront) is a CDN for caching content. Option B (Elastic Load Balancing) distributes traffic, but typically within a Region or across multiple AZs, and Route 53 is often used in conjunction with it for global routing. Option D (AWS VPN) is for secure network connections, not DNS-based traffic management.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain62_13',
  NOW(),
  25,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_62_5',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_62_m_62_5_19',
  'A company needs to connect its on-premises data center to its AWS VPC. The connection must be secure, encrypted, and established over the public internet. They have a moderate amount of data to transfer and are looking for a cost-effective solution that is relatively quick to set up. Which AWS networking service would be most suitable for creating this type of encrypted connection?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Direct Connect for a dedicated private connection.","AWS VPN for an encrypted connection over the internet.","VPC peering to connect two VPCs.","AWS Transit Gateway for connecting multiple VPCs and on-premises networks."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'AWS VPN (Virtual Private Network) allows you to establish a secure, encrypted connection between your on-premises network and your Amazon VPC over the public internet. It is a cost-effective and relatively quick solution for moderate data transfer needs. Option A (AWS Direct Connect) provides a dedicated private connection, which is more expensive and takes longer to set up, typically used for high-throughput, latency-sensitive workloads (anti-pattern ap-3.5-003). Option C (VPC peering) connects two VPCs within AWS. Option D (AWS Transit Gateway) is for connecting multiple VPCs and on-premises networks, which is a more advanced scenario than a single site-to-site connection.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain62_13',
  NOW(),
  26,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_62_5',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_62_m_62_5_20',
  'A company is hosting a static website, including HTML, CSS, JavaScript files, and images. They want a highly available, durable, and cost-effective storage solution that can also directly host their static website content. The solution should be able to scale automatically to handle varying traffic loads without requiring server management. Which AWS service is ideal for storing static website content and hosting static websites directly?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon EBS volumes attached to an EC2 instance.","Amazon S3 for object storage with static website hosting.","Amazon EFS for shared file storage.","Amazon RDS for storing website content in a database."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Amazon S3 (Simple Storage Service) is an object storage service that provides high durability, availability, and scalability. It can directly host static websites, serving HTML, CSS, JavaScript, and image files over HTTP/S, making it a cost-effective and serverless solution for static content. Option A (Amazon EBS) provides block storage for EC2 instances, not suitable for direct static website hosting. Option C (Amazon EFS) is a shared file system, not designed for public static website hosting. Option D (Amazon RDS) is a relational database, not suitable for storing static website files.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain62_13',
  NOW(),
  27,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_62_5',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────
-- Module: m_62_6
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_62_m_62_6_16',
  'A media company stores large video files that are frequently accessed by users for the first 30 days after upload, but then become infrequently accessed archival content. They need a storage solution that automatically optimizes costs by moving data to cheaper storage tiers as its access pattern changes, without manual intervention. The data must remain readily available when needed. Which Amazon S3 storage class is designed for automatic cost optimization based on changing access patterns?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon S3 Standard for frequently accessed data.","Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering for automatic cost optimization.","Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive for long-term archival.","Amazon S3 Standard-IA for infrequently accessed data."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering is an S3 storage class that automatically moves objects between two access tiers (frequently accessed and infrequently accessed) when access patterns change, optimizing storage costs without performance impact or operational overhead. This matches the requirement for automatic cost optimization based on changing access patterns. Option A (S3 Standard) is for frequently accessed data and does not automatically tier. Option C (S3 Glacier Deep Archive) is for long-term, rarely accessed archival data with retrieval times in hours (anti-pattern ap-3.6-005). Option D (S3 Standard-IA) is for infrequently accessed data but requires manual lifecycle policies to transition data.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain62_13',
  NOW(),
  28,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_62_6',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_62_m_62_6_17',
  'A company''s engineering team needs a shared file system that can be mounted by thousands of Amazon EC2 instances simultaneously. These instances run a high-performance computing (HPC) application that requires concurrent access to the same dataset. The file system must be fully managed, scalable, and highly available across multiple Availability Zones. Which AWS storage service is designed to provide shared file storage for multiple EC2 instances?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon EBS volumes attached to individual EC2 instances.","Amazon S3 buckets for object storage.","Amazon EFS for shared file storage.","AWS Storage Gateway for hybrid cloud storage."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Amazon EFS (Elastic File System) provides a simple, scalable, elastic file system for use with AWS Cloud services and on-premises resources. It can be mounted by thousands of EC2 instances concurrently, making it ideal for shared file access in HPC workloads (anti-pattern ap-3.6-002). Option A (Amazon EBS) volumes can typically only be attached to a single EC2 instance at a time (with Multi-Attach for io1/io2 being an exception, but not for thousands of instances). Option B (Amazon S3) is object storage, not a file system that can be mounted directly. Option D (AWS Storage Gateway) is for hybrid cloud storage, not for shared file access across multiple EC2 instances within AWS.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain62_13',
  NOW(),
  29,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_62_6',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_62_m_62_6_18',
  'A large enterprise has petabytes of on-premises archival data that needs to be migrated to Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive for long-term, low-cost storage. Transferring this volume of data over the internet would take an unacceptably long time and consume significant network bandwidth. They need an efficient and secure method for offline data transfer. Which AWS service family is designed for physical data transfer of large datasets into AWS?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Storage Gateway for connecting on-premises storage to AWS.","AWS Snow Family (Snowcone, Snowball, Snowmobile) for offline data migration.","Amazon S3 Transfer Acceleration for faster internet transfers.","AWS Direct Connect for a dedicated network connection."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'The AWS Snow Family (including Snowcone, Snowball, and Snowmobile) provides physical devices for securely transferring very large amounts of data (petabytes to exabytes) into and out of AWS. This is significantly faster and more cost-effective than transferring petabytes of data over the internet (anti-pattern ap-3.6-003). Option A (AWS Storage Gateway) provides hybrid cloud storage but not for large-scale offline migration. Option C (S3 Transfer Acceleration) speeds up internet transfers but is not suitable for petabyte-scale migrations that would take weeks or months. Option D (AWS Direct Connect) provides a dedicated network, but even with high bandwidth, petabytes of data can still take a very long time to transfer.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain62_13',
  NOW(),
  30,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_62_6',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_62_m_62_6_19',
  'A company stores critical business documents in an Amazon S3 bucket. They need to protect these documents from accidental deletion or modification, and also ensure that older versions of documents are retained for compliance purposes. Additionally, they want to automatically move older, less frequently accessed versions to lower-cost storage tiers after a certain period. Which Amazon S3 features should be enabled to meet these data protection and cost optimization requirements?',
  'mcq',
  '["Enable S3 Transfer Acceleration and S3 Block Public Access.","Enable S3 Versioning and S3 Lifecycle Policies.","Configure S3 Cross-Region Replication and S3 Event Notifications.","Apply S3 Object Lock and S3 Storage Class Analysis."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Amazon S3 Versioning keeps multiple versions of an object in the same bucket, protecting against accidental deletions and overwrites. Amazon S3 Lifecycle Policies automate the transition of objects to different storage classes (e.g., S3 Standard-IA, Glacier) or their expiration after a defined period, optimizing costs. Together, they provide data protection and cost optimization (anti-pattern ap-3.6-004). Option A (Transfer Acceleration and Block Public Access) are for performance and security, not versioning or lifecycle management. Option C (Cross-Region Replication and Event Notifications) are for data replication and event triggers. Option D (Object Lock and Storage Class Analysis) are for immutability and analysis, not general versioning and tiering.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain62_13',
  NOW(),
  31,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_62_6',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────
-- Module: m_62_8
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_62_m_62_8_16',
  'A company needs to send critical notifications to its customers via email and SMS when certain events occur in their application, such as order confirmations or security alerts. They require a highly available, scalable, and fully managed messaging service that can deliver messages to multiple subscribers and endpoints. Which AWS service is designed for sending messages to a large number of subscribers through various protocols?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon SQS for message queuing.","Amazon SNS for publish/subscribe messaging.","AWS Step Functions for orchestrating workflows.","Amazon EventBridge for event bus routing."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Amazon SNS (Simple Notification Service) is a fully managed messaging service that enables you to send messages to a large number of subscribers and endpoints, including email, SMS, and AWS Lambda functions, using a publish/subscribe model. This is ideal for critical notifications. Option A (Amazon SQS) is a message queuing service for decoupling application components, not for direct notifications to users. Option C (AWS Step Functions) is for orchestrating serverless workflows. Option D (Amazon EventBridge) is an event bus service for routing events between AWS services and SaaS applications.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain62_13',
  NOW(),
  32,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_62_8',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_62_m_62_8_17',
  'A microservices-based application needs to decouple its components to improve fault tolerance and scalability. When a user places an order, the order processing service should send a message to a queue, and multiple downstream services (e.g., inventory, payment, shipping) can then asynchronously process these messages at their own pace. The company needs a fully managed, highly available message queuing service. Which AWS service would be most suitable for this asynchronous communication pattern?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon SNS for fan-out notifications.","Amazon SQS for message queuing.","AWS Lambda for serverless compute.","Amazon Kinesis for real-time streaming data."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Amazon SQS (Simple Queue Service) is a fully managed message queuing service that enables you to decouple and scale microservices, distributed systems, and serverless applications. It allows messages to be stored in a queue until they are processed by a consumer, ensuring asynchronous communication and fault tolerance. Option A (Amazon SNS) is a publish/subscribe service for fan-out notifications, not primarily for decoupling services with a queue. Option C (AWS Lambda) is a compute service, not a messaging service. Option D (Amazon Kinesis) is for real-time data streaming, which is different from a message queue for asynchronous task processing.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain62_13',
  NOW(),
  33,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_62_8',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_62_m_62_8_18',
  'A company is building a new application that involves a complex, multi-step process, such as processing an order, fulfilling it, and then notifying the customer. Each step in this process is implemented as a separate AWS Lambda function. They need a way to coordinate these Lambda functions and other AWS services into a serverless workflow, handling state management, error handling, and retries automatically. Which AWS service is designed for orchestrating serverless workflows?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon SQS for message passing between steps.","AWS Step Functions for orchestrating serverless workflows.","Amazon SNS for broadcasting status updates.","Amazon EventBridge for routing events between services."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'AWS Step Functions is a serverless workflow service that lets you combine AWS Lambda functions and other AWS services to build business-critical applications. It provides visual workflows, manages state, handles errors, and retries, making it ideal for orchestrating complex, multi-step processes. Option A (Amazon SQS) can pass messages but doesn''t orchestrate a workflow with state management. Option C (Amazon SNS) is for notifications. Option D (Amazon EventBridge) is for routing events, not orchestrating a sequence of steps with state.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain62_13',
  NOW(),
  34,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_62_8',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_62_m_62_8_19',
  'A company is developing a new IoT solution that involves thousands of connected devices sending telemetry data to the cloud. They need a scalable and secure way to connect these devices to AWS, ingest their data, and manage them. The solution should support various device protocols and enable two-way communication between the devices and the cloud. Which AWS service is purpose-built for connecting, managing, and interacting with IoT devices?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon Kinesis for real-time data streaming.","AWS IoT Core for connecting and managing IoT devices.","Amazon SQS for message queuing from devices.","AWS Lambda for processing device data."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'AWS IoT Core is a fully managed cloud service that lets connected devices easily and securely interact with cloud applications and other devices. It supports various device protocols, enables two-way communication, and handles device authentication and authorization, making it ideal for managing large fleets of IoT devices. Option A (Amazon Kinesis) is for real-time data streaming, often used *with* IoT Core but not for device management itself. Option C (Amazon SQS) is a message queue, not designed for direct device connectivity and management. Option D (AWS Lambda) is a compute service for processing data, typically triggered by IoT Core rules.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain62_13',
  NOW(),
  35,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_62_8',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

