INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_1_36',
  'A development team is building a new serverless application using AWS Lambda functions that need to securely interact with Amazon S3 buckets to store and retrieve user-generated content. The team wants to ensure that the Lambda functions have only the necessary permissions to perform their tasks and avoid exposing long-term credentials. They are looking for a solution that adheres to the principle of least privilege and minimizes credential management overhead. Which approach should the solutions architect recommend for granting access?',
  'mcq',
  '["Create an IAM user with programmatic access keys for each Lambda function and embed these keys as environment variables within the function code.","Assign an IAM role to the AWS Lambda function with a policy granting specific permissions to the required Amazon S3 buckets.","Generate temporary security credentials using AWS STS AssumeRole API calls within the Lambda function code for each S3 operation.","Configure a resource-based policy directly on the Amazon S3 buckets to allow access from any AWS Lambda function in the account."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Assigning an IAM role to the AWS Lambda function is the recommended and most secure approach. This provides temporary credentials automatically to the function, eliminating the need to manage long-term access keys and adhering to the principle of least privilege. Option A (embedding access keys) is an anti-pattern (ap-1.1-002) as it risks exposure and makes key rotation difficult. Option C (manual STS AssumeRole calls) adds unnecessary complexity and operational overhead to the Lambda function code, which is typically managed by the service itself. Option D (resource-based policy on S3) would grant overly broad access to any Lambda function, violating the principle of least privilege, and is not the primary mechanism for granting service-to-service permissions from an invoking service.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  1,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_1_37',
  'A company is migrating its on-premises user directory to AWS and wants to provide a centralized identity management solution for its employees to access various AWS accounts and applications. The solution must simplify the sign-in experience for users, allow administrators to manage permissions centrally, and integrate with their existing Microsoft Active Directory. The company prioritizes minimizing administrative overhead for identity federation. Which AWS service should the solutions architect recommend?',
  'mcq',
  '["Configure AWS IAM Identity Center (successor to AWS SSO) and connect it to the existing Microsoft Active Directory.","Create individual IAM users in each AWS account and manually synchronize their credentials with the on-premises directory.","Set up an Amazon Cognito User Pool for employee authentication and integrate it with each AWS account.","Implement a custom identity broker application running on Amazon EC2 instances to federate access to AWS accounts."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'AWS IAM Identity Center (successor to AWS SSO) is designed for centralized access management to multiple AWS accounts and business applications, and it supports integration with existing Microsoft Active Directory for simplified user management and a single sign-on experience. This minimizes administrative overhead. Option B (individual IAM users) is highly unscalable and creates significant management burden. Option C (Amazon Cognito User Pool) is primarily for customer-facing applications, not for internal employee access to AWS accounts, and would require additional integration for authorization (ap-1.1-004). Option D (custom identity broker) introduces significant operational overhead for development, maintenance, and security, which contradicts the goal of minimizing administrative overhead.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  2,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_1_38',
  'A company has several AWS accounts managed under AWS Organizations. They need to ensure that all IAM users and roles created within any member account adhere to a strict security policy that prevents them from creating resources in unsupported AWS Regions. The security team wants to enforce this centrally without requiring manual configuration in each individual account. Which AWS Organizations feature should the solutions architect leverage to meet this requirement with the LEAST administrative effort?',
  'mcq',
  '["Apply an IAM policy to each individual IAM user and role in every member account to restrict Region access.","Configure a Service Control Policy (SCP) at the Organizational Unit (OU) level to deny actions in specified AWS Regions.","Use AWS Config rules in each member account to detect and report non-compliant resource creation in unsupported Regions.","Create a custom AWS Lambda function to monitor CloudTrail logs for resource creation in unauthorized Regions and revert them."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Configuring a Service Control Policy (SCP) at the Organizational Unit (OU) level is the most efficient way to centrally enforce Region restrictions across multiple accounts in AWS Organizations. SCPs are preventive controls that apply to all IAM users and roles within the affected accounts, minimizing administrative effort. Option A (individual IAM policies) is highly manual and error-prone across many accounts. Option C (AWS Config rules) provides detection but not prevention, allowing non-compliant actions to occur before reporting. Option D (custom Lambda function) introduces significant operational overhead for development and maintenance, and also acts reactively rather than preventively.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  3,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_1_39',
  'An application running on Amazon EC2 instances needs to access a private Amazon RDS database instance. The security team has identified a requirement to explicitly deny specific IP addresses from accessing the database, even if they are within the allowed range of the security group. The solution must provide a stateless filtering mechanism at the subnet level for defense-in-depth. Which networking construct should the solutions architect use to implement this explicit deny rule?',
  'mcq',
  '["Modify the security group associated with the Amazon RDS instance to include a deny rule for the specific IP addresses.","Configure a Network Access Control List (NACL) associated with the database subnet to explicitly deny inbound traffic from the specific IP addresses.","Implement AWS WAF on the Amazon RDS instance to block traffic from the identified malicious IP addresses.","Update the route table for the database subnet to blackhole traffic originating from the specific IP addresses."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Network Access Control Lists (NACLs) are stateless and operate at the subnet level, allowing explicit deny rules for inbound and outbound traffic. This perfectly meets the requirement for a stateless filtering mechanism at the subnet level and the ability to explicitly deny traffic. Option A (security groups for deny rules) is an anti-pattern (ap-1.1-003) because security groups are stateful and only support allow rules, not explicit deny rules. Option C (AWS WAF) is designed for web application firewalls and cannot be directly applied to an Amazon RDS instance for network-level filtering. Option D (route table blackholing) would prevent all traffic from those IPs from reaching the subnet, but NACLs provide more granular control for specific ports and protocols, and are the intended service for subnet-level deny rules.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  4,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_1_40',
  'A company is developing a new mobile application that requires users to sign up, sign in, and access AWS resources directly from the application. The application needs a scalable and secure way to manage user identities (authentication) and then grant those authenticated users temporary, limited-privilege access to AWS services like Amazon S3 for uploading profile pictures (authorization). Which combination of AWS services should the solutions architect recommend to achieve this?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon Cognito User Pools for authentication and Amazon Cognito Identity Pools for authorization.","AWS IAM Identity Center for both user authentication and authorization to AWS services.","Amazon Cognito User Pools for authentication and IAM roles with direct access keys for authorization.","AWS IAM users for authentication and Amazon Cognito Identity Pools for authorization."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Amazon Cognito User Pools handle user authentication (sign-up, sign-in, and token issuance), while Amazon Cognito Identity Pools handle authorization by exchanging those tokens for temporary AWS credentials, allowing users to access AWS services. This combination is ideal for mobile and web applications. Option B (AWS IAM Identity Center) is for workforce identities accessing AWS accounts, not for customer-facing applications. Option C (IAM roles with direct access keys) is an anti-pattern (ap-1.1-002) as it involves long-term credentials and is not suitable for direct application access. Option D (IAM users for authentication) is not scalable for customer-facing applications and is not the intended use for IAM users (ap-1.1-004).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  5,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_1_41',
  'An application running on Amazon EC2 instances in a private subnet requires access to Amazon S3 for storing logs and Amazon DynamoDB for data persistence. The company''s security policy dictates that traffic to AWS services must not traverse the public internet to minimize exposure. The solutions architect needs to implement this connectivity with the LEAST operational overhead. Which solution should be chosen?',
  'mcq',
  '["Deploy a NAT Gateway in a public subnet and configure private subnet route tables to route traffic through it.","Create Gateway VPC Endpoints for Amazon S3 and Amazon DynamoDB, and update the private subnet route tables.","Establish an AWS Site-to-Site VPN connection from the VPC to an on-premises data center, then route traffic to AWS services.","Assign Elastic IP addresses to the Amazon EC2 instances and configure an Internet Gateway for direct public access."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Creating Gateway VPC Endpoints for Amazon S3 and Amazon DynamoDB allows instances in private subnets to access these services without traversing the public internet, a NAT Gateway, or a VPN. Gateway endpoints are free and are configured as route table entries, requiring no additional infrastructure to manage, thus offering the least operational overhead. Option A (NAT Gateway) would work but adds cost and still routes traffic through the AWS public network edge, not entirely private. Option C (Site-to-Site VPN) adds unnecessary complexity and latency. Option D (Internet Gateway with Elastic IPs) would expose the instances to the internet, violating the security policy for private access.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  6,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_1_42',
  'A company requires that all IAM users accessing AWS resources use Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA) to enhance security. The security team wants to enforce this policy for all console and programmatic access across their AWS account. They need a mechanism that will prevent users from performing actions if they have not authenticated with MFA. Which IAM feature should the solutions architect implement?',
  'mcq',
  '["Enable MFA for the root user account and advise all IAM users to do the same.","Attach an IAM policy to all users that explicitly denies actions unless MFA is present in the request context.","Configure an AWS Config rule to detect IAM users without MFA enabled and report them.","Create a custom AWS Lambda function to disable IAM users who do not have MFA enabled."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Attaching an IAM policy that explicitly denies actions unless MFA is present in the request context (e.g., using the `aws:MultiFactorAuthPresent` condition key) is the most effective way to enforce MFA for all IAM users. This policy prevents users from performing actions if they haven''t authenticated with MFA. Option A (advising users) relies on manual compliance and is not an enforcement mechanism. Option C (AWS Config rule) provides detection and reporting but does not prevent non-MFA authenticated actions. Option D (custom Lambda function) is a reactive solution that adds operational overhead and does not prevent the initial non-MFA authenticated action.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  7,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────
-- Module: m_18_2
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_2_36',
  'A company is deploying a new public-facing web application on Amazon EC2 instances behind an Application Load Balancer. The application needs to accept HTTPS traffic, and the security team requires that all TLS certificates be managed by AWS, automatically renewed, and free of charge. The solutions architect must ensure that the application uses trusted certificates without manual intervention for certificate lifecycle management. Which service should be used to provision and manage these certificates?',
  'mcq',
  '["Generate and install self-signed SSL/TLS certificates directly on the Amazon EC2 instances.","Purchase SSL/TLS certificates from a third-party Certificate Authority and upload them to AWS IAM.","Provision and manage public SSL/TLS certificates using AWS Certificate Manager (ACM) and associate them with the Application Load Balancer.","Use AWS Secrets Manager to store and rotate self-signed certificates for the application."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'AWS Certificate Manager (ACM) provides free, publicly trusted SSL/TLS certificates that can be easily provisioned and associated with AWS services like Application Load Balancers. ACM automatically renews these certificates, eliminating manual intervention and meeting the requirements for trusted, auto-renewing, and free certificates. Option A (self-signed certificates) is an anti-pattern (ap-1.2-004) as they cause browser warnings and are not trusted by default. Option B (third-party certificates uploaded to IAM) involves purchasing and manual renewal, which contradicts the ''free'' and ''automatic renewal'' requirements. Option D (Secrets Manager for self-signed certificates) is incorrect as Secrets Manager is for credentials, not public certificates, and self-signed certificates are not suitable for public-facing endpoints.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  8,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_2_37',
  'A company is deploying a new internal web application that will be accessed by employees only. The application runs on Amazon EC2 instances within a VPC. The security team requires that all network traffic to and from the EC2 instances is strictly controlled, allowing only necessary ports and protocols. They also need to implement an additional layer of stateless network filtering at the subnet level to provide defense-in-depth against potential threats. Which combination of AWS services should the solutions architect use?',
  'mcq',
  '["Use only Security Groups to control inbound and outbound traffic to the EC2 instances.","Implement AWS WAF to filter traffic at the application layer and Security Groups for instance-level control.","Utilize Network Access Control Lists (NACLs) at the subnet level and Security Groups at the instance level.","Deploy an AWS Shield Advanced protection plan for the VPC and configure Security Groups."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Utilizing Network Access Control Lists (NACLs) at the subnet level and Security Groups at the instance level provides a robust defense-in-depth strategy. NACLs offer stateless filtering for all traffic entering or leaving a subnet, while Security Groups provide stateful filtering for individual instances. Option A (only Security Groups) lacks the subnet-level stateless filtering for defense-in-depth (ap-1.2-003). Option B (AWS WAF) is for web application protection, not general network traffic filtering at the subnet or instance level. Option D (AWS Shield Advanced) is for DDoS protection, not granular port/protocol control or subnet-level filtering.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  9,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_2_38',
  'A solutions architect needs to design a secure way for administrators to access Amazon EC2 instances for troubleshooting and maintenance. The current practice involves opening port 22 (SSH) to 0.0.0.0/0 in security groups, which the security team has identified as a critical vulnerability. The new solution must eliminate direct SSH access from the internet, provide secure shell access, and minimize operational overhead. Which AWS service should be recommended?',
  'mcq',
  '["Continue using SSH but restrict the security group to allow port 22 only from a bastion host with a public IP address.","Implement AWS Systems Manager Session Manager to provide secure shell access to EC2 instances without opening inbound ports.","Deploy an AWS Site-to-Site VPN connection to allow SSH access only from the corporate network.","Configure an AWS WAF rule to block SSH brute-force attacks on port 22."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'AWS Systems Manager Session Manager provides secure, auditable shell access to EC2 instances without requiring inbound ports (like SSH port 22) to be open in security groups, thus eliminating the vulnerability of opening port 22 to 0.0.0.0/0 (ap-1.2-001). It also minimizes operational overhead by not requiring bastion hosts or VPNs for simple shell access. Option A (bastion host) is a valid solution but adds operational overhead compared to Session Manager. Option C (Site-to-Site VPN) is also a valid solution but is more complex and adds overhead for simple shell access. Option D (AWS WAF) is for web application protection and cannot secure SSH access to EC2 instances.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  10,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_2_39',
  'A company is developing a new application that processes sensitive customer data. The application needs to store database credentials, API keys, and other secrets securely. These secrets must be automatically rotated, and access to them must be tightly controlled and auditable. The solutions architect needs a managed service that can handle the lifecycle of these secrets with minimal custom code. Which AWS service should be chosen?',
  'mcq',
  '["Store secrets as plain text files on Amazon S3 with bucket policies for access control.","Embed secrets directly into the application''s environment variables on the compute instances.","Use AWS Secrets Manager to store, retrieve, and automatically rotate database credentials and other secrets.","Encrypt secrets using AWS KMS and store them in an Amazon DynamoDB table with fine-grained access control."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'AWS Secrets Manager is specifically designed for securely storing, retrieving, and automatically rotating database credentials, API keys, and other secrets. It integrates with AWS services like Amazon RDS for automatic rotation and provides fine-grained access control and auditing through AWS CloudTrail. Option A (plain text in S3) is highly insecure. Option B (environment variables) is an anti-pattern (ap-1.2-002) as these are visible and not designed for secure, rotatable secret management. Option D (KMS + DynamoDB) is a custom solution that would require significant development and operational overhead to implement rotation and access control, whereas Secrets Manager provides this as a managed service.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  11,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_2_40',
  'A public-facing web application is experiencing frequent DDoS attacks, leading to service disruptions and poor user experience. The application runs on Amazon EC2 instances behind an Application Load Balancer. The security team needs a solution that can protect the application from common network and transport layer DDoS attacks, as well as provide basic protection against web application exploits, with minimal configuration. Which combination of AWS services should the solutions architect recommend?',
  'mcq',
  '["Deploy AWS Shield Standard for network layer protection and AWS WAF for application layer protection.","Implement Amazon GuardDuty for threat detection and Amazon Inspector for vulnerability assessment.","Configure Network Access Control Lists (NACLs) and Security Groups to block malicious IP addresses.","Subscribe to AWS Shield Advanced for comprehensive DDoS protection and use Amazon Macie for data discovery."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'AWS Shield Standard provides automatic protection against common network and transport layer (Layer 3 and 4) DDoS attacks for all AWS customers at no additional cost. AWS WAF protects web applications from common web exploits (Layer 7) that could affect application availability or compromise security. This combination provides robust protection against the described threats with minimal configuration. Option B (GuardDuty and Inspector) are for threat detection and vulnerability assessment, not active DDoS or web exploit prevention. Option C (NACLs and Security Groups) are fundamental network controls but are not designed to dynamically mitigate sophisticated DDoS attacks. Option D (AWS Shield Advanced and Macie) is a more comprehensive and costly solution, with Macie being for data discovery, not DDoS protection, exceeding the ''minimal configuration'' and ''basic protection'' requirement for web exploits.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  12,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_2_41',
  'A company needs to establish a secure and private connection between their on-premises data center and their AWS VPC. This connection will carry sensitive data and must ensure consistent network performance and dedicated bandwidth, bypassing the public internet. The solutions architect needs to choose a connectivity option that meets these requirements for a production environment. Which AWS service should be recommended?',
  'mcq',
  '["Configure an AWS Site-to-Site VPN connection over the public internet.","Establish an AWS Direct Connect connection between the data center and an AWS Direct Connect location.","Use an Internet Gateway with a public IP address on an EC2 instance to tunnel traffic.","Set up a VPC Peering connection between the on-premises network and the AWS VPC."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'AWS Direct Connect establishes a dedicated, private network connection from your on-premises data center to AWS, bypassing the public internet. This provides consistent network performance and dedicated bandwidth, which is critical for sensitive data and production environments. Option A (Site-to-Site VPN) uses the public internet, which does not guarantee consistent performance or dedicated bandwidth. Option C (Internet Gateway with EC2 tunnel) is an insecure and unscalable custom solution. Option D (VPC Peering) connects two VPCs within AWS, not an on-premises data center to a VPC.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  13,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_2_42',
  'An organization is deploying a new application that processes payment card industry (PCI) data. The application requires strict network segmentation and the ability to explicitly deny traffic from specific IP ranges at the subnet level. The security team also needs to ensure that the application''s EC2 instances are protected by stateful firewall rules. Which combination of AWS networking features should the solutions architect implement to achieve this defense-in-depth approach?',
  'mcq',
  '["Use only Security Groups to control both instance-level and subnet-level traffic.","Implement Network Access Control Lists (NACLs) for explicit deny rules at the subnet level and Security Groups for stateful instance-level filtering.","Deploy AWS WAF to filter all incoming traffic to the VPC and Security Groups for instance protection.","Configure AWS Shield Advanced for DDoS protection and rely on default VPC network settings."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Implementing Network Access Control Lists (NACLs) at the subnet level allows for explicit deny rules and stateless filtering, which is crucial for PCI compliance and defense-in-depth. Security Groups provide stateful firewall rules at the instance level, controlling inbound and outbound traffic. This combination offers robust, layered network security. Option A (only Security Groups) is an anti-pattern (ap-1.2-003) as security groups are stateful and do not support explicit deny rules at the subnet level. Option C (AWS WAF) is for web application protection, not general network traffic filtering at the subnet level. Option D (AWS Shield Advanced) is for DDoS protection and does not provide granular network access control.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  14,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_2_43',
  'A company has a critical internal web application that is only accessible from within their corporate network. The application runs on Amazon EC2 instances in a private subnet. The security team wants to ensure that no one accidentally opens port 22 (SSH) to the entire internet (0.0.0.0/0) for these instances, as this is a common security vulnerability. They need a secure and auditable method for administrators to gain shell access to these instances without exposing them publicly. Which solution is the MOST secure and operationally efficient?',
  'mcq',
  '["Allow SSH access from 0.0.0.0/0 in the security group and rely on strong passwords.","Configure a bastion host in a public subnet with a highly restricted security group, and use it to proxy SSH connections to private instances.","Utilize AWS Systems Manager Session Manager to establish secure shell sessions to the instances without opening any inbound ports.","Implement a custom VPN solution on an EC2 instance to provide secure remote access to the private subnet."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'AWS Systems Manager Session Manager provides secure, auditable, and browser-based shell access to EC2 instances without requiring inbound ports (like SSH port 22) to be open in security groups. This directly addresses the anti-pattern (ap-1.2-001) of opening port 22 to 0.0.0.0/0 and is operationally efficient as it eliminates the need for managing SSH keys or bastion hosts. Option A (opening port 22 to 0.0.0.0/0) is a critical security anti-pattern. Option B (bastion host) is a valid, more secure alternative to direct internet SSH, but Session Manager is generally more operationally efficient. Option D (custom VPN solution) adds significant operational overhead for setup and maintenance compared to Session Manager.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  15,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────
-- Module: m_18_3
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_3_36',
  'A company stores highly sensitive customer data in Amazon S3 buckets. Due to strict compliance regulations, all data must be encrypted at rest using keys that the company fully controls, with the ability to manage key policies, enable key rotation, and audit all key usage through AWS CloudTrail. The solutions architect needs to recommend an encryption strategy that meets these requirements while minimizing the operational burden of key management. Which S3 encryption option should be selected?',
  'mcq',
  '["Use Server-Side Encryption with Amazon S3-managed keys (SSE-S3) for all objects.","Implement Client-Side Encryption, managing encryption keys within the application itself.","Utilize Server-Side Encryption with AWS KMS customer managed keys (CMKs) for S3 buckets.","Employ Server-Side Encryption with customer-provided keys (SSE-C) for all data uploads."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Server-Side Encryption with AWS KMS customer managed keys (CMKs) allows the company to fully control the encryption keys, manage key policies, enable automatic key rotation, and audit all key usage through AWS CloudTrail, meeting all compliance requirements. This is a managed service, minimizing operational burden compared to manual key management. Option A (SSE-S3) uses AWS-managed keys, which do not provide the customer with full control or auditability (ap-1.3-001). Option B (Client-Side Encryption) places the full burden of key management, rotation, and auditing on the application, increasing operational complexity significantly. Option D (SSE-C) requires the customer to provide and manage keys with every request, which is operationally intensive and does not offer built-in rotation or centralized auditing.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  16,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_3_37',
  'A media company uses Amazon S3 to store large video files. They need to ensure that once a video file is uploaded, it cannot be accidentally or maliciously deleted or overwritten for a period of seven years, even by privileged users. The solution must support write-once-read-many (WORM) compliance and be easy to configure. Which Amazon S3 feature should the solutions architect enable?',
  'mcq',
  '["Configure S3 Lifecycle policies to transition objects to S3 Glacier after seven years.","Enable S3 Versioning on the bucket and then apply S3 Object Lock in WORM mode.","Set up a bucket policy that explicitly denies `s3:DeleteObject` and `s3:PutObject` actions for all users.","Use S3 Cross-Region Replication to copy objects to another bucket for redundancy."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Enabling S3 Versioning on the bucket and then applying S3 Object Lock in WORM (Write Once Read Many) mode with a retention period of seven years will prevent objects from being accidentally or maliciously deleted or overwritten. S3 Object Lock requires versioning to be enabled (ap-1.3-002). Option A (Lifecycle policies) is for cost optimization and data archival, not for WORM compliance or preventing deletion. Option C (bucket policy) can be circumvented by users with sufficient permissions to modify the policy itself. Option D (Cross-Region Replication) is for disaster recovery and redundancy, not for WORM compliance or preventing object modification within the source bucket.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  17,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_3_38',
  'A healthcare company stores patient records in Amazon S3. Due to strict HIPAA compliance requirements, all data must be encrypted at rest, and the encryption keys must be stored in a FIPS 140-2 Level 3 validated hardware security module (HSM) that the company has exclusive control over. The solutions architect needs to choose an encryption key management solution that meets this stringent requirement. Which AWS service should be recommended?',
  'mcq',
  '["Use Server-Side Encryption with Amazon S3-managed keys (SSE-S3).","Utilize Server-Side Encryption with AWS KMS customer managed keys (CMKs).","Implement AWS CloudHSM to generate and store encryption keys, integrating it with S3.","Employ Client-Side Encryption with keys managed by a third-party key management system."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'AWS CloudHSM provides dedicated, single-tenant hardware security modules (HSMs) that are FIPS 140-2 Level 3 validated, giving the customer exclusive control over their encryption keys. This is the only AWS service that meets the specific requirement for FIPS 140-2 Level 3 validation and exclusive customer control over the HSM. Option A (SSE-S3) uses AWS-managed keys and does not offer customer control over the HSM. Option B (AWS KMS CMKs) uses FIPS 140-2 Level 2 validated HSMs and is a multi-tenant service, not providing exclusive control (ap-1.3-004). Option D (third-party client-side encryption) introduces significant operational overhead and may not meet the ''FIPS 140-2 Level 3'' and ''exclusive control'' requirements as easily as CloudHSM.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  18,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_3_39',
  'A company is implementing a new data archiving strategy for regulatory compliance. They need to store immutable copies of critical documents in Amazon S3 for a minimum of five years, ensuring that these objects cannot be deleted or modified during this period. After the retention period, the objects can be automatically deleted. The solutions architect needs to design a cost-effective solution that enforces immutability. Which S3 features should be combined?',
  'mcq',
  '["Enable S3 Versioning and configure S3 Lifecycle policies to transition objects to S3 Glacier after five years.","Enable S3 Versioning and apply S3 Object Lock with a Governance retention mode for five years.","Enable S3 Versioning and apply S3 Object Lock with a Compliance retention mode for five years.","Use S3 bucket policies to deny delete and overwrite actions for all users for five years."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Enabling S3 Versioning and applying S3 Object Lock with a Compliance retention mode for five years ensures that objects cannot be overwritten or deleted by any user, including the root user, during the specified period. This satisfies the immutability requirement for regulatory compliance. Option A (Lifecycle policies) is for cost management and transitions, not immutability. Option B (Governance retention mode) allows some privileged users to bypass the lock, which does not meet the strict immutability requirement. Option D (bucket policies) can be modified or removed by users with sufficient permissions, making it less robust than Object Lock for immutability.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  19,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_3_40',
  'A company needs to encrypt data in transit for all communication between their Amazon EC2 instances and an Amazon RDS database instance within the same VPC. The security team requires that TLS encryption be enforced for all database connections to protect sensitive information. The solutions architect must implement this with minimal configuration and without introducing additional network latency. Which approach should be taken?',
  'mcq',
  '["Configure a custom VPN tunnel between the EC2 instances and the RDS instance.","Enable SSL/TLS encryption for the Amazon RDS database instance and configure the application to use TLS connections.","Use AWS PrivateLink to establish a secure connection between the EC2 instances and the RDS instance.","Deploy an Application Load Balancer in front of the RDS instance to handle TLS termination."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Enabling SSL/TLS encryption for the Amazon RDS database instance and configuring the application to use TLS connections is the standard and most straightforward way to encrypt data in transit between EC2 and RDS. RDS supports TLS natively, and this approach requires minimal configuration. Option A (custom VPN tunnel) is overly complex and introduces unnecessary operational overhead and potential latency within the same VPC. Option C (AWS PrivateLink) is for connecting to services across VPCs or accounts, not for encrypting traffic between EC2 and RDS within the same VPC. Option D (ALB in front of RDS) is not a typical or efficient pattern for database connections and would add unnecessary complexity and latency.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  20,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_3_41',
  'A company stores highly confidential documents in an Amazon S3 bucket. They have a strict data retention policy that requires all documents to be kept for exactly 10 years, and then permanently deleted to comply with data minimization regulations. The solutions architect needs to automate this process to ensure compliance and minimize storage costs over the long term. Which S3 feature should be configured?',
  'mcq',
  '["Manually review and delete documents after 10 years to ensure compliance.","Enable S3 Versioning and S3 Object Lock with a Compliance retention mode for 10 years.","Configure S3 Lifecycle policies to transition objects to S3 Glacier after 30 days and then expire them after 10 years.","Use S3 Cross-Region Replication to copy documents to another bucket and manage deletion there."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Configuring S3 Lifecycle policies to transition objects to a lower-cost storage class like S3 Glacier after an initial period (e.g., 30 days) and then expire them after 10 years (total retention) automates both cost optimization and the permanent deletion requirement. This ensures compliance with the data retention policy. Option A (manual deletion) is inefficient and prone to human error. Option B (Versioning and Object Lock) ensures immutability but does not automatically delete objects after the retention period; it only prevents premature deletion. Option D (Cross-Region Replication) is for disaster recovery and redundancy, not for automated data retention and deletion.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  21,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_3_42',
  'A manufacturing company uses Amazon S3 to store sensor data from factory equipment. The data is frequently accessed for real-time analytics for the first 30 days, but then becomes less frequently accessed and needs to be archived for compliance reasons for a total of 7 years. The solutions architect needs to design a solution that optimizes storage costs while ensuring data is available for analytics when needed and archived securely. Which S3 storage class and feature combination should be recommended?',
  'mcq',
  '["Store all data in S3 Standard-IA for 7 years to balance cost and availability.","Store all data in S3 Glacier Deep Archive from ingestion to minimize costs.","Use S3 Intelligent-Tiering to automatically move data between access tiers based on access patterns.","Configure S3 Lifecycle policies to transition data from S3 Standard to S3 One Zone-IA after 30 days, then to S3 Glacier after 90 days, and finally expire after 7 years."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'S3 Intelligent-Tiering is the most cost-effective solution for data with changing or unknown access patterns. It automatically moves objects between two access tiers (frequently accessed and infrequently accessed) and an optional archive access tier, optimizing storage costs without performance impact or operational overhead. This meets the requirement for real-time analytics for 30 days and then cost-effective archiving. Option A (S3 Standard-IA for 7 years) would be more expensive for the first 30 days of frequent access. Option B (S3 Glacier Deep Archive from ingestion) would make real-time analytics impossible due to retrieval times and costs. Option D (manual lifecycle policies) requires predicting access patterns and more complex configuration than Intelligent-Tiering, which automates this process.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  22,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_3_43',
  'A company is migrating a legacy application that uses a custom encryption mechanism to AWS. The application requires full control over the cryptographic keys, including the ability to import existing keys, generate new keys, and perform cryptographic operations within a dedicated, customer-controlled hardware security module (HSM). The compliance team mandates FIPS 140-2 Level 3 validation for the key storage. Which AWS service is best suited for this requirement?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Key Management Service (KMS) with customer managed keys (CMKs).","AWS Secrets Manager for storing encryption keys as secure strings.","AWS CloudHSM, providing dedicated hardware security modules.","Amazon DynamoDB to store encrypted keys with application-level encryption."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'AWS CloudHSM provides dedicated hardware security modules (HSMs) that are FIPS 140-2 Level 3 validated, giving the customer exclusive control over their encryption keys, including the ability to import, generate, and perform cryptographic operations directly within the HSM. This meets the stringent compliance and control requirements. Option A (AWS KMS CMKs) uses FIPS 140-2 Level 2 validated HSMs and is a multi-tenant service, not providing the exclusive control over the HSM (ap-1.3-004). Option B (AWS Secrets Manager) is for storing and rotating secrets, not for managing cryptographic keys within an HSM. Option D (Amazon DynamoDB) is a database service and not designed for secure, FIPS-validated key management.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  23,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_18_m_18_3_44',
  'A company needs to ensure that all data stored in Amazon S3 is encrypted at rest. While AWS-managed keys (SSE-S3) are acceptable for most data, certain highly sensitive data requires customer-managed keys (CMKs) with an audit trail of key usage for compliance purposes. The solutions architect needs to implement this encryption strategy with the ability to track every API call made to the encryption key. Which S3 encryption option should be chosen for the highly sensitive data?',
  'mcq',
  '["Server-Side Encryption with Amazon S3-managed keys (SSE-S3).","Client-Side Encryption with keys managed by the application.","Server-Side Encryption with AWS KMS customer managed keys (CMKs).","Server-Side Encryption with customer-provided keys (SSE-C)."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Server-Side Encryption with AWS KMS customer managed keys (CMKs) allows the company to use their own keys, manage key policies, and critically, all API calls made to the CMK are logged in AWS CloudTrail, providing a comprehensive audit trail of key usage. This directly addresses the requirement for an audit trail. Option A (SSE-S3) uses AWS-managed keys, which do not provide customer control or an audit trail of key usage (ap-1.3-001). Option B (Client-Side Encryption) requires the customer to implement and manage the audit trail, increasing operational overhead. Option D (SSE-C) uses customer-provided keys but does not provide an automatic audit trail of key usage through AWS CloudTrail for the key itself.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain_18_4',
  NOW(),
  24,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_18_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

