INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_19_m_19_1_63',
  'An e-commerce company processes customer orders, which can experience unpredictable spikes, especially during promotional events. Each order requires several backend processing steps, including inventory updates, payment authorization, and notification delivery. The current synchronous processing architecture frequently fails under heavy load, leading to lost orders and customer dissatisfaction. The company needs a solution to handle these spikes without losing messages and ensure reliable, asynchronous processing with MINIMAL operational overhead.',
  'mcq',
  '["Use Amazon API Gateway to receive orders, then directly invoke an AWS Lambda function synchronously for each order, configuring Lambda reserved concurrency to handle peak load.","Implement Amazon API Gateway to receive orders, send messages to an Amazon SQS queue, and process messages using an Auto Scaling group of Amazon EC2 instances configured for long polling.","Deploy an Application Load Balancer in front of an Auto Scaling group of Amazon EC2 instances, scaling based on CPU utilization, with instances processing orders synchronously.","Utilize Amazon Kinesis Data Streams to ingest order events and process them with AWS Lambda functions triggered by the Kinesis stream, ensuring sufficient shard capacity."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Sending messages to an Amazon SQS queue and processing them with an Auto Scaling group of Amazon EC2 instances (Option 1) provides a highly reliable and scalable solution. Amazon SQS acts as a buffer, decoupling the ingestion of orders from their processing, preventing message loss during spikes. The Auto Scaling group can dynamically adjust processing capacity based on queue depth, and long polling reduces empty receives and costs. Option 0 (Synchronous Lambda invocation) can hit concurrency limits and has a 15-minute timeout, making it less suitable for long-running, spiky workloads, and tightly couples the API to the processing. Option 2 (ALB with CPU-based scaling) reacts slower to sudden spikes and may drop requests during scale-out, and synchronous processing is an anti-pattern for resilience. Option 3 (Amazon Kinesis Data Streams) is suitable for high-throughput streaming but requires shard capacity planning and management, which increases operational overhead compared to SQS for this specific message queuing scenario.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain19_4',
  NOW(),
  1,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_19_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_19_m_19_1_64',
  'A gaming company is launching a new multiplayer online game that relies heavily on real-time, low-latency UDP traffic for game state synchronization between players. The game backend runs on Amazon EC2 instances in an Auto Scaling group. The company needs a load balancing solution that can efficiently distribute this UDP traffic to the backend instances while maintaining extremely low latency and high throughput. Which AWS service is MOST suitable for this requirement?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon CloudFront to cache game assets and reduce latency for static content delivery.","An Application Load Balancer (ALB) configured to distribute incoming UDP traffic to the Amazon EC2 instances.","An Amazon API Gateway endpoint to handle incoming game requests and route them to backend services.","A Network Load Balancer (NLB) to distribute UDP traffic to the Amazon EC2 instances in the Auto Scaling group."]'::json,
  '{3}',
  'A Network Load Balancer (NLB) (Option 3) is designed for extreme performance and static IP addresses, making it ideal for handling high-throughput, low-latency UDP traffic. NLBs operate at the connection level (Layer 4) and are highly efficient for non-HTTP/HTTPS protocols. Option 0 (Amazon CloudFront) is a Content Delivery Network (CDN) for caching static content, not for real-time game traffic. Option 1 (Application Load Balancer) only supports HTTP/HTTPS traffic (Anti-pattern: ap-2.1-002) and is not suitable for UDP. Option 2 (Amazon API Gateway) is designed for RESTful APIs over HTTP/HTTPS and would introduce unnecessary overhead and latency for real-time UDP gaming traffic.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain19_4',
  NOW(),
  2,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_19_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_19_m_19_1_65',
  'An online retail company experiences frequent traffic spikes to its product catalog, which is backed by an Amazon RDS for MySQL database. During these spikes, the database becomes a bottleneck, leading to increased latency for customers browsing products. The development team wants to significantly reduce the load on the database and improve the response time for frequently accessed product information, such as product details and pricing, with MINIMAL changes to the application code.',
  'mcq',
  '["Upgrade the Amazon RDS for MySQL instance to a larger instance type with more CPU and memory resources.","Implement Amazon ElastiCache for Redis as a caching layer between the application and the database.","Configure Amazon RDS Multi-AZ deployment to distribute read queries across multiple database instances.","Migrate the product catalog data from Amazon RDS to Amazon DynamoDB for better read scalability."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Implementing Amazon ElastiCache for Redis (Option 1) as a caching layer is the most effective way to reduce database load and improve read performance for frequently accessed data. ElastiCache provides sub-millisecond response times, allowing the application to retrieve cached product information without querying the database, especially during traffic spikes. Option 0 (Upgrading RDS instance) provides vertical scaling but has limits and does not address the fundamental issue of repeated queries hitting the database. Option 2 (Multi-AZ deployment) is for high availability and failover, not for read scaling, as the standby instance does not serve read traffic. Option 3 (Migrating to Amazon DynamoDB) would provide excellent read scalability but requires significant application re-architecture and data migration, which is not a minimal change.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain19_4',
  NOW(),
  3,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_19_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_19_m_19_1_66',
  'A data processing application uses an Auto Scaling group of Amazon EC2 instances to poll an Amazon SQS queue for new messages, which represent tasks to be processed. The current polling mechanism frequently results in empty responses, leading to unnecessary API calls to Amazon SQS and increased operational costs. The development team needs to optimize the polling strategy to MINIMIZE the number of empty responses and reduce costs.',
  'mcq',
  '["Configure the Amazon SQS queue consumers to use long polling by setting a `WaitTimeSeconds` value greater than zero.","Increase the number of Amazon EC2 instances in the Auto Scaling group to handle the message processing more quickly.","Implement a cron job on a single Amazon EC2 instance to poll the Amazon SQS queue at fixed intervals.","Switch from Amazon SQS to Amazon Kinesis Data Streams for message queuing, as it has a lower cost for polling."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Configuring Amazon SQS consumers to use long polling (Option 0) by setting a `WaitTimeSeconds` value greater than zero is the correct solution. Long polling allows Amazon SQS to wait for messages to arrive before sending a response, significantly reducing the number of empty responses and associated costs (Anti-pattern: ap-2.1-001). Option 1 (Increasing instance count) would exacerbate the problem by increasing the number of empty polls and costs. Option 2 (Cron job on a single instance) reduces scalability and introduces a single point of failure. Option 3 (Switching to Amazon Kinesis Data Streams) is a different service with a different pricing model and operational characteristics, not a direct solution for optimizing SQS polling efficiency.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain19_4',
  NOW(),
  4,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_19_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_19_m_19_1_67',
  'A company is refactoring its monolithic application into a microservices architecture. The new architecture uses multiple stateless Amazon EC2 instances behind an Application Load Balancer. However, user sessions are currently stored in memory on individual EC2 instances, leading to users losing their session data when routed to a different instance by the load balancer. The company needs a solution to manage user sessions that supports horizontal scaling and ensures session persistence across instances.',
  'mcq',
  '["Enable sticky sessions on the Application Load Balancer to ensure users are always routed to the same EC2 instance.","Store user session data directly in Amazon S3 objects, with each object representing a user''s session.","Implement an external session store using Amazon ElastiCache for Redis to centralize session data.","Configure a shared Amazon EFS file system to store session files accessible by all Amazon EC2 instances."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Implementing an external session store using Amazon ElastiCache for Redis (Option 2) is the most effective way to manage user sessions for a horizontally scalable, stateless microservices architecture. ElastiCache provides a fast, in-memory data store for session data, allowing any EC2 instance to retrieve session information, thus enabling true statelessness and resilience (Anti-pattern: ap-2.1-004). Option 0 (Sticky sessions) can work but limits horizontal scaling and resilience, as it creates affinity to specific instances. Option 1 (Storing sessions in Amazon S3) would introduce high latency for session lookups, making it unsuitable for real-time user sessions. Option 3 (Shared Amazon EFS) is a file system, not optimized for high-performance session management, and can introduce latency and complexity for this use case.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain19_4',
  NOW(),
  5,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_19_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_19_m_19_1_68',
  'A media company uploads new video content to an Amazon S3 bucket. Upon successful upload, a single event needs to trigger multiple independent downstream processes: one to transcode the video, another to generate thumbnails, and a third to update a content catalog in an Amazon DynamoDB table. The company requires a highly scalable and loosely coupled solution to distribute this single event to all interested services without requiring direct communication between them.',
  'mcq',
  '["Configure an Amazon S3 event notification to publish messages to an Amazon SNS topic, with each downstream service subscribing to the topic.","Use AWS Step Functions to orchestrate a workflow that sequentially invokes AWS Lambda functions for each downstream process.","Set up an Amazon S3 event notification to directly invoke three separate AWS Lambda functions for each downstream process.","Implement Amazon EventBridge rules to filter the Amazon S3 event and send it to an Amazon SQS queue for each downstream service."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Configuring an Amazon S3 event notification to publish messages to an Amazon SNS topic (Option 0) is the most efficient and scalable way to distribute a single event to multiple independent subscribers (fanout pattern). Each downstream service can subscribe to the SNS topic, receiving the event without direct coupling. Option 1 (AWS Step Functions) is for orchestrating complex workflows with state management, which is overkill for simple event fanout. Option 2 (Directly invoking three Lambda functions) creates tight coupling between S3 and the Lambda functions, making it harder to add or remove subscribers without modifying the S3 configuration. Option 3 (Amazon EventBridge to SQS queues) is a valid pattern but SNS is simpler and more direct for fanout to multiple subscribers without needing separate SQS queues for each, reducing complexity.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain19_4',
  NOW(),
  6,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_19_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_19_m_19_1_69',
  'A data analytics company needs to run daily batch processing jobs that involve containerized applications. These jobs have highly variable computational requirements, sometimes needing significant resources for a few hours and then remaining idle. The company wants to minimize the operational overhead associated with managing the underlying infrastructure for these containers, allowing their engineers to focus solely on application development. Which AWS service provides the LEAST operational overhead for running these containerized batch jobs?',
  'mcq',
  '["Deploy the containerized applications on Amazon EC2 instances managed by an Auto Scaling group and ECS.","Utilize AWS Fargate with Amazon ECS to run the containerized applications without managing servers.","Provision an Amazon EKS cluster and deploy the containerized applications using Kubernetes deployments.","Use AWS Lambda to run the containerized applications, packaging them as Lambda container images."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Utilizing AWS Fargate with Amazon ECS (Option 1) provides the least operational overhead for running containerized applications. Fargate is a serverless compute engine for containers, meaning you don''t need to provision, scale, or manage Amazon EC2 instances. You only pay for the compute resources consumed by your containers. Option 0 (EC2 instances with ECS) requires managing EC2 instances, including patching, scaling, and maintenance, increasing operational overhead. Option 2 (Amazon EKS cluster) also requires managing the Kubernetes control plane (though AWS manages it, you still manage nodes) or using Fargate for EKS, which is a specific configuration of Fargate. Option 3 (AWS Lambda with container images) is suitable for event-driven, short-duration functions, but batch processing jobs often have longer run times and different resource profiles that are better suited for Fargate.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain19_4',
  NOW(),
  7,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_19_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────
-- Module: m_19_2
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_19_m_19_2_58',
  'A financial services company runs a critical application using an Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL database. The application experiences heavy read traffic, especially during end-of-month reporting, leading to performance bottlenecks. The company requires a solution that can scale read operations horizontally to improve performance during these peak times while ensuring high availability for the primary database and keeping costs as LOW as possible for the read scaling component.',
  'mcq',
  '["Create Amazon RDS read replicas in the same AWS Region to offload read traffic from the primary instance.","Enable Amazon RDS Multi-AZ deployment to distribute read traffic across the primary and standby instances.","Upgrade the existing Amazon RDS instance to a larger instance type with Provisioned IOPS (io1) storage.","Migrate the database to Amazon Aurora PostgreSQL and enable Aurora Auto Scaling for read replicas."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Creating Amazon RDS read replicas (Option 0) is the most cost-effective way to scale read operations horizontally. Read replicas allow you to offload read traffic from the primary database instance, improving performance during peak read loads. Option 1 (Multi-AZ deployment) is for high availability and disaster recovery; the standby instance does NOT serve read traffic, which is a common misconception (Anti-pattern: ap-2.2-001). Option 2 (Upgrading instance type) provides vertical scaling but has a performance ceiling and is generally more expensive than read replicas for scaling reads. Option 3 (Amazon Aurora with Auto Scaling read replicas) is a highly scalable solution but typically involves higher costs and more significant migration effort than simply adding RDS read replicas for an existing RDS PostgreSQL database.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain19_4',
  NOW(),
  8,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_19_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_19_m_19_2_59',
  'A startup is launching a new web application that is expected to grow rapidly. The application relies on Amazon EC2 instances for its web servers and an Amazon RDS for MySQL database. The CTO emphasizes that the application must be designed for high availability from day one to avoid any single points of failure. The development team is considering deployment strategies. Which approach ensures high availability for the application''s infrastructure from its initial launch?',
  'mcq',
  '["Deploy all Amazon EC2 instances and the Amazon RDS database in a single Availability Zone, with a plan to migrate to Multi-AZ later.","Deploy Amazon EC2 instances across multiple Availability Zones using an Auto Scaling group, and configure Amazon RDS for MySQL with Multi-AZ deployment.","Place all Amazon EC2 instances behind an Application Load Balancer within a single Availability Zone to distribute traffic.","Utilize Amazon Route 53 with a simple routing policy to direct traffic to a single Amazon EC2 instance."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Deploying Amazon EC2 instances across multiple Availability Zones using an Auto Scaling group and configuring Amazon RDS for MySQL with Multi-AZ deployment (Option 1) ensures high availability from day one. This architecture protects against failures within a single Availability Zone, as resources are distributed and can automatically failover. Option 0 (Deploying all resources in a single AZ) creates a single point of failure and is an anti-pattern (Anti-pattern: ap-2.2-003), as retrofitting HA is complex. Option 2 (ALB in a single AZ) still leaves the entire deployment vulnerable to an AZ outage. Option 3 (Route 53 with simple routing to a single EC2 instance) provides no high availability or fault tolerance.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain19_4',
  NOW(),
  9,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_19_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_19_m_19_2_60',
  'A critical internal application for a manufacturing plant needs a disaster recovery strategy. The application uses Amazon EC2 instances, an Amazon RDS for SQL Server database, and Amazon S3 for critical documents. The business requires a Recovery Time Objective (RTO) of 5 minutes and a Recovery Point Objective (RPO) of 1 minute. The company is looking for a strategy that balances cost-effectiveness with the need for rapid recovery.',
  'mcq',
  '["Implement a backup and restore strategy, using AWS Backup to replicate backups of Amazon RDS and Amazon S3 to a secondary Region.","Configure a pilot light strategy in a secondary Region, maintaining an Amazon RDS cross-Region read replica and Amazon S3 cross-Region replication, with stopped Amazon EC2 instances.","Deploy a multi-site active/active architecture, running the full application stack in two separate AWS Regions with Amazon Route 53 weighted routing.","Establish a warm standby solution in a secondary Region, running a scaled-down version of the application, with an Amazon RDS cross-Region read replica and Amazon S3 cross-Region replication."]'::json,
  '{3}',
  'A warm standby solution (Option 3) best meets the RTO of 5 minutes and RPO of 1 minute while balancing cost. A warm standby keeps a scaled-down version of the application running in the secondary Region, allowing for quick scaling up and promotion of the Amazon RDS cross-Region read replica to primary, satisfying the RTO. Amazon S3 cross-Region replication ensures the RPO. Option 0 (Backup and restore) cannot meet the RTO/RPO requirements due to the time involved in restoring data and launching resources. Option 1 (Pilot light) typically has an RTO of 10+ minutes (Anti-pattern: ap-2.2-002), which is too long for a 5-minute RTO. Option 2 (Multi-site active/active) is the most expensive and exceeds the required RTO/RPO, making it less cost-effective for these specific requirements.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain19_4',
  NOW(),
  10,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_19_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_19_m_19_2_61',
  'A web application runs on Amazon EC2 instances within an Auto Scaling group behind an Application Load Balancer. The operations team wants to ensure that unhealthy instances are automatically detected and replaced without manual intervention, maintaining the desired capacity and availability of the application. Which configuration is essential to achieve this self-healing capability?',
  'mcq',
  '["Configure the Application Load Balancer to use sticky sessions to maintain user connections to specific instances.","Implement Amazon CloudWatch alarms to monitor CPU utilization and manually terminate instances when thresholds are breached.","Configure health checks on the Auto Scaling group and the Application Load Balancer to automatically replace unhealthy instances.","Set up AWS Config rules to monitor the state of Amazon EC2 instances and trigger AWS Lambda functions for remediation."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Configuring health checks on both the Auto Scaling group and the Application Load Balancer (Option 2) is essential for automatic detection and replacement of unhealthy instances. The ALB health checks determine if an instance can serve traffic, while Auto Scaling group health checks (EC2 or custom) determine if an instance is running and responsive, ensuring that unhealthy instances are terminated and new ones are launched automatically. Option 0 (Sticky sessions) is for session persistence, not health detection or self-healing. Option 1 (Manual termination based on CloudWatch) requires manual intervention and is not an automated self-healing mechanism. Option 3 (AWS Config rules with Lambda) can be used for compliance and remediation but is not the primary mechanism for automatic instance replacement within an Auto Scaling group.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain19_4',
  NOW(),
  11,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_19_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

