INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_19_m_19_2_62',
  'A financial services company operates a critical application that relies on an Amazon RDS for MySQL database. The application requires high availability to ensure continuous operation, even in the event of an Availability Zone outage. The company needs an automated solution that provides fast failover with minimal data loss. Which Amazon RDS feature should be implemented to meet this high availability requirement?',
  'mcq',
  '["Configure Amazon RDS Multi-AZ deployment for the database instance.","Create an Amazon RDS read replica in a different Availability Zone to handle failover.","Schedule regular backups of the Amazon RDS database to Amazon S3 and restore from backup during an outage.","Deploy the Amazon RDS instance in a single Availability Zone and use an Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling group for the application layer."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Configuring Amazon RDS Multi-AZ deployment (Option 0) is the correct approach for high availability with automatic failover. In a Multi-AZ deployment, Amazon RDS automatically provisions and maintains a synchronous standby replica in a different Availability Zone. In case of an outage, Amazon RDS automatically fails over to the standby replica, minimizing downtime and data loss. Option 1 (read replica) is for read scaling, not automatic failover for the primary instance, and the standby in a Multi-AZ deployment does not serve read traffic (Anti-pattern: ap-2.2-001). Option 2 (backup and restore) has a much higher RTO and RPO than required for critical applications. Option 3 (single AZ deployment) introduces a single point of failure for the database, which directly contradicts the high availability requirement (Anti-pattern: ap-2.2-003).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain19_4',
  NOW(),
  1,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_19_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_19_m_19_2_63',
  'An international e-commerce company hosts its primary website in the us-east-1 Region and a disaster recovery site in us-west-2. They use Amazon Route 53 to manage DNS records for their website. In the event of an outage in us-east-1, the company needs to automatically redirect all user traffic to the us-west-2 site. The solution must detect the primary site''s unavailability and initiate the failover without manual intervention. Which Amazon Route 53 configuration is required to achieve this automatic failover?',
  'mcq',
  '["Configure Amazon Route 53 failover routing policy with associated Amazon Route 53 health checks for the us-east-1 endpoints.","Implement a simple routing policy in Amazon Route 53 and manually update the DNS record to point to us-west-2 during an outage.","Set up a weighted routing policy in Amazon Route 53, assigning 100% weight to us-east-1 and 0% to us-west-2, and manually change weights during an outage.","Use a latency-based routing policy in Amazon Route 53 to automatically direct users to the Region with the lowest latency, which will inherently failover during an outage."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'To achieve automatic failover, Amazon Route 53 failover routing policy must be combined with Amazon Route 53 health checks (Option 0). The health checks monitor the availability of the primary site''s endpoints. If the health checks fail, Route 53 automatically routes traffic to the secondary (failover) endpoint. Option 1 (simple routing with manual update) requires manual intervention, which fails the requirement for automatic failover. Option 2 (weighted routing with manual change) also requires manual intervention. Option 3 (latency-based routing) directs traffic based on latency, not endpoint health, and does not inherently provide failover capability if an endpoint becomes unhealthy (Anti-pattern: ap-2.2-004).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain19_4',
  NOW(),
  2,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_19_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_19_m_19_2_64',
  'A startup is launching a new web application and needs to ensure high availability from day one. The application consists of Amazon EC2 instances and an Amazon RDS MySQL database. The development team initially deployed all resources within a single Availability Zone to simplify the setup. However, the business requires the application to withstand an Availability Zone outage without significant downtime. What is the MOST critical architectural principle the team should adopt immediately to meet the high availability requirement?',
  'mcq',
  '["Implement an Amazon CloudFront distribution to cache static content and improve performance.","Configure an Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling group to automatically replace unhealthy instances.","Set up Amazon Route 53 health checks to monitor the application endpoints.","Redesign the architecture to deploy application resources and the Amazon RDS database across multiple Availability Zones."]'::json,
  '{3}',
  'Redesigning the architecture to deploy resources across multiple Availability Zones (Option 3) is the most critical step for achieving high availability from day one. A single Availability Zone deployment is a single point of failure, meaning an outage in that AZ would bring down the entire application (Anti-pattern: ap-2.2-003). Multi-AZ deployment ensures that if one AZ becomes unavailable, the application can continue to operate using resources in another AZ. Option 0 (CloudFront) improves performance and resilience for static content but does not address the core application or database availability. Option 1 (Auto Scaling) helps replace unhealthy instances within an AZ but cannot recover from an entire AZ outage. Option 2 (Route 53 health checks) are essential for detecting failures but do not, by themselves, provide the underlying multi-AZ infrastructure for recovery.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain19_4',
  NOW(),
  3,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_19_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_19_m_19_2_65',
  'A global enterprise requires a disaster recovery strategy for its mission-critical application, which serves customers worldwide. The application is deployed in us-east-1 and ap-southeast-2, using Amazon Aurora Global Database for its data layer and Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling groups for its application servers. The business demands an RTO and RPO of near zero, meaning no data loss and immediate recovery in case of a regional outage. Which disaster recovery strategy should be implemented to meet these stringent requirements?',
  'mcq',
  '["Implement a pilot light strategy, maintaining a scaled-down infrastructure in the secondary Region with an Amazon Aurora cross-Region read replica.","Configure a warm standby strategy, running a scaled-down version of the application in the secondary Region with continuous data replication.","Deploy a multi-site active/active architecture, utilizing Amazon Route 53 with weighted routing and health checks to distribute traffic across both Regions.","Use a backup and restore strategy, performing daily backups of the Amazon Aurora database to Amazon S3 in the secondary Region."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'A multi-site active/active architecture (Option 2) is required to meet an RTO and RPO of near zero. With this strategy, the full application stack is deployed and actively serving traffic in both Regions. Amazon Route 53 with weighted routing and health checks can seamlessly direct users to the healthy Region, providing immediate failover and continuous operation. Amazon Aurora Global Database already provides near real-time replication for the data layer. Option 0 (pilot light) and Option 1 (warm standby) are designed for higher RTOs (minutes to hours) and RPOs, making them unsuitable for near-zero requirements (Anti-pattern: ap-2.2-002). Option 3 (backup and restore) has the highest RTO and RPO and is entirely inadequate for mission-critical applications with near-zero requirements.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain19_4',
  NOW(),
  4,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_19_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_19_m_19_2_66',
  'A web application experiences fluctuating traffic patterns throughout the day, with peak loads occurring in the evenings. The application runs on Amazon EC2 instances, and the company wants to ensure that it can automatically adjust its capacity to match demand, maintaining performance during peak times while optimizing costs during off-peak hours. The solution must also replace any unhealthy instances automatically. Which AWS service should be used to achieve this dynamic scaling and self-healing capability?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling with dynamic scaling policies.","AWS CloudFormation for infrastructure as code deployment.","Amazon CloudWatch for monitoring and alarming.","AWS Systems Manager for operational insights and automation."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling with dynamic scaling policies (Option 0) is the correct service to automatically adjust EC2 capacity based on demand and replace unhealthy instances. Auto Scaling groups ensure that a specified number of healthy instances are always running, scaling out during peak loads and scaling in during off-peak times, thus optimizing costs and maintaining performance. Option 1 (AWS CloudFormation) is for provisioning infrastructure, not for dynamic scaling or self-healing. Option 2 (Amazon CloudWatch) is used for monitoring and creating alarms, which can *trigger* Auto Scaling policies, but it doesn''t perform the scaling itself. Option 3 (AWS Systems Manager) provides operational insights and automation for managing EC2 instances but is not designed for automatic capacity scaling.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain19_4',
  NOW(),
  5,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_19_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────
-- Module: m_19_1
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_19_m_19_1_70',
  'A popular news website experiences frequent, unpredictable traffic surges when major breaking news occurs. The website''s backend is hosted on Amazon EC2 instances and uses an Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL database. During these traffic spikes, the database becomes a bottleneck, leading to slow page load times and occasional application errors due to increased read load. The development team needs to reduce the load on the database and improve the responsiveness of the website, especially for frequently accessed articles. Which architectural change would MOST effectively address the database read bottleneck?',
  'mcq',
  '["Upgrade the Amazon RDS instance to a larger instance type with Provisioned IOPS (io1) storage to increase database throughput.","Implement Amazon ElastiCache for Redis as a caching layer for frequently accessed data between the application and the database.","Configure Amazon RDS Multi-AZ deployment to distribute read queries across the primary and standby database instances.","Create several Amazon RDS read replicas and modify the application to distribute read queries among them."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Implementing Amazon ElastiCache for Redis (Option 1) as a caching layer is the most effective solution for reducing database read load and improving responsiveness for frequently accessed data. ElastiCache provides sub-millisecond latency, allowing the application to retrieve cached data much faster than querying the database. This significantly offloads the Amazon RDS instance during traffic spikes. Option 0 (upgrading instance type) provides a vertical scaling improvement but has a performance ceiling and does not fundamentally change the read pattern. Option 2 (Multi-AZ deployment) is for high availability and automatic failover; the standby instance does not serve read traffic (Anti-pattern: ap-2.2-001). Option 3 (read replicas) improves read capacity but still involves database queries, which are slower than in-memory caching and might not alleviate the load sufficiently for ''frequently accessed'' data.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain19_4',
  NOW(),
  6,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_19_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_19_m_19_1_71',
  'A mobile gaming company hosts its multiplayer game servers on Amazon EC2 instances. These servers communicate with game clients using custom TCP protocols over specific ports, not HTTP or HTTPS. The company needs a solution to distribute incoming game client connections evenly across multiple game servers and ensure high availability. The solution must handle millions of concurrent connections with extremely low latency. Which AWS service is the MOST appropriate choice for load balancing this type of traffic?',
  'mcq',
  '["An Application Load Balancer (ALB) configured with a target group of Amazon EC2 instances.","An Amazon CloudFront distribution with an origin pointing to the Amazon EC2 instances.","A Network Load Balancer (NLB) configured with a target group of Amazon EC2 instances.","An Amazon API Gateway endpoint configured as a proxy for the Amazon EC2 instances."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'A Network Load Balancer (NLB) (Option 2) is specifically designed for high-performance TCP/UDP traffic, making it the most appropriate choice for distributing game client connections using custom TCP protocols. NLB can handle millions of requests per second with ultra-low latency. Option 0 (Application Load Balancer) only supports HTTP/HTTPS traffic and is not suitable for custom TCP protocols (Anti-pattern: ap-2.1-002). Option 1 (Amazon CloudFront) is a Content Delivery Network (CDN) primarily for caching web content and accelerating HTTP/HTTPS traffic, not for direct TCP load balancing. Option 3 (Amazon API Gateway) is for building, deploying, and managing RESTful APIs and WebSocket APIs, primarily for HTTP/HTTPS-based microservices, not raw TCP game server traffic.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain19_4',
  NOW(),
  7,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_19_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_19_m_19_1_72',
  'A company is refactoring its monolithic application into microservices. The new architecture requires several microservices to communicate with each other asynchronously to process customer orders. Each order processing step can take varying amounts of time, and the system must be resilient to individual service failures without impacting the entire order workflow. The team wants to avoid direct, synchronous API calls between services to prevent tight coupling and cascading failures. Which combination of AWS services should be used to achieve this loosely coupled, event-driven communication?',
  'mcq',
  '["Use Amazon API Gateway for each microservice and have them make direct HTTP calls to each other.","Implement AWS Step Functions to orchestrate synchronous calls between AWS Lambda functions for each microservice.","Utilize an Application Load Balancer to distribute requests directly to the appropriate Amazon EC2 instances running each microservice.","Publish events to Amazon SNS topics, which then fan out messages to Amazon SQS queues, with AWS Lambda functions consuming messages from the queues."]'::json,
  '{3}',
  'Publishing events to Amazon SNS topics that fan out to Amazon SQS queues, with AWS Lambda functions consuming messages (Option 3), is an excellent pattern for loosely coupled, event-driven microservices. Amazon SNS provides a publish/subscribe mechanism, and Amazon SQS queues act as buffers, ensuring messages are not lost and services can process them at their own pace. AWS Lambda functions can then process these messages asynchronously. Option 0 (direct HTTP calls via API Gateway) creates tight coupling and can lead to cascading failures (Anti-pattern: ap-2.1-003). Option 1 (AWS Step Functions with synchronous Lambda calls) is for orchestrating workflows but implies synchronous steps by default, which is not ideal for loosely coupled asynchronous communication unless designed specifically for asynchronous callbacks. Option 2 (ALB distributing requests directly) is a load balancing solution for synchronous HTTP traffic and does not provide the asynchronous decoupling required.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain19_4',
  NOW(),
  8,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_19_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_19_m_19_1_73',
  'An e-commerce company hosts its web application on Amazon EC2 instances behind an Application Load Balancer. Users frequently complain about losing their shopping cart contents or login status when navigating the site, especially during periods of high traffic when instances are scaled. The company needs to ensure that user sessions are consistently maintained across all instances, even as the Auto Scaling group adds or removes instances. Which approach should the Solutions Architect recommend to manage user session state effectively and enable horizontal scaling?',
  'mcq',
  '["Store session data in a distributed, in-memory cache like Amazon ElastiCache for Redis or Amazon DynamoDB.","Enable sticky sessions (session affinity) on the Application Load Balancer to route users to the same instance.","Configure the Amazon EC2 instances to store session data in local file systems and replicate it across instances.","Increase the instance size of the Amazon EC2 instances to handle more concurrent sessions locally."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Storing session data in a distributed, external store like Amazon ElastiCache for Redis or Amazon DynamoDB (Option 0) is the best practice for managing user sessions in a horizontally scalable web application. This decouples session state from individual EC2 instances, making the application stateless and resilient to instance failures or scaling events. Option 1 (sticky sessions on ALB) can help but hinders true horizontal scalability and resilience, as it creates dependencies on specific instances and can lead to uneven load distribution or session loss if an instance fails (Anti-pattern: ap-2.1-004). Option 2 (local file system replication) is complex to implement, inefficient, and prone to consistency issues. Option 3 (increasing instance size) is vertical scaling, which has limits and does not solve the fundamental problem of session persistence across multiple instances.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain19_4',
  NOW(),
  9,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_19_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_19_m_19_1_74',
  'A data processing pipeline uses an Amazon SQS queue to buffer incoming messages before they are processed by an AWS Lambda function. The Lambda function polls the SQS queue for new messages. The current configuration uses short polling, which results in many empty responses and increased API call costs, especially during periods of low message volume. The company wants to optimize costs and reduce the number of empty responses without impacting message delivery. Which change should be implemented for the Amazon SQS queue configuration?',
  'mcq',
  '["Increase the visibility timeout of the Amazon SQS queue.","Configure a Dead-Letter Queue (DLQ) for the Amazon SQS queue.","Reduce the maximum message size allowed in the Amazon SQS queue.","Enable long polling by setting the WaitTimeSeconds parameter for the Amazon SQS queue."]'::json,
  '{3}',
  'Enabling long polling by setting the WaitTimeSeconds parameter (Option 3) for the Amazon SQS queue is the correct solution. Long polling allows the SQS queue to wait for messages to arrive before sending a response, reducing the number of empty responses and the associated API call costs. This is a direct counter to the anti-pattern of short polling causing increased cost (Anti-pattern: ap-2.1-001). Option 0 (increasing visibility timeout) prevents messages from being processed multiple times but does not reduce empty receives. Option 1 (configuring a DLQ) is for handling failed messages, not for optimizing polling efficiency. Option 2 (reducing maximum message size) affects message content, not polling behavior or cost efficiency.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain19_4',
  NOW(),
  10,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_19_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_19_m_19_1_75',
  'A small startup is developing a new mobile application that requires a backend API to manage user profiles and retrieve product information. The company has limited development resources and wants to focus on application logic rather than infrastructure management. The API needs to be highly scalable, handle unpredictable traffic patterns, and incur costs only when actively used. Which serverless architecture should the Solutions Architect recommend for building this API with the LEAST operational overhead?',
  'mcq',
  '["Use Amazon API Gateway to create RESTful endpoints that invoke AWS Lambda functions, storing data in Amazon DynamoDB.","Deploy the API on Amazon EC2 instances within an Auto Scaling group behind an Application Load Balancer, using Amazon RDS for data storage.","Implement the API using AWS Fargate for containerized microservices, with an Amazon Aurora database.","Develop the API using AWS AppSync for GraphQL endpoints, backed by Amazon OpenSearch Service for data storage."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Using Amazon API Gateway with AWS Lambda and Amazon DynamoDB (Option 0) provides a fully serverless architecture with the least operational overhead. Amazon API Gateway handles API management, AWS Lambda executes the backend logic without server provisioning, and Amazon DynamoDB offers a fully managed NoSQL database. All these services scale automatically and operate on a pay-per-use model, aligning with the company''s goals. Option 1 (EC2, ALB, RDS) involves managing servers and databases, leading to higher operational overhead. Option 2 (AWS Fargate with Aurora) is also a managed container solution, but it still involves more configuration and management than a pure Lambda-based serverless approach, and Aurora is a relational database with more operational considerations than DynamoDB for simple API backends. Option 3 (AWS AppSync with OpenSearch) is a GraphQL solution, which might be an overkill for a simple RESTful API requirement, and OpenSearch is typically for search and analytics, not primary transactional data storage.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain19_4',
  NOW(),
  11,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_19_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_19_m_19_1_76',
  'An online retail company hosts its static website content (HTML, CSS, JavaScript, images) in an Amazon S3 bucket. Customers accessing the website from various geographical locations experience inconsistent load times, particularly those far from the AWS Region where the S3 bucket is located. The company wants to improve the website''s global performance and reduce the load on the S3 origin for frequently accessed content. Which AWS service should be implemented to achieve these goals?',
  'mcq',
  '["Configure an Application Load Balancer (ALB) in front of the Amazon S3 bucket.","Implement Amazon CloudFront as a Content Delivery Network (CDN) for the Amazon S3 bucket.","Use Amazon Route 53 latency-based routing to direct users to the closest Amazon S3 Region.","Migrate the static content from Amazon S3 to Amazon EBS volumes attached to Amazon EC2 instances."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Implementing Amazon CloudFront as a Content Delivery Network (CDN) (Option 1) is the most effective solution for improving global website performance and reducing load on the Amazon S3 origin. CloudFront caches static content at edge locations closer to users worldwide, significantly reducing latency and improving load times. It also offloads requests from the S3 bucket. Option 0 (ALB in front of S3) is not a standard or efficient way to serve static content from S3; S3 itself is an object storage service that can serve content directly. Option 2 (Route 53 latency-based routing) would direct users to the closest S3 bucket if content were replicated across regions, but it doesn''t cache content at edge locations or reduce origin load in the same way CloudFront does. Option 3 (migrating to EBS/EC2) would introduce significant operational overhead and cost for static content that S3 and CloudFront are designed to handle efficiently.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain19_4',
  NOW(),
  12,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_19_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

