INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_19_m_19_2_67',
  'A company operates a fleet of Amazon EC2 instances running a stateless API backend. Individual instances can occasionally become unhealthy or unresponsive due to software issues or underlying infrastructure problems. The company needs an automated mechanism to detect these failures and replace the unhealthy instances without manual intervention, ensuring continuous service availability. Which combination of services provides the MOST resilient and automated self-healing architecture?',
  'mcq',
  '["An Application Load Balancer with health checks configured to monitor the Amazon EC2 instances, and an Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling group to automatically replace unhealthy instances.","Amazon Route 53 health checks configured to monitor the public IP addresses of the Amazon EC2 instances, with manual intervention to replace instances detected as unhealthy.","AWS CloudWatch alarms configured to monitor CPU utilization of individual Amazon EC2 instances, triggering an AWS Lambda function to terminate and relaunch instances when thresholds are breached.","A custom script running on a dedicated Amazon EC2 instance that periodically pings other instances and uses the AWS CLI to terminate and launch replacements for unresponsive ones."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'An Application Load Balancer (ALB) with health checks, combined with an Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling group, provides a fully automated and resilient self-healing architecture. The ALB detects unhealthy instances via health checks and stops sending traffic to them, while the Auto Scaling group automatically terminates and replaces them, maintaining desired capacity. Option 1 uses Route 53 health checks, but these are primarily for DNS failover and do not automatically replace instances, requiring manual intervention. Option 2 uses CloudWatch alarms and Lambda, which is a custom solution with higher operational overhead and complexity compared to native Auto Scaling. Option 3 is a custom, self-managed solution that introduces a single point of failure (the dedicated EC2 instance) and significantly higher operational overhead.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain19_4',
  NOW(),
  1,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_19_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_19_m_19_2_68',
  'A startup has a web application deployed in a single AWS Region. They need a disaster recovery strategy to protect against a regional outage, with a Recovery Time Objective (RTO) of several hours and a Recovery Point Objective (RPO) of up to one day. They want to minimize ongoing costs in the secondary Region while maintaining the ability to recover the application. Which disaster recovery strategy is MOST cost-effective for these requirements?',
  'mcq',
  '["Implement a warm standby strategy by running a scaled-down version of the application in a secondary AWS Region with Amazon RDS cross-Region read replicas and Amazon S3 cross-Region replication.","Utilize a multi-site active/active strategy by deploying the full application stack in two separate AWS Regions and distributing traffic using Amazon Route 53 with weighted routing.","Adopt a pilot light strategy by maintaining Amazon S3 cross-Region replication and an Amazon RDS cross-Region read replica, with pre-configured but stopped Amazon EC2 instances in the DR Region.","Rely on a backup and restore strategy by using AWS Backup to regularly back up Amazon EC2 instance data and Amazon S3 buckets to a secondary Region, restoring only when a disaster occurs."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'A pilot light strategy is the most cost-effective for an RTO of several hours and an RPO of up to one day. It involves maintaining core services (like RDS read replicas and S3 replication) in the DR Region, with compute resources (EC2 instances) pre-configured but stopped, only to be launched during a disaster. This minimizes ongoing costs. Option 0 (warm standby) is more expensive as it requires running scaled-down compute resources continuously. Option 1 (multi-site active/active) is the most expensive as it requires running a full, active stack in two regions. Option 3 (backup and restore) might not reliably meet an RTO of ''several hours'' for a full application stack, as restoration times can be unpredictable and manual effort is high.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain19_4',
  NOW(),
  2,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_19_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_19_m_19_2_69',
  'A data analytics company uses an Amazon RDS MySQL database for its reporting application. The application experiences high read loads, and the company needs to improve read performance while also ensuring high availability for the database in case of an Availability Zone outage. The solution should be robust and minimize manual intervention for failover. Which configuration meets these requirements MOST cost-effectively?',
  'mcq',
  '["Upgrade the Amazon RDS instance to a larger instance type with Provisioned IOPS (io1) storage to handle both high read loads and provide higher availability.","Enable Amazon RDS Multi-AZ deployment for high availability and provision one or more Amazon RDS read replicas to offload read traffic from the primary instance.","Migrate the entire database to Amazon DynamoDB to leverage its high scalability and built-in fault tolerance for read-heavy workloads.","Enable Amazon RDS Multi-AZ deployment only, assuming the standby instance will automatically serve read traffic during normal operations."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Configuring Amazon RDS Multi-AZ deployment provides high availability with automatic failover to a standby instance in a different Availability Zone. Adding Amazon RDS read replicas is the correct approach to scale read performance by distributing read queries across multiple instances, offloading the primary. Option 0 (upgrading instance type) is a vertical scaling approach that has a performance ceiling and is generally less cost-effective than read replicas for read scaling. Option 2 (DynamoDB migration) is a significant re-architecture and not cost-effective for an existing relational database. Option 3 (Multi-AZ only) is incorrect because the Multi-AZ standby replica is for failover only and does not serve read traffic, which is a common misconception (anti-pattern: ap-2.2-001).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain19_4',
  NOW(),
  3,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_19_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_19_m_19_2_70',
  'A startup is launching a new SaaS product and initially plans to deploy all its Amazon EC2 instances and Amazon RDS database in a single Availability Zone to simplify deployment. They intend to re-architect for high availability later as the business grows. What is the primary risk associated with this initial single Availability Zone deployment strategy?',
  'mcq',
  '["It will lead to significantly higher latency for users accessing the application from different geographic locations.","It creates a single point of failure, making the entire application vulnerable to an outage if that specific Availability Zone experiences an issue, and retrofitting high availability is complex and risky.","It will prevent the use of Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling, limiting the application''s ability to scale horizontally.","It will be more expensive to deploy resources in a single Availability Zone compared to distributing them across multiple Availability Zones from the start."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Deploying all resources in a single Availability Zone creates a single point of failure. If that AZ experiences an outage (e.g., power loss, network issues), the entire application becomes unavailable. Retrofitting high availability into an existing single-AZ architecture is significantly more complex, time-consuming, and risky than designing for multi-AZ from the outset (anti-pattern: ap-2.2-003). Option 0 (higher latency) is not the primary risk; latency depends more on user proximity to the Region. Option 2 (prevents Auto Scaling) is false; Auto Scaling can operate within a single AZ, though it doesn''t provide AZ-level resilience. Option 3 (more expensive) is generally false; a single AZ deployment is initially cheaper, but the cost of downtime and retrofitting can be much higher.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain19_4',
  NOW(),
  4,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_19_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_19_m_19_2_71',
  'A company has deployed its web application across two Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling groups in different AWS Regions for disaster recovery. They have configured Amazon Route 53 with a failover routing policy to direct traffic to the secondary Region if the primary becomes unhealthy. What critical component is missing from this configuration to ensure automatic detection of the primary Region''s unhealthiness and subsequent failover?',
  'mcq',
  '["An Application Load Balancer in the primary Region to distribute traffic to healthy EC2 instances.","Amazon Route 53 health checks associated with the primary endpoint to monitor its availability and trigger the failover policy.","AWS Global Accelerator to improve performance and automatically route traffic to the closest healthy endpoint.","An Amazon SQS queue to buffer requests during the failover process, preventing data loss."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Amazon Route 53 failover routing policies require associated Route 53 health checks to monitor the health of the primary endpoint. Without these health checks, Route 53 has no mechanism to detect that the primary Region is unhealthy and will not automatically trigger the failover to the secondary Region (anti-pattern: ap-2.2-004). Option 0 (Application Load Balancer) is important for distributing traffic within a Region and performing instance-level health checks, but Route 53 needs its own health checks for DNS-level failover. Option 2 (AWS Global Accelerator) can improve performance and routing but doesn''t replace the need for Route 53 health checks for failover. Option 3 (Amazon SQS queue) is for message decoupling, not for DNS failover.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain19_4',
  NOW(),
  5,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_19_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────
-- Module: m_19_1
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_19_m_19_1_77',
  'A development team is building a new microservices application using Docker containers. They need to deploy and manage these containers on AWS, but they want to avoid managing the underlying Amazon EC2 instances, including patching, scaling, and networking configuration for the cluster control plane. The primary goal is to minimize operational overhead for the container infrastructure. Which AWS service combination best meets this requirement?',
  'mcq',
  '["Deploy containers using Amazon ECS with the Amazon EC2 launch type, managing the EC2 instances in an Auto Scaling group.","Deploy containers using Amazon ECS with the AWS Fargate launch type, which abstracts away the underlying infrastructure management.","Deploy containers using Amazon EKS, managing the Kubernetes control plane and worker nodes on Amazon EC2 instances.","Manually deploy Docker containers directly onto Amazon EC2 instances, using a custom orchestration tool for management."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Amazon ECS with the AWS Fargate launch type is designed to minimize operational overhead by abstracting away the underlying infrastructure. With Fargate, you only need to define your container specifications, and AWS manages the EC2 instances, patching, scaling, and cluster networking. Option 0 (ECS with EC2 launch type) requires managing the EC2 instances. Option 2 (Amazon EKS) involves managing the Kubernetes control plane (unless using EKS Fargate, which is still more complex than ECS Fargate for simple container deployment) and worker nodes. Option 3 (manual deployment on EC2) has the highest operational overhead as it requires full manual management of all aspects of the container environment.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain19_4',
  NOW(),
  6,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_19_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_19_m_19_1_78',
  'A new social media platform is experiencing rapid, unpredictable growth, leading to sudden traffic spikes. The backend is built on stateless Amazon EC2 instances. The company needs to automatically scale its compute capacity to match demand, ensuring consistent performance during spikes while minimizing costs during low-traffic periods. Which strategy provides the MOST cost-effective and responsive solution for scaling compute resources?',
  'mcq',
  '["Manually scale Amazon EC2 instances up and down based on monitoring AWS CloudWatch alarms for CPU utilization.","Configure an Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling group with dynamic scaling policies based on metrics like CPU utilization or Application Load Balancer request count, behind an Application Load Balancer.","Use larger Amazon EC2 instance types (vertical scaling) to handle peak loads, ensuring they are always provisioned for the maximum expected traffic.","Deploy all Amazon EC2 instances in a single Availability Zone and scale vertically by upgrading instance types as traffic increases."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'An Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling group with dynamic scaling policies (e.g., target tracking for CPU utilization or ALB request count) behind an Application Load Balancer is the most cost-effective and responsive solution. It automatically adjusts compute capacity to match demand, scaling out during spikes and scaling in during low periods, optimizing costs. Option 0 (manual scaling) is labor-intensive and slow to react to sudden spikes. Option 2 (vertical scaling with larger instances) is not cost-effective for unpredictable spikes as you pay for unused capacity during low traffic. Option 3 (single AZ vertical scaling) introduces a single point of failure and does not provide horizontal scalability or resilience.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain19_4',
  NOW(),
  7,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_19_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_19_m_19_1_79',
  'A mobile application development team needs to expose a set of backend services implemented as AWS Lambda functions to their mobile clients. The solution must handle millions of requests per day, provide secure access with authentication, and scale automatically without managing servers. The team also requires features like request throttling and API key management. Which AWS service is the MOST suitable for creating and managing these API endpoints?',
  'mcq',
  '["Deploy an Application Load Balancer in front of Amazon EC2 instances running a custom API gateway application.","Use Amazon API Gateway to create a REST API endpoint that integrates with AWS Lambda functions, enabling features like authentication, throttling, and API key management.","Directly invoke AWS Lambda functions from mobile clients using AWS SDKs, handling authentication and authorization within the Lambda code.","Utilize Amazon CloudFront to cache API responses and distribute requests to the AWS Lambda functions directly."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Amazon API Gateway is specifically designed for creating, publishing, maintaining, monitoring, and securing REST, HTTP, and WebSocket APIs at any scale. It seamlessly integrates with AWS Lambda, provides built-in features like authentication, throttling, and API key management, and scales automatically without server management. Option 0 (ALB with EC2) introduces server management overhead and requires implementing API management features manually. Option 2 (direct Lambda invocation) bypasses API management features, posing security and scalability challenges for public-facing APIs. Option 3 (CloudFront) is a Content Delivery Network (CDN) primarily for caching and distribution, not for full API management and backend integration.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain19_4',
  NOW(),
  8,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_19_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_19_m_19_1_80',
  'A team has an application that processes messages from an Amazon SQS queue. The application polls the queue frequently but often receives empty responses, leading to unnecessary API calls and increased costs. The team wants to optimize the message consumption to reduce costs and improve efficiency by minimizing empty responses. Which configuration change should the team implement?',
  'mcq',
  '["Increase the polling frequency of the Amazon SQS queue to ensure messages are retrieved faster.","Configure the Amazon SQS consumer application to use long polling by setting the `WaitTimeSeconds` parameter to a value greater than 0.","Decrease the `VisibilityTimeout` of the Amazon SQS queue to make messages available for processing sooner.","Migrate the message queuing system from Amazon SQS to Amazon SNS for message delivery to reduce polling."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Configuring the Amazon SQS consumer application to use long polling by setting `WaitTimeSeconds` to a value greater than 0 is the correct solution. Long polling allows the `ReceiveMessage` API call to wait for messages to arrive in the queue, reducing the number of empty responses and thus decreasing API call costs and improving efficiency (anti-pattern: ap-2.1-001). Option 0 (increasing polling frequency) would exacerbate the problem by increasing empty responses and costs. Option 2 (decreasing `VisibilityTimeout`) affects how long a message is invisible after being read, not the efficiency of polling. Option 3 (migrating to SNS) is a different messaging pattern (pub/sub) and does not address the issue of inefficient polling for a queue-based consumer.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain19_4',
  NOW(),
  9,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_19_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

