INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_2_43',
  'A startup is developing a new image processing service. Users upload images, and the service performs several complex, CPU-intensive transformations that can take anywhere from 5 to 20 minutes per image. The company expects variable load, with peak times requiring hundreds of concurrent transformations, but wants to pay only for the compute resources consumed during actual processing, minimizing idle capacity costs.',
  'mcq',
  '["Deploy the image processing application on Amazon EC2 instances within an Auto Scaling group configured for CPU utilization, ensuring capacity scales with demand.","Utilize AWS Lambda to execute the image processing code, triggering a new function invocation for each image upload, leveraging its serverless nature.","Package the image processing application as a container and deploy it on AWS Fargate, triggered by new image uploads, to handle variable, long-running tasks.","Implement the image processing as a job on AWS Batch, submitting jobs to a queue that processes them using a fleet of EC2 instances managed by AWS Batch."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'AWS Fargate (Option C) is the most appropriate solution. It allows running containers without managing the underlying EC2 instances, providing a serverless experience. Its primary advantage here is that it supports longer-running tasks (up to 1440 minutes) compared to AWS Lambda, and you only pay for the compute resources consumed during the task''s execution, perfectly aligning with the goal of minimizing idle capacity costs for variable, long-running jobs. AWS Lambda (Option B) has a maximum execution duration of 15 minutes, which is insufficient for tasks that can take up to 20 minutes (Anti-pattern: ap-3.2-001). Deploying on Amazon EC2 with Auto Scaling (Option A) would require managing EC2 instances and potentially incur costs for idle capacity during low demand, even with scaling policies. AWS Batch (Option D) is suitable for batch processing but typically involves managing compute environments or using Fargate as a compute environment; for variable, individual long-running tasks, Fargate directly offers a more serverless and cost-effective approach without the overhead of batch job orchestration if not strictly needed.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  1,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_2_44',
  'A data analytics team needs to run daily batch processing jobs that analyze large datasets. These jobs are fault-tolerant, meaning they can be interrupted and resumed without significant loss of progress. The team is highly cost-conscious and wants to achieve the lowest possible compute cost for these non-urgent, flexible workloads.',
  'mcq',
  '["Configure AWS Batch to run the jobs using EC2 Spot Instances in its compute environment.","Provision a fleet of Amazon EC2 On-Demand Instances and use a custom script to schedule and run the batch jobs daily.","Utilize AWS Lambda functions orchestrated by AWS Step Functions to process the data in small, parallel chunks.","Deploy the processing application on Amazon EMR clusters, using On-Demand Instances for the entire cluster to ensure stability."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Configuring AWS Batch to use EC2 Spot Instances (Option A) is the most cost-effective solution for fault-tolerant batch jobs. Spot Instances offer significant savings (up to 90%) compared to On-Demand pricing, and AWS Batch can manage the interruptions by automatically provisioning new instances or resuming jobs. This aligns with the requirement for the lowest possible compute cost for flexible workloads (Anti-pattern: ap-3.2-003). Provisioning Amazon EC2 On-Demand Instances (Option B) would be significantly more expensive. AWS Lambda (Option C) has a 15-minute execution limit, which might not be suitable for large dataset processing, and its cost model might not be the lowest for sustained, large-scale batch processing. Using Amazon EMR with On-Demand Instances (Option D) is also more expensive than using Spot Instances for fault-tolerant workloads.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  2,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_2_45',
  'A web application experiences unpredictable traffic spikes throughout the day, sometimes increasing demand by 500% within minutes. The operations team struggles to manually scale EC2 instances up and down to meet this demand, leading to either over-provisioning and wasted costs or under-provisioning and poor user experience. The company needs an automated solution to match compute capacity to demand.',
  'mcq',
  '["Manually adjust the number of Amazon EC2 instances based on historical traffic patterns and anticipated spikes.","Implement an Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling group with dynamic scaling policies based on CPU utilization or network I/O.","Deploy the application on AWS Fargate, allowing it to automatically scale container instances based on incoming request volume.","Use AWS Lambda for the web application''s backend, leveraging its automatic scaling capabilities to handle traffic fluctuations."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Implementing an Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling group with dynamic scaling policies (Option B) is the ideal automated solution. Auto Scaling groups automatically adjust the number of EC2 instances in response to actual demand, preventing both over-provisioning (wasted costs) and under-provisioning (poor user experience) (Anti-pattern: ap-3.2-002). Manually adjusting instances (Option A) is precisely the problem the company is trying to solve and is not an automated solution. AWS Fargate (Option C) is a serverless container service that can scale, but the question specifically mentions EC2 instances and the need for an automated solution for existing EC2-based applications. AWS Lambda (Option D) is serverless and scales automatically, but it''s typically used for event-driven functions, not as a direct replacement for a traditional web application running on EC2 instances, and may require significant re-architecture.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  3,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_2_46',
  'A company runs a legacy application on a single EC2 instance that requires a specific, large amount of memory and CPU. The application''s workload is consistent and predictable, but the current instance type (m5.large) is insufficient, causing performance bottlenecks. The team needs to upgrade the instance to meet performance requirements without increasing operational complexity.',
  'mcq',
  '["Migrate the application to a larger Amazon EC2 instance type, such as m5.xlarge or r5.large, through a stop-start operation.","Implement an Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling group to add more m5.large instances and distribute the workload across them.","Re-architect the application to be serverless and deploy it on AWS Lambda functions to handle the increased memory and CPU demands.","Containerize the application and deploy it on AWS Fargate, configuring it with higher CPU and memory resources."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Migrating the application to a larger Amazon EC2 instance type (Option A) is the most straightforward way to meet increased memory and CPU requirements for a single-instance legacy application without increasing operational complexity. This is known as vertical scaling. Implementing an Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling group (Option B) is for horizontal scaling, which would involve re-architecting the application to be distributed, adding significant operational complexity for a single-instance legacy application. Re-architecting to AWS Lambda (Option C) or containerizing for AWS Fargate (Option D) would involve substantial re-development and testing, which goes against the requirement of ''without increasing operational complexity'' for a legacy application.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  4,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────
-- Module: m_20_4
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_4_40',
  'A company hosts a popular e-commerce website with global customers. The website serves a mix of static content (images, CSS, JavaScript) and dynamic, personalized user data. Users frequently complain about slow loading times, especially those far from the primary AWS Region. The solutions architect needs to improve content delivery speed for all users, globally, for both static and dynamic content.',
  'mcq',
  '["Deploy the application in a single AWS Region and use Amazon Route 53 latency-based routing to direct users to the closest DNS server.","Utilize Amazon CloudFront with caching for static assets and configure it to forward dynamic requests to the origin with appropriate cache behavior settings.","Implement AWS Global Accelerator to route all user traffic through the AWS global network to the application''s Elastic Load Balancer.","Migrate all static content to Amazon S3 and serve it directly from the S3 bucket, while keeping dynamic content on EC2 instances."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Utilizing Amazon CloudFront (Option B) is the most effective solution for improving content delivery speed globally for both static and dynamic content. CloudFront caches static assets at edge locations close to users, significantly reducing latency. For dynamic content, CloudFront can optimize the connection to the origin, perform TLS termination at the edge, and forward requests, improving performance even if the content is not cached (Anti-pattern: ap-3.4-002). Amazon Route 53 latency-based routing (Option A) only directs DNS queries to the closest DNS server, not the content itself, and doesn''t cache content. AWS Global Accelerator (Option C) improves network routing to the application endpoints but does not cache content, making it less suitable for static content delivery. Serving static content directly from Amazon S3 (Option D) would not leverage a global CDN, meaning users far from the S3 bucket''s region would still experience high latency.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  5,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_4',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_4_41',
  'An enterprise has multiple VPCs across several AWS accounts, each hosting a different department''s applications. They need to establish secure and scalable network connectivity between all these VPCs, allowing any VPC to communicate with any other VPC, and also providing a centralized egress point to the internet. The current setup using individual VPC peering connections is becoming unmanageable.',
  'mcq',
  '["Create a mesh of VPC peering connections between every pair of VPCs to ensure full connectivity.","Establish a central Transit VPC using a dedicated EC2 instance running routing software to manage all inter-VPC traffic.","Implement AWS Transit Gateway, connecting all VPCs to it and configuring routing tables for inter-VPC communication and centralized egress.","Use AWS VPN connections to establish site-to-site VPNs between each VPC and a central on-premises firewall."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Implementing AWS Transit Gateway (Option C) is the most scalable and manageable solution for connecting multiple VPCs in a hub-and-spoke model and providing centralized egress. Transit Gateway acts as a central hub, simplifying network architecture and management, especially with a growing number of VPCs (Anti-pattern: ap-3.4-003). Creating a mesh of VPC peering connections (Option A) becomes unmanageable as the number of VPCs grows (O(n^2) connections). A central Transit VPC with an EC2 instance (Option B) is an older pattern that introduces a single point of failure and requires managing the EC2 instance, which is less scalable and more operationally complex than Transit Gateway. Using AWS VPN connections to an on-premises firewall (Option D) is for hybrid connectivity, not for inter-VPC communication within AWS.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  6,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_4',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_4_42',
  'A company needs to establish a dedicated, private network connection between its on-premises data center and its AWS VPC. The connection must provide consistent, high-bandwidth, and low-latency connectivity for transferring large datasets and supporting real-time applications. The existing internet-based VPN solution is experiencing inconsistent performance and is not meeting the throughput requirements.',
  'mcq',
  '["Implement AWS Direct Connect to establish a dedicated network connection from the on-premises data center to an AWS Direct Connect location.","Upgrade the existing AWS VPN connection to a higher bandwidth tier and configure it with redundant tunnels.","Use AWS Storage Gateway to create a file gateway on-premises to synchronize data to Amazon S3.","Set up a dedicated Amazon EC2 instance in a public subnet to act as a proxy for all traffic between on-premises and AWS."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Implementing AWS Direct Connect (Option A) is the correct solution for establishing a dedicated, private, consistent high-bandwidth, and low-latency connection between an on-premises data center and AWS. It bypasses the public internet, offering predictable performance. The existing internet-based VPN (Option B) is explicitly stated as insufficient due to inconsistent performance and throughput (Anti-pattern: ap-3.4-001). AWS Storage Gateway (Option C) is for data transfer and synchronization, not for establishing a general-purpose network connection for all applications. A dedicated EC2 instance as a proxy (Option D) introduces a single point of failure, adds latency, and is not a scalable or secure solution for a dedicated private connection.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  7,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_4',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_4_43',
  'A new web application is being deployed on Amazon EC2 instances within a private subnet. The application needs to distribute incoming HTTP/HTTPS traffic across multiple instances, automatically scale based on demand, and ensure high availability by directing traffic only to healthy instances. The solutions architect needs to implement a solution that provides these capabilities with minimal management overhead.',
  'mcq',
  '["Configure a Network Load Balancer (NLB) to distribute TCP traffic to the EC2 instances and use Route 53 health checks.","Set up an Amazon EC2 instance with Nginx to act as a reverse proxy and manually manage its scaling and health checks.","Deploy an Application Load Balancer (ALB) in front of the EC2 instances, integrated with an Auto Scaling group and health checks.","Use Amazon CloudFront to distribute traffic globally and cache static content, pointing it to the EC2 instances directly."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Deploying an Application Load Balancer (ALB) (Option C) is the most appropriate solution. ALBs are designed for HTTP/HTTPS traffic, can distribute requests across multiple EC2 instances, integrate seamlessly with Auto Scaling groups for automatic scaling, and perform health checks to ensure traffic is only sent to healthy instances. This provides high availability and minimal management overhead. A Network Load Balancer (NLB) (Option A) operates at the TCP layer and is not ideal for HTTP/HTTPS specific features like path-based routing or host-based routing. An EC2 instance with Nginx (Option B) would require significant manual management for scaling, health checks, and high availability, increasing operational overhead. Amazon CloudFront (Option D) is a Content Delivery Network (CDN) primarily for caching and global distribution, not for load balancing internal EC2 instances within a VPC for a primary application backend.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  8,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_4',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_4_44',
  'A global gaming company hosts its game servers in multiple AWS Regions. They need to provide a single entry point for players worldwide, ensuring that player traffic is routed to the closest healthy game server endpoint, regardless of whether it''s an EC2 instance or an Elastic Load Balancer. The solution must optimize for network performance and fault tolerance across Regions.',
  'mcq',
  '["Use Amazon CloudFront to distribute game content and route player connections to the nearest origin.","Configure Amazon Route 53 with latency-based routing to direct players to the closest game server IP address.","Implement an Application Load Balancer (ALB) in each Region and use a global DNS service to point to the closest ALB.","Utilize AWS Global Accelerator to route user traffic through the AWS global network to optimal application endpoints in different Regions."]'::json,
  '{3}',
  'AWS Global Accelerator (Option D) is designed for this exact scenario. It uses the AWS global network to route user traffic to the closest healthy endpoint, providing a single entry point and optimizing network performance and fault tolerance for non-HTTP/HTTPS traffic like game servers. Amazon CloudFront (Option A) is primarily a CDN for caching web content and is less suitable for direct game server connections. Amazon Route 53 with latency-based routing (Option B) relies on DNS resolution, which can be cached by clients and doesn''t optimize the actual network path beyond the DNS lookup. An Application Load Balancer (ALB) (Option C) is for HTTP/HTTPS traffic and is regional, requiring an additional global routing mechanism, which Global Accelerator provides more effectively.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  9,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_4',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────
-- Module: m_20_1
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_1_35',
  'A research institution needs to store petabytes of scientific research data. This data is rarely accessed after its initial upload, but when it is, retrieval within a few hours is acceptable. The primary concern is to minimize storage costs while ensuring long-term durability and availability. The data consists of large, immutable files.',
  'mcq',
  '["Store the data in Amazon S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval, leveraging its low cost and retrieval options.","Keep the data in Amazon S3 Standard-Infrequent Access (S3 Standard-IA) for quick retrieval when needed.","Utilize Amazon EBS Cold HDD (sc1) volumes attached to a fleet of EC2 instances for archival storage.","Store the data in Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive, configured for bulk retrievals to minimize costs further."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Storing the data in Amazon S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval (Option A) is the most cost-effective solution that meets the retrieval requirement. It offers very low storage costs and allows retrieval within minutes to hours (typically 3-5 hours for standard retrieval), which is well within the ''few hours'' acceptable timeframe. Amazon S3 Standard-IA (Option B) would be significantly more expensive for rarely accessed petabytes of data. Amazon EBS Cold HDD (sc1) volumes (Option C) are block storage, not object storage, and are not designed for petabyte-scale archival of immutable files, nor would they be as cost-effective or durable as S3 Glacier. Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive (Option D) offers even lower costs but has a minimum retrieval time of 12 hours for standard retrieval, which might not consistently meet the ''few hours'' requirement.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  10,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_1_36',
  'A database administrator needs to provision block storage for an Amazon RDS for MySQL instance. The database requires consistent baseline performance of at least 5000 IOPS and 200 MiB/s throughput, regardless of burst credits. The primary goal is to ensure predictable performance for the database workload at the lowest possible cost.',
  'mcq',
  '["Provision an Amazon EBS General Purpose SSD (gp2) volume with sufficient size to achieve the required IOPS and throughput.","Provision an Amazon EBS General Purpose SSD (gp3) volume, specifying 5000 IOPS and 200 MiB/s throughput.","Use an Amazon EBS Provisioned IOPS SSD (io2) volume, configuring it for 5000 IOPS and 200 MiB/s throughput.","Migrate the database to Amazon DynamoDB to leverage its on-demand capacity for high IOPS and throughput."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Provisioning an Amazon EBS General Purpose SSD (gp3) volume (Option B) is the most cost-effective way to achieve consistent baseline performance for IOPS and throughput. gp3 volumes allow you to provision IOPS and throughput independently of storage size, and they provide a consistent baseline performance without relying on burst credits. Amazon EBS General Purpose SSD (gp2) (Option A) relies on burst credits for high performance and would not provide consistent baseline performance for sustained high IOPS and throughput (Anti-pattern: ap-3.1-001). Amazon EBS Provisioned IOPS SSD (io2) (Option C) would also meet the performance requirements but is typically more expensive than gp3 for these specific performance levels. Amazon DynamoDB (Option D) is a NoSQL database and not a block storage solution for an Amazon RDS instance, requiring a significant re-architecture of the database.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  11,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_1_37',
  'A development team is running a content management system (CMS) on a single Amazon EC2 instance. The CMS requires a file system that supports POSIX-compliant file operations, including file locking and append operations, for its content repository. The team needs a storage solution that offers low-latency access for this single instance.',
  'mcq',
  '["Use Amazon S3 to store the content, leveraging its object storage capabilities for high durability.","Attach an Amazon EFS file system to the EC2 instance to provide shared, POSIX-compliant storage.","Attach an Amazon EBS General Purpose SSD (gp3) volume to the EC2 instance and format it with a suitable file system.","Implement AWS Storage Gateway File Gateway to provide an NFS interface to Amazon S3 for the CMS."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Attaching an Amazon EBS General Purpose SSD (gp3) volume (Option C) is the best solution. EBS provides block storage that can be formatted with a POSIX-compliant file system (e.g., ext4, XFS) and offers the lowest latency for a single EC2 instance, directly addressing the low-latency requirement for single-instance workloads (Anti-pattern: ap-3.1-003). Amazon S3 (Option A) is object storage and does not support POSIX file system semantics like file locking or append operations (Anti-pattern: ap-3.1-002). Amazon EFS (Option B) provides shared POSIX-compliant file storage but introduces network latency, making it suboptimal for latency-sensitive single-instance workloads. AWS Storage Gateway File Gateway (Option D) provides an NFS interface to S3 but is typically used for hybrid cloud scenarios and would add more latency and complexity than direct EBS attachment for a single instance.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  12,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_1_38',
  'A company is migrating an on-premises application that relies heavily on a shared file system accessible by multiple Linux servers. The application frequently modifies files, requires strict POSIX compliance, and needs to scale horizontally by adding more compute instances that share the same data. The solutions architect needs a fully managed, scalable file storage solution.',
  'mcq',
  '["Store the application data in Amazon S3, using it as an object store for all file operations.","Attach individual Amazon EBS volumes to each Linux server, and manage data synchronization manually.","Implement Amazon EFS, mounting it across all Linux servers to provide a shared, POSIX-compliant file system.","Deploy Amazon FSx for Lustre, configuring it for high-performance computing workloads requiring parallel access."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Implementing Amazon EFS (Option C) is the most suitable solution. Amazon EFS provides a scalable, shared, and fully managed NFS file system that is POSIX-compliant, allowing multiple Linux servers to access and modify the same data concurrently. This directly addresses the requirement for a shared file system with horizontal scaling. Amazon S3 (Option A) is object storage and does not support POSIX file system semantics like file locking or append operations, making it unsuitable for this use case (Anti-pattern: ap-3.1-002). Attaching individual Amazon EBS volumes (Option B) to each server would create isolated storage, requiring complex manual data synchronization, which is not scalable or efficient. Amazon FSx for Lustre (Option D) is a high-performance file system for specific HPC workloads, which might be overkill and more complex than needed for a general shared file system, and EFS is generally preferred for broader Linux file sharing needs.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  13,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────
-- Module: m_20_3
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_3_31',
  'An e-commerce application uses an Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL database. During peak sales events, the application experiences slow query performance due to a high volume of identical read queries for popular product information. The solutions architect needs to reduce the load on the database and improve read latency for these frequently accessed items, without modifying the database schema.',
  'mcq',
  '["Add more Amazon RDS read replicas to distribute the read workload across multiple database instances.","Migrate the frequently accessed product information to Amazon DynamoDB for faster key-value lookups.","Implement Amazon ElastiCache for Redis to cache the results of popular product information queries.","Upgrade the Amazon RDS instance to a larger instance type with more CPU and memory resources."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Implementing Amazon ElastiCache for Redis (Option C) is the most effective solution to reduce database load and improve read latency for frequently accessed, identical queries without modifying the database schema. ElastiCache is an in-memory caching service that stores query results, serving subsequent identical requests directly from the cache, bypassing the database (Anti-pattern: ap-3.3-003). Adding more Amazon RDS read replicas (Option A) would distribute the read load but would still hit the database for every query, not eliminating the database load for identical queries. Migrating data to Amazon DynamoDB (Option B) would require significant application code changes and schema modification, which is against the constraint. Upgrading the Amazon RDS instance (Option D) might provide some relief but is a vertical scaling solution that doesn''t address the root cause of repeated identical queries hitting the database.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  14,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_3_32',
  'A financial application processes millions of transactions per second, requiring extremely low-latency read and write access to individual items. The data model is simple, consisting of key-value pairs, and does not require complex joins or multi-table transactions. The solutions architect needs a database solution that can scale to handle massive throughput with predictable performance.',
  'mcq',
  '["Use Amazon DynamoDB, leveraging its fully managed NoSQL capabilities for high throughput and low latency.","Deploy Amazon Aurora PostgreSQL with multiple read replicas to handle the high volume of read and write operations.","Provision an Amazon RDS for MySQL instance with Provisioned IOPS (io2) EBS volumes for predictable performance.","Implement Amazon Redshift for its analytical capabilities to process millions of transactions per second efficiently."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Amazon DynamoDB (Option A) is the ideal choice for this scenario. It is a fully managed, serverless NoSQL database service designed for high-performance, low-latency key-value workloads that require massive scale and predictable performance. Its architecture is optimized for single-item access patterns and can handle millions of requests per second. Amazon Aurora PostgreSQL (Option B) is a relational database, and while highly scalable, it is not optimized for the extreme key-value throughput described and is designed for complex relational queries (Anti-pattern: ap-3.3-002 is about *not* using DynamoDB for complex joins, this question highlights when it *is* appropriate). Amazon RDS for MySQL (Option C) is a relational database and would struggle to achieve ''millions of transactions per second'' with extremely low latency for individual items without significant sharding and operational complexity. Amazon Redshift (Option D) is a data warehouse service optimized for analytical queries over large datasets, not for high-volume transactional processing.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  15,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_3_33',
  'A serverless application built with AWS Lambda functions frequently connects to an Amazon RDS for MySQL database. The high concurrency of Lambda functions often leads to connection exhaustion on the RDS instance, causing application errors. The solutions architect needs to manage database connections more efficiently to prevent these issues.',
  'mcq',
  '["Increase the maximum connections parameter in the Amazon RDS parameter group to allow more concurrent connections.","Implement Amazon RDS Proxy between the AWS Lambda functions and the Amazon RDS database.","Refactor the AWS Lambda functions to use persistent connections and reuse them across invocations.","Migrate the database from Amazon RDS for MySQL to Amazon Aurora MySQL, which supports more connections."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Implementing Amazon RDS Proxy (Option B) is the most effective solution. RDS Proxy is a fully managed, highly available database proxy that pools and shares database connections, significantly reducing the load on the RDS instance caused by a high volume of short-lived connections from AWS Lambda functions (Anti-pattern: ap-3.3-004). Increasing the maximum connections parameter (Option A) might temporarily alleviate the issue but can lead to performance degradation on the RDS instance itself due to increased resource consumption. Refactoring Lambda functions for persistent connections (Option C) is complex and often not fully effective due to the ephemeral nature of Lambda execution environments. Migrating to Amazon Aurora MySQL (Option D) might offer higher connection limits but does not fundamentally solve the connection management challenge posed by high-concurrency serverless applications as efficiently as RDS Proxy.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  16,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_3_34',
  'A reporting application generates complex analytical reports by querying an Amazon Aurora MySQL database. These reports are read-heavy, often running long-running queries that consume significant database resources, impacting the performance of the primary transactional workload. The solutions architect needs to offload these read queries to improve the performance of the main application.',
  'mcq',
  '["Configure Amazon Aurora to use a Multi-Master setup to distribute both read and write workloads.","Create an Amazon Aurora read replica and direct all reporting application queries to this replica.","Migrate the reporting data to Amazon Redshift, an analytical data warehouse, for complex queries.","Implement Amazon ElastiCache for Memcached to cache the results of the long-running analytical queries."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Creating an Amazon Aurora read replica (Option B) and directing all reporting application queries to it is the standard and most effective way to offload read-heavy analytical workloads from the primary database instance. Read replicas are designed to handle read traffic, allowing the primary instance to focus on transactional writes and reads, thereby improving the performance of the main application. Aurora Multi-Master (Option A) is designed for write scaling and high availability across multiple masters, not specifically for offloading read-heavy analytical queries from a single primary (Anti-pattern: ap-3.3-001 is about using read replicas for write scaling, this question is about using them for read scaling). Migrating reporting data to Amazon Redshift (Option C) is a valid option for very complex, large-scale analytics, but for simply offloading read queries from an existing Aurora database, a read replica is a simpler and often sufficient solution. Amazon ElastiCache (Option D) is for caching frequently accessed data, not for processing long-running, complex analytical queries.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  17,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_3_35',
  'A global social media application needs a highly available and scalable relational database. The application experiences sudden, massive spikes in read traffic, especially during viral events. The solutions architect needs a database solution that can automatically scale read capacity to handle these unpredictable spikes without manual intervention, while maintaining high availability.',
  'mcq',
  '["Deploy Amazon RDS for MySQL with Multi-AZ and manually add read replicas during anticipated traffic spikes.","Use Amazon DynamoDB with on-demand capacity mode to handle variable read and write throughput automatically.","Implement Amazon Aurora MySQL with Aurora Auto Scaling for read replicas, configured to automatically adjust capacity.","Set up a sharded database on Amazon EC2 instances, manually managing the shards and scaling the underlying instances."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Implementing Amazon Aurora MySQL with Aurora Auto Scaling for read replicas (Option C) is the best solution. Aurora Auto Scaling automatically adjusts the number of Aurora Replicas to handle sudden, massive spikes in read traffic, ensuring high availability and scalability without manual intervention. Amazon RDS for MySQL with Multi-AZ (Option A) provides high availability but requires manual scaling of read replicas, which doesn''t meet the ''automatically scale'' requirement. Amazon DynamoDB (Option B) is a NoSQL database, not a relational one, and would require a significant re-architecture of a relational application. Setting up a sharded database on Amazon EC2 (Option D) involves significant operational overhead for manual management and scaling, which is contrary to the goal of automatic scaling and high availability with minimal intervention.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  18,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────
-- Module: m_20_5
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_5_35',
  'A company needs to transfer 300 TB of historical sensor data from its on-premises data center to Amazon S3 for long-term archival and future analytics. The on-premises internet connection is limited, and transferring this volume of data over the network would take several months. The solutions architect needs the most efficient and cost-effective method for this one-time bulk data transfer.',
  'mcq',
  '["Utilize AWS Snowball to physically ship a storage appliance to the data center, load the data, and ship it back to AWS.","Use AWS DataSync to establish a secure online data transfer link between the on-premises storage and Amazon S3.","Increase the bandwidth of the on-premises internet connection to facilitate a faster transfer using standard S3 upload tools.","Implement AWS Storage Gateway File Gateway on-premises to synchronize the data incrementally to Amazon S3."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Utilizing AWS Snowball (Option A) is the most efficient and cost-effective method for transferring 300 TB of data when the on-premises internet connection is limited. Snowball devices are designed for large-scale data transfers (100+ TB) that would take too long or be too expensive over the network (Anti-pattern: ap-3.5-002). AWS DataSync (Option B) is for online data transfer and synchronization, but for 300 TB over a limited internet connection, it would still take a very long time. Increasing internet bandwidth (Option C) can be very expensive and may not be feasible or quick to implement. AWS Storage Gateway File Gateway (Option D) is for continuous synchronization and hybrid cloud storage, not for a one-time bulk transfer of this magnitude with limited network capacity.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  19,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_5',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_5_36',
  'A marketing team collects website clickstream data from millions of users in real-time. This data needs to be continuously ingested, processed, and made available for immediate analytics and dashboard updates. The data is time-ordered, and the team requires a solution that can handle high throughput and low latency for streaming data.',
  'mcq',
  '["Store the clickstream data in Amazon S3 buckets and process it daily using AWS Glue batch jobs.","Use Amazon Kinesis Data Streams to ingest the real-time, time-ordered clickstream data for immediate processing.","Send the clickstream data to an Amazon SQS queue, and have AWS Lambda functions process messages in batches.","Upload the clickstream data to an Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL database for real-time querying and analysis."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Using Amazon Kinesis Data Streams (Option B) is the most appropriate solution. Kinesis Data Streams is designed for real-time, time-ordered streaming data ingestion, capable of handling high throughput and low latency, making it ideal for immediate analytics and dashboard updates (Anti-pattern: ap-3.5-001 is about *not* using Kinesis when batch processing suffices, this question highlights when it *is* appropriate). Storing data in Amazon S3 and processing with AWS Glue batch jobs (Option A) would introduce significant latency, as it''s a batch-oriented approach. Sending data to an Amazon SQS queue (Option C) is suitable for message queuing and decoupling, but SQS does not guarantee message order and is not optimized for time-ordered streaming analytics. Uploading to an Amazon RDS database (Option D) would quickly become a performance bottleneck for millions of real-time transactions per second.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  20,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_5',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_5_37',
  'A data engineering team needs to perform Extract, Transform, and Load (ETL) operations on large datasets stored in Amazon S3. These jobs involve complex data transformations, schema evolution, and require a serverless, auto-scaling environment to minimize operational overhead. The team wants to avoid managing EC2 instances for their ETL workloads.',
  'mcq',
  '["Utilize AWS Glue, a serverless data integration service, to run the ETL jobs and manage the Data Catalog.","Provision a cluster of Amazon EC2 instances and install Apache Spark to run the ETL jobs manually.","Use Amazon EMR to run Spark jobs, managing the EC2 instances that form the EMR cluster.","Develop custom Python scripts and run them on AWS Lambda functions, triggered by S3 events."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Utilizing AWS Glue (Option A) is the best solution. AWS Glue is a fully managed, serverless ETL service that automatically scales to handle large datasets and complex transformations. It integrates with the AWS Glue Data Catalog for schema management and eliminates the need to provision or manage EC2 instances, directly addressing the requirement to minimize operational overhead (Anti-pattern: ap-3.5-003). Provisioning EC2 instances with Apache Spark (Option B) or using Amazon EMR (Option C) both involve managing EC2 instances, which the team wants to avoid. AWS Lambda (Option D) has a 15-minute execution limit, making it unsuitable for large, complex ETL jobs.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  21,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_5',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_5_38',
  'A company has various data sources, including relational databases, operational data stores, and streaming data, which they want to consolidate into a central data lake on Amazon S3. They need a service that can discover schemas, catalog metadata, and prepare the data for analytics using SQL queries, without provisioning or managing servers.',
  'mcq',
  '["Use Amazon Redshift to store and query the data, leveraging its columnar storage for analytical workloads.","Implement AWS Glue Data Catalog for metadata management and Amazon Athena for serverless SQL querying of data in S3.","Deploy Amazon EMR clusters to run Apache Hive for schema discovery and querying on the data lake.","Utilize Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL to store structured data and run SQL queries for analytics."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Implementing AWS Glue Data Catalog for metadata management and Amazon Athena for serverless SQL querying (Option B) is the ideal solution for this scenario. AWS Glue Data Catalog automatically discovers schemas and metadata from various data sources, storing it in a central repository. Amazon Athena then allows running standard SQL queries directly against data stored in Amazon S3, without provisioning or managing any servers. Amazon Redshift (Option A) is a data warehouse that requires provisioning and managing clusters, and while it''s good for analytics, it''s not a serverless data lake solution for S3. Amazon EMR (Option C) requires managing EC2 instances for the cluster. Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL (Option D) is a relational database and not suitable for a data lake architecture on S3 or for handling diverse, large-scale data sources in a serverless manner.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  22,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_5',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_5_39',
  'An enterprise needs to synchronize large files (up to 10 TB each) from an on-premises Network File System (NFS) share to Amazon S3 on a daily basis. The transfer needs to be automated, secure, and resilient to network interruptions, ensuring data integrity. The solutions architect needs a managed service that can handle incremental transfers efficiently.',
  'mcq',
  '["Use AWS DataSync to automate and accelerate the transfer of files from the on-premises NFS share to Amazon S3.","Manually copy files using the AWS CLI or S3 API from the on-premises NFS share to Amazon S3 daily.","Implement AWS Storage Gateway File Gateway on-premises to provide an NFS interface that stores files in S3.","Set up a custom script on an on-premises server to compress and upload files to Amazon S3 using multi-part uploads."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Using AWS DataSync (Option A) is the most appropriate solution. AWS DataSync is a fully managed data transfer service that simplifies, automates, and accelerates moving data between on-premises storage and AWS storage services like Amazon S3. It handles large files, ensures data integrity, is resilient to network interruptions, and efficiently performs incremental transfers. Manually copying files (Option B) is not automated, secure, or resilient for large, daily transfers. AWS Storage Gateway File Gateway (Option C) provides an NFS interface to S3, but its primary use case is to provide on-premises applications with file access to cloud storage, not necessarily for large-scale, automated, one-way synchronization of existing data. Custom scripts (Option D) would require significant development, maintenance, and lack the built-in resilience and optimization of a managed service like DataSync.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  23,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_5',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

