INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_2_47',
  'A rapidly growing e-commerce company experiences unpredictable traffic spikes on its web application, hosted on Amazon EC2 instances. During peak sales events, CPU utilization often exceeds 90%, leading to slow response times and customer dissatisfaction. The operations team needs a solution that automatically adjusts compute capacity to match demand, ensuring consistent performance while minimizing costs during off-peak periods. They want to avoid manual scaling efforts.',
  'mcq',
  '["Manually provision a larger EC2 instance type and scale up before anticipated peak events, then scale down afterward.","Implement Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling with a dynamic scaling policy based on CPU utilization to add or remove instances.","Use AWS Lambda functions to handle all incoming web requests, eliminating the need for EC2 instances.","Configure a fixed number of large Amazon EC2 instances to handle the maximum expected load at all times."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling with dynamic scaling policies (Option 1) is the most appropriate solution. It automatically adjusts the number of EC2 instances based on metrics like CPU utilization, ensuring capacity matches demand. This provides consistent performance during spikes and minimizes costs during off-peak hours by terminating unused instances. Manually provisioning (Option 0) is reactive and prone to human error, failing the automatic adjustment requirement. AWS Lambda (Option 2) is a serverless compute service but would require a significant re-architecture of an existing EC2-based web application, which is not implied by the scenario and might not be suitable for all web application types. Configuring a fixed number of large instances (Option 3) leads to over-provisioning and wasted costs during off-peak periods, representing the anti-pattern [ap-3.2-002] of over-provisioning instead of using Auto Scaling.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  1,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_2_48',
  'A research institution processes large scientific simulations that can run for several hours. These simulations are fault-tolerant, meaning they can be interrupted and resumed without significant loss of progress. The institution needs to run these jobs on AWS while minimizing compute costs as much as possible, without compromising the ability to complete the simulations.',
  'mcq',
  '["Run the simulations on Amazon EC2 On-Demand Instances to ensure uninterrupted processing.","Utilize Amazon EC2 Reserved Instances for the simulation workloads to achieve significant cost savings.","Leverage Amazon EC2 Spot Instances for the fault-tolerant simulation jobs to reduce compute costs.","Use AWS Lambda functions to execute the simulation code, benefiting from serverless cost efficiency."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Leveraging Amazon EC2 Spot Instances (Option 2) is the most cost-effective solution for fault-tolerant, long-running batch jobs like scientific simulations. Spot Instances offer significant savings (up to 90% off On-Demand prices) with the trade-off of potential interruptions, which is acceptable for fault-tolerant workloads, directly addressing the anti-pattern [ap-3.2-003]. Running on On-Demand Instances (Option 0) ensures no interruptions but is significantly more expensive. Reserved Instances (Option 1) provide cost savings for predictable, long-term workloads but offer less flexibility and typically lower savings than Spot Instances for interruptible jobs. AWS Lambda (Option 3) has a maximum execution duration of 15 minutes, making it unsuitable for jobs that run for several hours, representing the anti-pattern [ap-3.2-001].',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  2,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_2_49',
  'A development team is building a new containerized microservices application. They want to deploy their Docker containers on AWS without having to provision, patch, or manage any underlying EC2 instances. The team prioritizes operational simplicity and a serverless experience for their container orchestration.',
  'mcq',
  '["Deploy the containers on Amazon ECS with EC2 launch type and manage the underlying EC2 instances.","Use AWS Batch to run the containerized workloads as scheduled jobs on EC2 instances.","Utilize Amazon Elastic Container Service (Amazon ECS) with AWS Fargate launch type for serverless containers.","Provision a Kubernetes cluster on Amazon EKS and manage the worker nodes manually."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Utilizing Amazon Elastic Container Service (Amazon ECS) with AWS Fargate launch type (Option 2) provides a serverless experience for running containers. With Fargate, the development team does not need to provision, patch, or manage the underlying EC2 instances, aligning perfectly with their goal of operational simplicity and zero infrastructure management, directly addressing the anti-pattern [ap-3.2-004]. Deploying on Amazon ECS with EC2 launch type (Option 0) requires managing the underlying EC2 instances, which is what the team wants to avoid. AWS Batch (Option 1) is for batch computing and typically runs on EC2 instances, not primarily for continuous microservices, and still involves managing compute environments. Provisioning a Kubernetes cluster on Amazon EKS (Option 3) with manual worker node management also involves infrastructure management, which the team wants to avoid.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  3,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_2_50',
  'A company''s web application experiences varying traffic patterns throughout the day, with significant spikes during promotional events. The application''s backend processes are handled by AWS Lambda functions, which sometimes exceed their maximum execution duration during heavy load, causing timeouts and failed requests. The solutions architect needs to ensure long-running background tasks complete reliably.',
  'mcq',
  '["Increase the memory allocated to the AWS Lambda functions to improve their processing speed.","Refactor the long-running tasks to run on Amazon EC2 instances managed by an Auto Scaling group.","Break down the long-running tasks into smaller, independent Lambda functions that execute sequentially.","Migrate the long-running background tasks to run on AWS Fargate for Amazon ECS."]'::json,
  '{3}',
  'Migrating the long-running background tasks to run on AWS Fargate for Amazon ECS (Option 3) is the most appropriate solution. AWS Lambda has a hard 15-minute execution limit, making it unsuitable for tasks that exceed this duration, representing the anti-pattern [ap-3.2-001]. AWS Fargate allows running containers without managing servers, and containers can run for much longer durations, making it ideal for long-running background tasks. Increasing Lambda memory (Option 0) might speed up execution but will not overcome the 15-minute hard limit. Refactoring to EC2 instances (Option 1) is a valid option but introduces server management overhead, which Fargate avoids. Breaking tasks into smaller Lambda functions (Option 2) can work for some workloads but adds complexity and might not be feasible for inherently long, atomic operations.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  4,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_2_51',
  'A company is migrating its batch processing workloads to AWS. These jobs involve processing large datasets that require significant compute resources for several hours. The company wants a fully managed service that can automatically provision and scale compute resources based on job queues, without requiring them to manage any servers.',
  'mcq',
  '["Run the batch jobs on Amazon EC2 instances within an Auto Scaling group, managing the scaling policies manually.","Use AWS Lambda functions to process the batch jobs, benefiting from its serverless nature.","Utilize AWS Batch to efficiently run containerized batch jobs on a fully managed compute environment.","Deploy an Amazon EMR cluster and manually configure it to process the batch jobs."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Utilizing AWS Batch (Option 2) is the most suitable solution. AWS Batch is a fully managed service that enables customers to run batch computing workloads on AWS. It automatically provisions and scales compute resources (EC2 instances, Spot Instances, or Fargate) based on the volume of jobs submitted, eliminating the need for manual server management. Running on Amazon EC2 with Auto Scaling (Option 0) still requires managing the EC2 instances and scaling policies. AWS Lambda (Option 1) has a 15-minute execution limit, making it unsuitable for jobs that run for several hours, representing the anti-pattern [ap-3.2-001]. Deploying an Amazon EMR cluster (Option 3) is for big data processing frameworks like Hadoop and Spark, which is more complex than needed for general batch jobs and still requires some cluster management, even if EMR is managed.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  5,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_2_52',
  'A company''s marketing website, hosted on Amazon EC2 instances, experiences sudden, unpredictable surges in traffic during flash sales. These surges overwhelm the current server capacity, leading to website unavailability. The solutions architect needs to design a highly available and elastic architecture that can automatically scale compute resources to handle these traffic spikes without manual intervention.',
  'mcq',
  '["Manually scale up the EC2 instances to larger types before each flash sale event.","Implement Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling with a target tracking scaling policy based on Application Load Balancer request count per target.","Provision a fixed number of large Amazon EC2 instances to always handle the maximum expected traffic.","Migrate the entire website to AWS Lambda functions to handle all web requests."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Implementing Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling with a target tracking scaling policy based on Application Load Balancer request count per target (Option 1) is the most effective solution. This allows the system to automatically adjust the number of EC2 instances to maintain a target utilization level, ensuring the website can handle unpredictable traffic spikes elastically and without manual intervention. Manually scaling up (Option 0) is reactive and not automatic. Provisioning a fixed number of large instances (Option 2) leads to over-provisioning and wasted costs during off-peak periods, representing the anti-pattern [ap-3.2-002]. Migrating the entire website to AWS Lambda (Option 3) would require a significant re-architecture and might not be suitable for all website types, especially if it''s a traditional server-based application.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  6,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────
-- Module: m_20_4
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_4_45',
  'A media company hosts a popular video streaming platform in a single AWS Region. Users worldwide complain about high latency and buffering, especially those located far from the primary Region. The platform delivers both static content (thumbnails, CSS, JavaScript) and dynamic user-specific video streams. The company wants to improve the global user experience by reducing latency for all content types.',
  'mcq',
  '["Deploy additional application stacks in multiple AWS Regions and use Amazon Route 53 latency-based routing to direct users.","Implement Amazon CloudFront with caching for static assets and configure it to optimize dynamic content delivery to origins.","Use AWS Global Accelerator to route all user traffic to the nearest AWS edge location and then to the application origin.","Migrate all video assets to Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive and serve them directly from S3 buckets."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Amazon CloudFront (Option 1) is designed to deliver both static and dynamic content with low latency by caching at edge locations and optimizing connections to origin servers. It can cache static assets with long TTLs and improve dynamic content delivery through persistent connections, TLS termination, and TCP optimizations at the edge, directly addressing the anti-pattern [ap-3.4-002] by using CloudFront for dynamic content. Deploying multiple application stacks (Option 0) would reduce latency for dynamic content but is a more complex and costly solution than leveraging CloudFront''s capabilities for both content types. AWS Global Accelerator (Option 2) improves routing for TCP/UDP traffic but does not cache content, making CloudFront a more comprehensive solution for content delivery. Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive (Option 3) is for archival storage with retrieval times of hours, completely unsuitable for a video streaming platform.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  7,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_4',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_4_46',
  'A company needs to establish a private, dedicated network connection between its on-premises data center and its AWS VPC. The connection must provide consistent, low-latency, and high-throughput performance for mission-critical applications. Existing VPN connections over the public internet are experiencing inconsistent performance and are not meeting the strict requirements.',
  'mcq',
  '["Continue using AWS Site-to-Site VPN connections and increase the internet bandwidth at the data center.","Implement AWS Direct Connect to establish a dedicated private network connection to AWS.","Utilize Amazon VPC peering to connect the on-premises network to the AWS VPC.","Configure an Internet Gateway in the VPC and route all on-premises traffic through it."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Implementing AWS Direct Connect (Option 1) provides a dedicated private network connection between the on-premises data center and AWS. This ensures consistent, low-latency, and high-throughput performance, which is critical for mission-critical applications and superior to VPN connections over the public internet, directly addressing the anti-pattern [ap-3.4-001]. Continuing to use AWS Site-to-Site VPN (Option 0) still relies on the public internet, which inherently has variable latency and bandwidth, failing to meet the consistent performance requirement. Amazon VPC peering (Option 2) is used to connect two VPCs within AWS, not an on-premises network. Configuring an Internet Gateway (Option 3) is for allowing internet access to resources in a VPC and does not provide a private, dedicated connection to an on-premises network.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  8,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_4',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_4_47',
  'A company''s existing network architecture uses Amazon VPC peering connections to link several VPCs in a hub-and-spoke topology. As the number of VPCs has grown to over 15, managing the peering connections has become complex and difficult to scale, requiring a new connection for every pair of VPCs. The solutions architect needs a more scalable and manageable solution for inter-VPC connectivity.',
  'mcq',
  '["Continue using VPC peering connections and automate their creation with AWS CloudFormation.","Implement AWS Transit Gateway to centralize routing and simplify inter-VPC connectivity.","Create a dedicated Amazon EC2 instance in a central VPC to act as a routing hub for all peered VPCs.","Establish a separate AWS Direct Connect connection for each VPC to ensure dedicated network paths."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Implementing AWS Transit Gateway (Option 1) is the most scalable and manageable solution for a hub-and-spoke network topology with a growing number of VPCs. Transit Gateway acts as a central hub, simplifying inter-VPC connectivity and eliminating the need for O(n^2) peering connections, directly addressing the anti-pattern [ap-3.4-003]. Continuing to use VPC peering (Option 0) becomes unmanageable and non-transitive with many VPCs, even with automation. Creating an EC2 instance as a routing hub (Option 2) introduces a single point of failure and requires manual routing configuration and management. Establishing separate AWS Direct Connect connections (Option 3) is for connecting on-premises networks to AWS, not for inter-VPC connectivity within AWS, and would be extremely costly and complex.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  9,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_4',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_4_48',
  'A company hosts a web application in a single AWS Region. Users in different geographic locations experience varying levels of latency when accessing the application. The application serves both static content (images, CSS) and dynamic content (API calls to backend services). The solutions architect needs to improve the user experience by reducing latency for all users globally.',
  'mcq',
  '["Deploy the application in multiple AWS Regions and use Amazon Route 53 with latency-based routing.","Configure Amazon CloudFront to cache static content and optimize dynamic content delivery to the origin.","Use AWS Global Accelerator to route user traffic to the nearest AWS edge location and then to the application origin.","Implement an Application Load Balancer (ALB) in each Availability Zone within the single Region."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Configuring Amazon CloudFront (Option 1) is a highly effective way to reduce latency for global users by caching static content at edge locations and optimizing dynamic content delivery. CloudFront terminates TLS at the edge and uses optimized connections to the origin, improving performance for both content types. While deploying in multiple Regions with Route 53 (Option 0) would also reduce latency for dynamic content, CloudFront specifically addresses content delivery network capabilities for both static and dynamic content. AWS Global Accelerator (Option 2) routes traffic to the nearest edge location but does not cache content, making CloudFront a more comprehensive solution for content delivery. Implementing ALBs within a single Region (Option 3) improves availability and load distribution within that Region but does not address latency for users geographically distant from that Region.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  10,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_4',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────
-- Module: m_20_1
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_1_39',
  'A data analytics team needs a shared storage solution for their Linux-based Amazon EC2 instances. The instances process large datasets, requiring a file system that supports concurrent access from multiple servers, POSIX file system semantics (like file locking), and automatic scalability. The solution must integrate seamlessly with their existing Linux applications.',
  'mcq',
  '["Store the datasets in Amazon S3 buckets and access them via the S3 API from each EC2 instance.","Attach a single Amazon EBS Provisioned IOPS SSD (io2) volume to one EC2 instance and share it via NFS.","Utilize Amazon Elastic File System (Amazon EFS) to provide a scalable, shared file system across multiple EC2 instances.","Use Amazon FSx for Windows File Server to provide a highly available and scalable file share."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Amazon Elastic File System (Amazon EFS) (Option 2) provides a scalable, shared file system that supports POSIX semantics, allowing multiple EC2 instances to concurrently access and modify data. It automatically scales storage capacity and performance, making it ideal for the described workload. Storing data in Amazon S3 (Option 0) is object storage and does not provide POSIX file system semantics or file locking, making it unsuitable for frequently modified data requiring such features, representing the anti-pattern [ap-3.1-002]. Attaching an EBS volume to one instance and sharing via NFS (Option 1) creates a single point of failure and requires manual management, which is less scalable and highly available than EFS. Amazon FSx for Windows File Server (Option 3) is designed for Windows-based applications and would not integrate seamlessly with Linux-based EC2 instances.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  11,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_1_40',
  'A company is migrating a legacy application that stores critical data on a local file server. This application requires block-level storage with extremely low latency and high IOPS for a single Amazon EC2 instance. The application is highly sensitive to network overhead and needs consistent performance. The solutions architect must choose the most appropriate storage solution.',
  'mcq',
  '["Migrate the data to Amazon S3 and access it using the S3 API.","Use Amazon Elastic File System (Amazon EFS) for shared file access.","Attach an Amazon EBS Provisioned IOPS SSD (io2) volume to the EC2 instance.","Implement AWS Storage Gateway File Gateway to connect the EC2 instance to cloud storage."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Attaching an Amazon EBS Provisioned IOPS SSD (io2) volume (Option 2) provides block-level storage with extremely low latency and high, consistent IOPS, directly attached to a single EC2 instance. This is ideal for latency-sensitive single-instance workloads, as EFS (Option 1) would introduce network overhead for shared file access, representing the anti-pattern [ap-3.1-003]. Amazon S3 (Option 0) is object storage, not block storage, and is not suitable for this use case. AWS Storage Gateway File Gateway (Option 3) is for hybrid cloud storage and would also introduce network latency, making it unsuitable for extremely low-latency requirements.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  12,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_1_41',
  'A company is developing a new application that requires a highly available, durable, and scalable object storage solution for storing user-generated content, such as photos and videos. This content will be accessed frequently by users globally. The solutions architect needs to choose a storage service that meets these requirements and offers robust security features.',
  'mcq',
  '["Provision an Amazon EBS General Purpose SSD (gp3) volume and attach it to an EC2 instance.","Use Amazon Elastic File System (Amazon EFS) for shared file storage across multiple instances.","Store the content in Amazon S3, leveraging its high durability, availability, and scalability for objects.","Set up an Amazon FSx for Lustre file system for high-performance computing workloads."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Amazon S3 (Option 2) is the ideal service for storing user-generated content like photos and videos. It offers extremely high durability and availability, scales automatically to petabytes of data, and provides robust security features, making it perfect for frequently accessed objects. Amazon EBS (Option 0) provides block storage for single EC2 instances and is not suitable for a highly scalable, globally accessible object store. Amazon EFS (Option 1) provides shared file system access for multiple EC2 instances but is not designed for global object storage. Amazon FSx for Lustre (Option 3) is a high-performance file system for HPC workloads, which is overkill and not suitable for general user-generated content storage.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  13,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_1_42',
  'A company''s legacy application runs on a single Amazon EC2 instance and processes large files. The application needs high-performance block storage with a consistent baseline performance of at least 3,000 IOPS and 125 MiB/s throughput, regardless of burst credits. The current Amazon EBS General Purpose SSD (gp2) volume is experiencing performance degradation under sustained load.',
  'mcq',
  '["Increase the size of the existing Amazon EBS gp2 volume to gain more burst credits and higher baseline performance.","Migrate the data to Amazon S3 and refactor the application to use object storage APIs.","Replace the Amazon EBS gp2 volume with an Amazon EBS General Purpose SSD (gp3) volume.","Provision an Amazon EBS Throughput Optimized HDD (st1) volume for higher throughput."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Replacing the Amazon EBS gp2 volume with an Amazon EBS General Purpose SSD (gp3) volume (Option 2) is the correct solution. gp3 volumes provide a consistent baseline performance of 3,000 IOPS and 125 MiB/s throughput, which can be provisioned independently of storage size, without relying on burst credits. This directly addresses the anti-pattern [ap-3.1-001] of using gp2 when throughput demands exceed burst credits. Increasing the size of the gp2 volume (Option 0) would increase burst credits and baseline performance, but gp3 offers a better cost-performance ratio and guaranteed baseline without burst credit dependency. Migrating to Amazon S3 (Option 1) changes the storage type from block to object, requiring significant application refactoring. Provisioning an Amazon EBS Throughput Optimized HDD (st1) volume (Option 3) is designed for throughput-intensive, frequently accessed workloads like log processing, but it is HDD-based and not suitable for the high IOPS and low-latency requirements of an application experiencing performance degradation on SSD.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  14,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_1_43',
  'A company needs to store large video files that are frequently accessed during the first 30 days after upload, but then become infrequently accessed for the next 90 days. After 120 days, the videos are rarely accessed but must be available for retrieval within 5-12 hours. The solutions architect needs to design a cost-effective storage solution.',
  'mcq',
  '["Store all video files in Amazon S3 Standard for consistent performance and availability.","Implement an S3 Lifecycle policy to transition objects to S3 Standard-IA after 30 days, then to S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval after 120 days.","Use Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering to automatically move objects between access tiers based on access patterns.","Transition objects to Amazon S3 One Zone-IA after 30 days, and then to Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive after 120 days."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Implementing an S3 Lifecycle policy to transition objects to S3 Standard-IA after 30 days, and then to S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval after 120 days (Option 1) is the most cost-effective solution that meets the access requirements. S3 Standard-IA is for infrequently accessed data with millisecond retrieval. S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval offers lower costs for archival data with retrieval options typically ranging from minutes to hours (standard retrieval is 3-5 hours), meeting the 5-12 hour requirement. Storing all data in S3 Standard (Option 0) would be more expensive for infrequently accessed and archival data. S3 Intelligent-Tiering (Option 2) is a valid option but incurs per-object monitoring fees, which might be less cost-effective than a well-defined lifecycle policy for predictable access patterns. Transitioning to S3 One Zone-IA (Option 3) risks data loss in an Availability Zone failure, which is generally not suitable for critical video files, and S3 Glacier Deep Archive has a minimum standard retrieval time of 12 hours, which might not reliably meet the ''within 5-12 hours'' requirement.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  15,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────
-- Module: m_20_3
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_3_36',
  'A social media application uses Amazon Aurora PostgreSQL as its backend database. During peak usage, the application experiences slow query performance due to a high volume of read requests, primarily from users browsing feeds and profiles. The development team wants to improve read throughput and reduce latency without impacting the primary database''s write performance or requiring significant application code changes.',
  'mcq',
  '["Increase the instance size of the primary Amazon Aurora PostgreSQL database instance to handle more connections.","Implement Amazon ElastiCache for Redis to cache frequently accessed data, reducing direct database reads.","Configure Amazon Aurora Read Replicas and distribute read traffic across them.","Migrate the database to Amazon DynamoDB to leverage its high read/write throughput capabilities."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Configuring Amazon Aurora Read Replicas (Option 2) is the most effective solution for offloading read traffic and improving read throughput without impacting the primary database''s write performance. Aurora Read Replicas are designed for this exact purpose, allowing applications to distribute read queries across multiple instances. Increasing the primary instance size (Option 0) might provide some relief but does not scale reads horizontally as effectively as read replicas and can be more costly. Implementing Amazon ElastiCache (Option 1) is a valid caching strategy for repeated queries but requires application code changes to integrate caching logic, which the scenario aims to minimize. Migrating to Amazon DynamoDB (Option 3) would require a complete re-architecture of the application and database schema, as DynamoDB is a NoSQL database, making it unsuitable for minimizing code changes and complex relational data, representing the anti-pattern [ap-3.3-002].',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  16,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_3_37',
  'A small startup is developing a new serverless application using AWS Lambda functions. These functions frequently interact with an Amazon RDS for MySQL database. The development team observes occasional connection errors and performance issues under heavy load, as each Lambda invocation establishes a new database connection, potentially exhausting the database''s connection limits. They need a solution to efficiently manage database connections.',
  'mcq',
  '["Increase the maximum connections allowed on the Amazon RDS for MySQL database instance.","Implement Amazon RDS Proxy to pool and share database connections for AWS Lambda functions.","Refactor the Lambda functions to use a single, long-lived database connection for all invocations.","Migrate the application''s data to Amazon DynamoDB to eliminate the need for traditional database connections."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Amazon RDS Proxy (Option 1) is specifically designed to pool and share database connections, making it ideal for managing connections from AWS Lambda functions to Amazon RDS databases. This prevents connection exhaustion and improves application resilience and performance, directly addressing the anti-pattern [ap-3.3-004] of ignoring RDS Proxy for Lambda-to-RDS connections. Increasing maximum connections (Option 0) might temporarily alleviate the issue but does not solve the underlying problem of connection storms from Lambda and can consume more database resources. Refactoring Lambda functions (Option 2) to use long-lived connections is not a standard or efficient pattern for serverless functions, which are stateless and short-lived. Migrating to Amazon DynamoDB (Option 3) would require a complete re-architecture of the application and database, which is a much larger undertaking than solving the connection management issue.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  17,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_3_38',
  'A company''s marketing department frequently generates reports from their Amazon RDS for MySQL database. Many of these reports run the same complex queries repeatedly throughout the day, leading to increased database load and slower report generation times. The solutions architect needs to improve the performance of these read-heavy, repetitive queries without significantly altering the database schema or application code.',
  'mcq',
  '["Increase the provisioned IOPS of the Amazon RDS for MySQL database instance.","Implement Amazon ElastiCache for Memcached to cache the results of frequently run queries.","Create multiple Amazon RDS Read Replicas and distribute the reporting queries across them.","Migrate the reporting data to Amazon Redshift for optimized analytical querying."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Implementing Amazon ElastiCache for Memcached (Option 1) is an effective solution for caching the results of frequently run, repetitive queries. This reduces the load on the Amazon RDS database and improves report generation times by serving cached data, directly addressing the anti-pattern [ap-3.3-003]. While increasing provisioned IOPS (Option 0) might offer some performance improvement, it doesn''t address the inefficiency of re-running identical queries. Creating Amazon RDS Read Replicas (Option 2) helps distribute read load but doesn''t prevent the database from processing the same query multiple times if the results are not cached. Migrating to Amazon Redshift (Option 3) is a data warehousing solution for large-scale analytics, which is a much larger undertaking than simply caching repetitive queries and might not be suitable for real-time reporting from an operational database.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  18,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_3_39',
  'A company is migrating a critical enterprise application to AWS. The application uses a relational database that requires high availability with automatic failover, and the ability to scale read operations across multiple instances. The solutions architect needs to choose a database service that provides these features with minimal administrative overhead.',
  'mcq',
  '["Deploy a self-managed MySQL database on Amazon EC2 instances with a custom failover mechanism.","Use Amazon DynamoDB for its high scalability and availability, refactoring the application for NoSQL.","Provision an Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL database with Multi-AZ deployment and configure read replicas.","Set up an Amazon Redshift cluster for the relational database workload."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Provisioning an Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL database with Multi-AZ deployment and configuring read replicas (Option 2) provides high availability with automatic failover and the ability to scale read operations. RDS manages the underlying infrastructure, minimizing administrative overhead. Deploying a self-managed MySQL database on EC2 (Option 0) requires significant administrative effort for high availability and failover, contradicting the minimal overhead requirement. Amazon DynamoDB (Option 1) is a NoSQL database and would require significant application refactoring for a relational workload, representing the anti-pattern [ap-3.3-002]. Amazon Redshift (Option 3) is a data warehousing service, not an operational relational database for an enterprise application.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  19,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_3_40',
  'A global company operates a mission-critical application that uses Amazon Aurora MySQL. The application experiences very high read traffic during peak hours, leading to performance bottlenecks. The solutions architect needs to implement a solution that can significantly scale read capacity to handle millions of requests per second, without impacting the primary database''s write performance.',
  'mcq',
  '["Configure Amazon Aurora Read Replicas and distribute read traffic across them.","Migrate the database to Amazon DynamoDB Global Tables for massive read/write scalability.","Increase the instance size of the primary Amazon Aurora MySQL database instance.","Implement Amazon ElastiCache for Redis to cache frequently accessed data."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Configuring Amazon Aurora Read Replicas (Option 0) is the most effective solution for scaling read capacity for Amazon Aurora MySQL. Aurora Read Replicas share the same underlying storage volume as the primary instance, allowing for rapid provisioning and up to 15 read replicas, which can handle millions of read requests per second without impacting the primary''s write performance. Migrating to Amazon DynamoDB Global Tables (Option 1) would require a complete re-architecture of the application and database schema, as DynamoDB is a NoSQL database, which is not suitable for a relational database with existing complex queries, representing the anti-pattern [ap-3.3-002]. Increasing the primary instance size (Option 2) only scales vertically and does not provide the horizontal read scaling needed for millions of requests per second. Implementing Amazon ElastiCache (Option 3) is a good caching strategy but requires application code changes and is complementary to, not a replacement for, read replicas for general read scaling.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  20,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────
-- Module: m_20_5
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_5_40',
  'A manufacturing company collects operational data from factory sensors, generating terabytes of non-time-ordered log files daily. These files are temporarily stored and then need to be processed, transformed, and loaded into a data lake for analytical purposes. The company requires a serverless, cost-effective solution that minimizes operational overhead for data transformation.',
  'mcq',
  '["Use Amazon Kinesis Data Streams to ingest the log files in real-time and process them with AWS Lambda.","Provision a cluster of Amazon EC2 instances and install Apache Spark for custom ETL jobs.","Utilize AWS Glue to catalog the data, perform serverless ETL transformations, and load it into Amazon S3.","Manually upload the log files to Amazon S3 and use Amazon Athena for direct querying without transformation."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'AWS Glue (Option 2) is a serverless data integration service that is ideal for ETL (Extract, Transform, Load) operations. It can automatically discover schema, catalog data in the AWS Glue Data Catalog, and run serverless Spark-based ETL jobs, minimizing operational overhead and being cost-effective. Amazon Kinesis Data Streams (Option 0) is designed for real-time, time-ordered streaming data, which is not the primary requirement for non-time-ordered log files processed in batches, representing the anti-pattern [ap-3.5-001]. Provisioning EC2 instances for Apache Spark (Option 1) requires significant operational overhead for infrastructure management, patching, and scaling, which contradicts the serverless and minimal overhead requirements, representing the anti-pattern [ap-3.5-003]. Using Amazon Athena (Option 3) for direct querying without transformation might not meet the requirement for processing and transforming data before loading into a data lake for analytical purposes.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  21,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_5',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_5_41',
  'A company operates a data center with several petabytes of historical archival data on tape. They need to migrate this data to AWS for long-term retention and occasional retrieval within 24 hours. Transferring this volume of data over the internet is impractical due to bandwidth limitations and time constraints. The solutions architect needs a secure and efficient method for this one-time bulk transfer.',
  'mcq',
  '["Use AWS DataSync to transfer the data over the internet to Amazon S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval.","Ship the tape drives directly to an AWS data center for manual ingestion.","Utilize AWS Snowball Edge Storage Optimized devices to physically transfer the data to AWS.","Establish an AWS Direct Connect connection and transfer the data over the dedicated network link."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Utilizing AWS Snowball Edge Storage Optimized devices (Option 2) is the most appropriate solution for transferring petabytes of historical archival data. Snowball devices are physical appliances that AWS ships to the customer, allowing for offline data transfer that is significantly faster and more cost-effective than transferring large datasets over the network, directly addressing the anti-pattern [ap-3.5-002]. AWS DataSync (Option 0) is suitable for online transfers but would be impractical for petabytes over typical internet connections. Shipping tape drives (Option 1) is not an AWS-supported service for data ingestion. AWS Direct Connect (Option 3) provides dedicated network connectivity but would still take a very long time and be costly for petabytes of data, making Snowball a better choice for a one-time bulk transfer of this magnitude.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  22,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_5',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_5_42',
  'A data engineering team needs to build a data lake on AWS. They have various data sources, including relational databases, application logs, and streaming data. The team requires a solution to centralize, secure, and govern access to this diverse data, enabling different personas (analysts, data scientists) to access specific datasets without compromising security or data integrity.',
  'mcq',
  '["Store all data in Amazon S3 and manage access using S3 bucket policies and IAM roles.","Use AWS Lake Formation to build a secure data lake, centralizing data governance and access control.","Ingest all data into Amazon Redshift for centralized storage and querying.","Implement Amazon DynamoDB Global Tables to store and replicate data across multiple Regions."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'AWS Lake Formation (Option 1) is specifically designed to build, secure, and manage data lakes on AWS. It provides a centralized console to define security, governance, and auditing policies for data stored in Amazon S3 and integrated with various analytics services, allowing fine-grained access control for different personas. Storing data in Amazon S3 with bucket policies (Option 0) provides basic security but lacks the centralized governance, fine-grained access control, and integration with other analytics services that Lake Formation offers. Ingesting all data into Amazon Redshift (Option 2) is for data warehousing and analytics, not for building a diverse data lake with raw and semi-structured data. Amazon DynamoDB Global Tables (Option 3) is a NoSQL database service for high-performance applications, not a data lake solution for diverse data types and governance.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  23,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_5',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_5_43',
  'A company needs to transfer petabytes of sensor data from an on-premises data center to Amazon S3 for long-term archival and analysis. The data center has limited internet bandwidth, making direct online transfer impractical within the required timeframe. The solutions architect must choose a secure and efficient method for this initial large-scale data migration.',
  'mcq',
  '["Use AWS DataSync to establish an encrypted online transfer over the existing internet connection.","Provision a dedicated AWS Direct Connect connection to increase network bandwidth for the transfer.","Utilize AWS Snowball Edge Storage Optimized devices to physically transport the data to AWS.","Compress the data significantly and then upload it to Amazon S3 using multi-part uploads."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Utilizing AWS Snowball Edge Storage Optimized devices (Option 2) is the most efficient and secure method for transferring petabytes of data from on-premises to AWS when internet bandwidth is limited. Snowball devices are physical appliances that enable offline data transfer, which is significantly faster and more cost-effective for large datasets than network transfers, directly addressing the anti-pattern [ap-3.5-002]. AWS DataSync (Option 0) and AWS Direct Connect (Option 1) are suitable for online transfers, but they would still be impractical or too slow for petabytes of data with limited bandwidth. Compressing and using multi-part uploads (Option 3) helps optimize online transfers but does not overcome the fundamental limitation of insufficient internet bandwidth for petabyte-scale data within a reasonable timeframe.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  24,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_5',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_20_m_20_5_44',
  'A company is building a new application that processes real-time sensor data from thousands of devices. The data arrives continuously, is time-ordered, and needs to be processed immediately for anomaly detection. The solutions architect needs a service that can ingest and process high-throughput streaming data efficiently.',
  'mcq',
  '["Use Amazon SQS to queue the sensor data for batch processing by AWS Lambda functions.","Store the sensor data in Amazon S3 buckets and process it periodically with AWS Glue.","Utilize Amazon Kinesis Data Streams to ingest and process the real-time, time-ordered sensor data.","Ingest the data directly into an Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL database for immediate analysis."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Utilizing Amazon Kinesis Data Streams (Option 2) is the most appropriate service for ingesting and processing high-throughput, real-time, time-ordered streaming data. Kinesis Data Streams is designed for continuous data ingestion and allows for immediate processing by downstream consumers. Amazon SQS (Option 0) is a message queuing service suitable for decoupled components and batch processing, not optimized for real-time, time-ordered streaming, representing the anti-pattern [ap-3.5-001]. Storing data in Amazon S3 (Option 1) and processing with AWS Glue is suitable for batch processing of large datasets, not real-time streaming. Ingesting directly into an Amazon RDS database (Option 3) would likely overwhelm the database with high-throughput streaming data and is not designed for this scale of ingestion.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain20_4',
  NOW(),
  25,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_20_5',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

