INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_21_m_21_2_26',
  'A media company processes large volumes of video files for transcoding. These tasks are fault-tolerant, can be interrupted, and do not require immediate completion. The company wants to minimize compute costs for this batch processing workload, which can run at any time during the day. They need a solution that automatically scales based on the processing queue and is highly cost-effective.',
  'mcq',
  '["Launch Amazon EC2 Spot Instances within an Auto Scaling group, configured to process items from an Amazon SQS queue.","Provision Amazon EC2 Reserved Instances for a 3-year term to cover the expected compute capacity for the batch processing.","Utilize AWS Lambda functions to process video files, orchestrating the workflow with AWS Step Functions for state management.","Deploy the video processing application on AWS Fargate using a scheduled task, scaling the number of tasks based on a fixed schedule."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A is the most cost-effective solution for fault-tolerant, interruptible workloads. Amazon EC2 Spot Instances allow the company to bid on unused EC2 capacity, offering significant discounts compared to On-Demand prices. Integrating them with an Auto Scaling group and an Amazon SQS queue ensures automatic scaling and resilience to interruptions, as new instances can pick up tasks from the queue. Option B (Reserved Instances) is unsuitable for variable or interruptible workloads and would result in paying for unused capacity, representing anti-pattern ap-4.2-001. Option C (AWS Lambda) might be suitable for smaller, short-duration tasks, but large video transcoding can exceed Lambda''s execution limits (15 minutes) and memory constraints, making it less ideal for this specific heavy batch processing. Option D (AWS Fargate with a scheduled task) provides serverless containers but a scheduled task wouldn''t dynamically scale based on queue depth, and while Fargate can be cost-effective, Spot Instances are generally cheaper for interruptible batch jobs.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain21_4',
  NOW(),
  1,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_21_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_21_m_21_2_27',
  'A startup is developing a new microservice that performs image resizing upon user upload. The service is expected to have highly variable and unpredictable traffic patterns, with periods of intense activity followed by long idle times. The team needs a solution that requires minimal operational overhead for managing servers and scales automatically to handle spikes, while also being cost-efficient by only paying for actual usage.',
  'mcq',
  '["Deploy the microservice on an Amazon EC2 instance within an Auto Scaling group, scaling based on CPU utilization metrics.","Implement the image resizing logic as an AWS Lambda function triggered by Amazon S3 object creation events.","Run the microservice as a container on AWS Fargate, configuring a target tracking scaling policy for CPU utilization.","Utilize Amazon EC2 Reserved Instances for a 1-year term to ensure compute capacity is always available at a reduced rate."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is the most suitable choice. AWS Lambda is a serverless compute service that automatically scales and charges only for the compute time consumed, making it highly cost-effective for unpredictable, intermittent workloads. Triggering it directly from Amazon S3 object creation events provides an event-driven architecture with minimal operational overhead. Option A (Amazon EC2 with Auto Scaling) would incur costs even during idle periods if instances are running, and requires managing EC2 instances. Option C (AWS Fargate) is serverless containers but still incurs costs for running tasks, even if scaled down, and might be overkill for simple image resizing that fits within Lambda''s limits. Option D (Reserved Instances) is designed for steady-state, predictable workloads and would be highly inefficient for variable usage, aligning with anti-pattern ap-4.2-001.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain21_4',
  NOW(),
  2,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_21_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_21_m_21_2_28',
  'A development team is building a new containerized web application using a Linux-based operating system. They are deploying it on Amazon EC2 instances and are looking for the MOST cost-effective compute solution that also offers superior performance. The application''s workload is general-purpose and compatible with different processor architectures. The team wants to optimize price/performance.',
  'mcq',
  '["Select the latest generation x86-based Amazon EC2 instances (e.g., m6i) to ensure broad compatibility and performance.","Choose Amazon EC2 Spot Instances of any architecture to minimize costs, prioritizing availability over specific instance types.","Deploy the application on AWS Fargate to eliminate server management overhead, leveraging its serverless container capabilities.","Utilize Amazon EC2 Graviton (ARM-based) instances (e.g., m6g) for the containerized application."]'::json,
  '{3}',
  'Option D, utilizing Amazon EC2 Graviton (ARM-based) instances, is the most effective choice for optimizing price/performance for compatible Linux-based workloads. Graviton instances provide up to 40% better price/performance over comparable x86 instances (anti-pattern ap-4.2-004) and are well-suited for containerized applications with minimal or no code changes. Option A (x86-based instances) are widely compatible but generally offer a lower price/performance ratio compared to Graviton for compatible workloads. Option B (Spot Instances) are cost-effective but primarily for fault-tolerant, interruptible workloads, and don''t specifically address the price/performance aspect for a general-purpose application. Option C (AWS Fargate) is a serverless container option that reduces operational overhead but might not offer the same level of price/performance optimization as Graviton EC2 instances for a consistently running application where the team wants to manage the underlying compute architecture.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain21_4',
  NOW(),
  3,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_21_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_21_m_21_2_29',
  'A company maintains several development and testing environments on Amazon EC2 instances. These environments are only actively used by developers during standard business hours (9 AM to 5 PM, Monday to Friday) and remain idle outside of these times. The company wants to significantly reduce its compute costs for these non-production environments. Which strategy offers the MOST cost-effective solution?',
  'mcq',
  '["Implement a scheduled automation using AWS Lambda and Amazon EventBridge to stop instances at 5 PM and start them at 9 AM on weekdays.","Purchase 1-year Amazon EC2 Reserved Instances for all development and testing environments to secure a discounted rate.","Convert all development and testing environments to use Amazon EC2 Spot Instances to take advantage of lower pricing.","Downsize all Amazon EC2 instances in development and testing environments to the smallest available instance type (e.g., t3.nano) and run them 24/7."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A is the most cost-effective solution for development environments used only during business hours. By stopping instances when not in use, the company avoids paying for compute capacity during idle periods (evenings, weekends), directly addressing anti-pattern ap-4.2-002. AWS Lambda and Amazon EventBridge can automate this process with minimal operational overhead. Option B (Reserved Instances) would still incur costs 24/7, making it inefficient for environments that are idle for most of the time. Option C (Spot Instances) are suitable for fault-tolerant workloads but are not ideal for development environments that require continuous availability during working hours and might be interrupted. Option D (Downsizing to t3.nano) would reduce costs compared to larger instances but still results in paying for 24/7 compute, which is unnecessary for idle periods.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain21_4',
  NOW(),
  4,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_21_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────
-- Module: m_21_3
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_21_m_21_3_26',
  'An analytics startup is building a new internal dashboard application. The database for this application will experience highly variable and unpredictable usage patterns throughout the day, with peak loads during business hours and minimal activity overnight. The team needs a relational database solution that automatically scales capacity up and down based on demand, and they want to pay only for the database resources consumed, avoiding over-provisioning. Which Amazon Aurora deployment model is MOST cost-effective for this scenario?',
  'mcq',
  '["Deploy Amazon Aurora with provisioned capacity, manually scaling the instance size based on observed peak usage.","Utilize Amazon Aurora Serverless v2, which automatically scales compute capacity in fine-grained increments.","Implement Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL on an Amazon EC2 instance, managing scaling manually.","Configure Amazon Aurora with a Multi-AZ deployment to ensure high availability, running 24/7."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B, utilizing Amazon Aurora Serverless v2, is the most cost-effective solution for highly variable and unpredictable database workloads. Aurora Serverless v2 scales compute capacity in fine-grained increments, scaling down to minimal ACUs (Aurora Capacity Units) during idle periods and eliminating the need to over-provision capacity, directly addressing anti-pattern ap-4.3-003. This ''pay-per-use'' model is ideal for intermittent applications. Option A (provisioned Aurora) would require manual scaling and would incur costs for provisioned capacity even during idle times. Option C (RDS on EC2) introduces significant operational overhead for database management and scaling. Option D (Multi-AZ Aurora) focuses on high availability and would incur costs for a standby instance 24/7, which is not the primary concern for cost optimization of variable usage in this scenario.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain21_4',
  NOW(),
  5,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_21_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_21_m_21_3_27',
  'A mobile gaming company is launching a new game that stores player data, scores, and in-game purchases. The application is expected to have unpredictable traffic patterns, with potential for rapid spikes in usage during promotional events or viral growth. The development team wants a fully managed NoSQL database that can handle millions of requests per second, scale seamlessly without manual intervention, and optimize costs by paying only for actual reads and writes, rather than pre-provisioned capacity.',
  'mcq',
  '["Configure an Amazon RDS for MySQL database with read replicas to handle increased read traffic.","Deploy Amazon Aurora with provisioned capacity, using Auto Scaling to adjust read replicas based on demand.","Use Amazon DynamoDB in On-Demand capacity mode for the player data.","Implement Amazon ElastiCache for Redis to store player data, relying on its in-memory performance."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C, using Amazon DynamoDB in On-Demand capacity mode, is the most suitable and cost-effective solution for unpredictable traffic patterns. On-Demand mode automatically scales read and write capacity to accommodate sudden traffic spikes and charges only for the requests made, eliminating the need for capacity planning and preventing over-provisioning or throttling (anti-pattern ap-4.3-002). This aligns perfectly with the requirement for a fully managed, seamlessly scaling NoSQL database that optimizes costs for variable usage. Option A (RDS for MySQL) and Option B (Amazon Aurora with provisioned capacity) are relational databases and would require more active management for scaling, and provisioned capacity is less cost-effective for unpredictable workloads. Option D (Amazon ElastiCache) is a caching service, not a primary persistent database, and would not meet the requirement for storing all player data.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain21_4',
  NOW(),
  6,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_21_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_21_m_21_3_28',
  'An e-commerce company runs its main product catalog on an Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL database. The application experiences heavy read traffic, especially during peak shopping seasons, as customers browse products. The write traffic (e.g., inventory updates) is significantly lower. The company wants to improve the performance of read-heavy queries and offload the read workload from the primary database instance to reduce costs and maintain responsiveness during high demand. Which Amazon RDS feature should be implemented?',
  'mcq',
  '["Enable Multi-AZ deployment for the Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL instance to improve read performance and availability.","Increase the instance size of the primary Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL database to handle the increased read load.","Migrate the product catalog to Amazon DynamoDB to leverage its high-throughput read capabilities.","Create Amazon RDS Read Replicas for the PostgreSQL database and direct read traffic to them."]'::json,
  '{3}',
  'Option D, creating Amazon RDS Read Replicas, is the most effective solution for offloading read-heavy workloads and improving performance while optimizing costs. Read Replicas allow you to scale out your database''s read capacity by creating one or more copies of your primary database, which can then serve read traffic. This reduces the load on the primary instance and can be more cost-effective than vertically scaling the primary. Option A (Multi-AZ deployment) is for high availability and disaster recovery, not for scaling read performance. Option B (increasing instance size) is vertical scaling, which has limits and can be more expensive than horizontal scaling with read replicas. Option C (migrating to DynamoDB) would be a significant architectural change and is not the most direct or cost-effective solution for scaling reads on an existing relational database.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain21_4',
  NOW(),
  7,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_21_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_21_m_21_3_29',
  'A web application frequently queries a relational database for user profile information, which changes infrequently. These queries contribute significantly to the database''s load and operational costs. The company wants to reduce the load on the Amazon RDS database and improve application response times for these common requests, thereby optimizing database costs. Which service should the company implement to achieve this goal?',
  'mcq',
  '["Implement Amazon ElastiCache for Redis to cache frequently accessed user profile data.","Migrate the user profile data to Amazon DynamoDB for faster access.","Increase the provisioned IOPS of the Amazon RDS database to handle more queries.","Configure Amazon RDS Proxy to manage database connections more efficiently."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A, implementing Amazon ElastiCache for Redis, is the most effective solution. Caching frequently accessed, infrequently changing data in an in-memory store like ElastiCache significantly reduces the number of queries hitting the primary Amazon RDS database. This decreases database load, improves application response times, and directly contributes to cost optimization by potentially allowing for smaller database instances or reducing I/O operations. Option B (DynamoDB migration) is a database migration, not a caching strategy, and might be overkill for simply reducing read load. Option C (increasing provisioned IOPS) would improve performance but also increase costs and doesn''t address the root cause of excessive queries. Option D (Amazon RDS Proxy) helps manage database connections and connection pooling, which can improve efficiency but doesn''t reduce the actual query load on the database itself.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain21_4',
  NOW(),
  8,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_21_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────
-- Module: m_21_4
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_21_m_21_4_26',
  'A company has several Amazon EC2 instances running in private subnets within a Virtual Private Cloud (VPC). These instances frequently need to retrieve and store large objects in Amazon S3 buckets. To enhance security and reduce data transfer costs, the company wants to ensure that all traffic between the EC2 instances and Amazon S3 remains within the AWS network and does not traverse the public internet or incur NAT Gateway charges. Which networking solution should be implemented?',
  'mcq',
  '["Configure a NAT Gateway in a public subnet to allow instances in private subnets to access Amazon S3.","Establish a VPC Peering connection between the VPC and a dedicated S3 VPC to route traffic privately.","Create an Amazon S3 Gateway VPC Endpoint in the VPC.","Route all S3 traffic through an AWS Transit Gateway connected to the VPC."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C, creating an Amazon S3 Gateway VPC Endpoint, is the most cost-effective and secure solution. Gateway VPC endpoints for Amazon S3 are free of charge, keep traffic within the AWS network, and do not incur NAT Gateway processing charges (anti-pattern ap-4.4-001). This meets the requirement to access S3 privately and cost-effectively from private subnets. Option A (NAT Gateway) would allow access but incurs hourly charges and data processing charges, which the company wants to avoid. Option B (VPC Peering to a dedicated S3 VPC) is not a standard or necessary pattern for S3 access and S3 is not deployed in a customer VPC. Option D (AWS Transit Gateway) is used for connecting multiple VPCs and on-premises networks, not for direct private access to Amazon S3 within a single VPC.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain21_4',
  NOW(),
  9,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_21_4',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_21_m_21_4_27',
  'A company operates two distinct applications, each residing in its own Amazon VPC within the same AWS Region. These applications frequently need to communicate with each other to exchange data. The company requires a secure, private, and cost-effective network connection between these two VPCs without routing traffic over the public internet. Which networking feature should be used to establish this connection?',
  'mcq',
  '["Configure a Virtual Private Network (VPN) connection between the two VPCs using AWS Site-to-Site VPN.","Set up an AWS Direct Connect connection to link the two VPCs privately.","Utilize an AWS Transit Gateway to connect both VPCs and manage routing.","Establish a VPC Peering connection between the two VPCs."]'::json,
  '{3}',
  'Option D, establishing a VPC Peering connection, is the most appropriate and cost-effective solution for connecting two VPCs within the same AWS Region. VPC peering allows private IP address communication between instances in different VPCs as if they were in the same network, without using the public internet. It''s simpler and generally more cost-effective for a direct, one-to-one VPC connection compared to other options. Option A (Site-to-Site VPN) is typically used for connecting VPCs to on-premises networks, not directly between VPCs in the same region, and incurs VPN connection costs. Option B (AWS Direct Connect) is for dedicated private connections from on-premises to AWS, which is not applicable here. Option C (AWS Transit Gateway) is designed for connecting a large number of VPCs and on-premises networks in a hub-and-spoke model, which would be overkill and more expensive for just two VPCs, though it is a valid option for more complex scenarios.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain21_4',
  NOW(),
  10,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_21_4',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_21_m_21_4_28',
  'A global company has users located in various geographical regions who frequently upload large files (several gigabytes each) to a central Amazon S3 bucket in a single AWS Region. Users in distant locations experience slow upload speeds due to network latency and geographical distance. The company wants to improve the upload performance for these geographically dispersed users while leveraging the AWS global network. Which Amazon S3 feature should be enabled?',
  'mcq',
  '["Enable Amazon S3 Transfer Acceleration on the target S3 bucket.","Configure Amazon CloudFront with S3 as an origin to cache uploads.","Use Amazon S3 Cross-Region Replication to copy data to a closer Region.","Implement a custom upload application running on Amazon EC2 instances in multiple Regions."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A, enabling Amazon S3 Transfer Acceleration, is the correct solution. S3 Transfer Acceleration utilizes Amazon CloudFront''s globally distributed edge locations to route uploads over the optimized AWS backbone network, significantly improving transfer speeds for clients geographically distant from the S3 bucket (anti-pattern ap-4.4-003). Option B (CloudFront for caching uploads) is not its primary use case for uploads; CloudFront is primarily for content delivery (downloads). Option C (Cross-Region Replication) is for data redundancy and disaster recovery, not for accelerating initial uploads. Option D (custom EC2 application) would involve significant operational overhead and development effort, and might not match the global optimization of Transfer Acceleration.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain21_4',
  NOW(),
  11,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_21_4',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_21_m_21_4_29',
  'A financial services company needs to securely connect its applications running in a private subnet within its Amazon VPC to a third-party SaaS application hosted in another AWS account. The company has strict security and compliance requirements, demanding that all traffic remains private and does not traverse the public internet. They also want to minimize the operational complexity of managing network infrastructure. Which AWS networking service should be used to establish this private connection?',
  'mcq',
  '["Utilize AWS PrivateLink to create a private endpoint for the SaaS application within their VPC.","Configure a VPC Peering connection between their VPC and the SaaS provider''s VPC.","Set up an AWS Site-to-Site VPN connection to the SaaS provider''s network.","Route traffic to the SaaS application through a NAT Gateway in a public subnet."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A, utilizing AWS PrivateLink, is the most appropriate solution. AWS PrivateLink allows you to establish private connectivity between VPCs, AWS services, and on-premises applications without exposing your data to the public internet. It creates a private endpoint in your VPC, making the SaaS application appear as if it''s hosted within your own VPC, meeting strict security and compliance requirements with minimal operational overhead. Option B (VPC Peering) requires the SaaS provider to peer with every customer VPC, which is not scalable or secure for a multi-tenant SaaS. Option C (Site-to-Site VPN) is typically for connecting to on-premises networks and is less efficient for direct VPC-to-VPC service consumption. Option D (NAT Gateway) routes traffic over the public internet, which violates the requirement for private connectivity and security.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain21_4',
  NOW(),
  12,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_21_4',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_21_m_21_4_30',
  'A large organization operates numerous Amazon VPCs across several AWS Regions, each hosting different departmental applications. They need to establish secure and scalable network connectivity between all these VPCs and also connect them to their on-premises data centers. The organization wants to simplify network management, reduce the complexity of managing many-to-many peering connections, and optimize data transfer costs. Which AWS networking service is designed for this type of complex, hub-and-spoke network topology?',
  'mcq',
  '["Implement individual VPC Peering connections between every pair of VPCs that need to communicate.","Use AWS Direct Connect to establish dedicated private connections from each VPC to the on-premises data centers.","Deploy an AWS Transit Gateway and connect all VPCs and on-premises networks to it.","Configure a series of AWS Site-to-Site VPN connections between each VPC and the on-premises data centers."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C, deploying an AWS Transit Gateway, is the ideal solution for this scenario. AWS Transit Gateway simplifies network management by acting as a central hub for connecting multiple VPCs and on-premises networks. This eliminates the need for complex, many-to-many VPC peering connections (which become unmanageable with a large number of VPCs) and provides a scalable, cost-effective way to route traffic. Option A (individual VPC Peering connections) quickly becomes complex and difficult to manage as the number of VPCs grows. Option B (AWS Direct Connect) is for connecting on-premises to AWS, but doesn''t solve the inter-VPC connectivity problem efficiently. Option D (Site-to-Site VPN connections) would also result in a complex mesh of connections for a large number of VPCs and on-premises locations.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain21_4',
  NOW(),
  13,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_21_4',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────
-- Module: m_21_1
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_21_m_21_1_26',
  'A company is launching new Amazon EC2 instances for its general-purpose web application. Each EC2 instance requires a boot volume and a data volume for application files. The application needs a balanced price/performance for its block storage, with a baseline of 3,000 IOPS and 125 MiB/s throughput, which is sufficient for its current needs. The company wants to select the MOST cost-effective Amazon EBS volume type that meets these requirements without over-provisioning.',
  'mcq',
  '["Provision an io2 Block Express volume to ensure the highest possible performance for the application.","Select gp3 volumes for both the boot and data volumes.","Choose gp2 volumes, as they are the default and provide good general-purpose performance.","Utilize st1 volumes for the data volume, as they are optimized for throughput for large, sequential workloads."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B, selecting gp3 volumes, is the most cost-effective choice. gp3 volumes provide a baseline of 3,000 IOPS and 125 MiB/s throughput at no extra cost, which perfectly meets the application''s requirements. They offer a 20% lower cost per GB than gp2 volumes and allow independent provisioning of IOPS and throughput, making them highly cost-efficient for general-purpose workloads. Option A (io2 Block Express) is designed for the highest performance, mission-critical applications requiring up to 256,000 IOPS and is significantly more expensive than gp3, representing anti-pattern ap-4.1-002. Option C (gp2 volumes) are older generation and more expensive than gp3 for the same performance. Option D (st1 volumes) are throughput-optimized HDD volumes, unsuitable for boot volumes or workloads requiring balanced IOPS and throughput.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain21_4',
  NOW(),
  14,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_21_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_21_m_21_1_27',
  'A research institution generates large datasets that must be retained for decades for compliance and future analysis. These datasets are rarely accessed, perhaps once a year or less, but when they are needed, retrieval must be possible within minutes. The institution''s primary concern is minimizing long-term storage costs. Which Amazon S3 storage class is the MOST cost-effective for this archival data with infrequent, rapid retrieval needs?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon S3 Standard, for immediate access and high durability.","Amazon S3 Standard-Infrequent Access (S3 Standard-IA), for data accessed less frequently but requiring rapid access.","Amazon S3 Glacier Instant Retrieval, for long-term archives that need millisecond retrieval.","Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive, for the lowest cost archival storage with retrieval times of hours."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C, Amazon S3 Glacier Instant Retrieval, is the most cost-effective solution for archival data that is rarely accessed but requires retrieval within minutes. This storage class offers the lowest cost storage for immediate access archives, with retrieval times in milliseconds, making it ideal for the specified requirement. Option A (S3 Standard) is too expensive for rarely accessed data. Option B (S3 Standard-IA) is for data accessed less frequently but still more often than ''once a year or less'', and is more expensive than Glacier Instant Retrieval for deep archives. Option D (S3 Glacier Deep Archive) offers the absolute lowest storage cost but has retrieval times measured in hours, which does not meet the ''within minutes'' requirement.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain21_4',
  NOW(),
  15,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_21_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_21_m_21_1_28',
  'A company provides a large public dataset to its community of researchers and partners. This dataset is stored in an Amazon S3 bucket. The company wants to allow external users to access this data directly, but they want to ensure that the costs associated with data retrieval (data transfer out, S3 requests) are borne by the users who access the data, rather than by the company itself. Which Amazon S3 feature should the company enable on its bucket?',
  'mcq',
  '["Configure an S3 Lifecycle policy to transition data to S3 Glacier after a certain period.","Implement S3 Block Public Access settings to restrict unauthorized access to the data.","Utilize S3 Cross-Region Replication to make the data available in multiple AWS Regions.","Enable S3 Requester Pays on the bucket."]'::json,
  '{3}',
  'Option D, enabling S3 Requester Pays, is the correct solution. When Requester Pays is enabled on an Amazon S3 bucket, the requester (the user downloading the data) is charged for the data transfer and request costs, rather than the bucket owner. This directly addresses the company''s requirement to shift retrieval costs to the external users. Option A (S3 Lifecycle policy) is for managing storage costs by moving data to cheaper tiers over time, not for shifting retrieval costs. Option B (S3 Block Public Access) is for security, preventing public access to the bucket. Option C (S3 Cross-Region Replication) is for data redundancy and availability across regions, not for cost allocation to requesters.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain21_4',
  NOW(),
  16,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_21_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

