INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_21_m_21_2_30',
  'A media company processes large volumes of video files for transcoding. These processing jobs are fault-tolerant and can tolerate interruptions, but require significant compute resources when active. The company needs to minimize compute costs for these non-urgent, flexible workloads. They currently use On-Demand Amazon EC2 instances, which are becoming expensive due to the scale of operations. The solution must ensure that the video processing can be completed within a reasonable timeframe, even with potential interruptions, and adhere to the lowest possible cost model.',
  'mcq',
  '["Utilize Amazon EC2 Spot Instances for the video transcoding workloads.","Purchase Amazon EC2 Reserved Instances with a 3-year commitment for the processing servers.","Implement AWS Savings Plans for the entire compute fleet, including the video transcoding instances.","Migrate the video transcoding application to AWS Lambda functions to benefit from serverless cost efficiency."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A is the most cost-effective solution for fault-tolerant, flexible workloads that can tolerate interruptions. Amazon EC2 Spot Instances allow customers to bid on unused EC2 capacity, offering significant discounts (up to 90% off On-Demand prices). Since the video transcoding jobs are fault-tolerant, they can be stopped and restarted without major issues, making Spot Instances an ideal fit for cost optimization. Option B is incorrect because Reserved Instances are best suited for steady-state, predictable workloads over a long term, and using them for variable or unpredictable workloads (like fault-tolerant processing that can be interrupted) is an anti-pattern ([ap-4.2-001]). Option C, AWS Savings Plans, offers flexibility across instance types and Regions but still assumes a consistent compute spend commitment, which might not be fully utilized if workloads are frequently interrupted or scaled down dramatically. While more flexible than RIs, Spot Instances offer deeper discounts for interruptible workloads. Option D, AWS Lambda, is designed for event-driven, short-duration functions (up to 15 minutes per invocation). Large-scale video transcoding typically involves longer-running processes and significant memory/CPU, making Lambda less suitable or requiring complex orchestration, which increases operational overhead and might exceed Lambda''s practical limits for this type of workload.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain21_4',
  NOW(),
  1,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_21_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_21_m_21_2_31',
  'A financial analytics firm runs a suite of reporting applications on Amazon EC2 instances. These applications have a consistent baseline usage but experience predictable spikes during month-end reporting cycles. The firm wants to reduce its overall compute costs while maintaining flexibility to adapt to changes in instance families or operating systems over time. They are currently using On-Demand instances and are looking for a long-term commitment strategy that offers significant savings without locking them into specific instance configurations.',
  'mcq',
  '["Purchase Amazon EC2 Reserved Instances for the expected baseline capacity.","Implement AWS Savings Plans for a 1-year compute commitment.","Utilize Amazon EC2 Spot Instances for all reporting applications.","Right-size all Amazon EC2 instances to the smallest possible type and rely solely on Auto Scaling."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B, implementing AWS Savings Plans, is the most appropriate and cost-effective solution. Savings Plans offer significant discounts (up to 72%) in exchange for a commitment to a consistent amount of compute usage (measured in USD/hour) for a 1-year or 3-year term. They provide flexibility across Amazon EC2 instance families, sizes, operating systems, and even AWS Regions, making them ideal for workloads with consistent usage but evolving instance requirements. Option A, purchasing Amazon EC2 Reserved Instances, offers similar discounts but locks the customer into specific instance types, operating systems, and tenancy, which lacks the flexibility the firm desires. Option C, utilizing Amazon EC2 Spot Instances, is not suitable for critical reporting applications that cannot tolerate interruptions, even if they are fault-tolerant. Spot Instances are best for flexible, non-critical, or batch workloads. Option D, while right-sizing is a good practice, relying solely on it without a purchasing option like Savings Plans will not achieve the same level of cost reduction for consistent baseline usage and does not address the long-term commitment aspect.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain21_4',
  NOW(),
  2,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_21_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_21_m_21_2_32',
  'A development team frequently provisions Amazon EC2 instances for testing new features. These instances are typically used for 8-10 hours during weekdays and are left running overnight and on weekends, leading to unnecessary costs. The team needs a solution to automatically stop these development environments when not in use and restart them when needed, ensuring the LEAST operational overhead for the developers. The solution should be simple to implement and manage.',
  'mcq',
  '["Manually stop and start Amazon EC2 instances at the beginning and end of each workday.","Implement a custom script on an Amazon EC2 instance to stop and start other instances based on a schedule.","Use AWS Lambda and Amazon EventBridge to create a scheduled solution for stopping and starting instances.","Purchase Amazon EC2 Reserved Instances for all development environments to reduce the hourly cost."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C, using AWS Lambda and Amazon EventBridge, is the most cost-effective and operationally efficient solution. An EventBridge scheduled rule can trigger an AWS Lambda function to stop instances at the end of the workday and start them at the beginning, eliminating idle costs. This approach is serverless, requires no dedicated infrastructure for the scheduler, and has minimal operational overhead. Option A, manually stopping and starting instances, is prone to human error and high operational overhead, failing the ''LEAST operational overhead'' constraint. Option B, implementing a custom script on an Amazon EC2 instance, introduces additional cost for the scheduler instance itself and requires management of that instance, which is more operational overhead than a serverless solution. Option D, purchasing Amazon EC2 Reserved Instances, is an anti-pattern ([ap-4.2-002]) for idle development environments. While it reduces the hourly rate, it still commits to paying for 24/7 capacity, which is not cost-optimized for instances used only part-time.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain21_4',
  NOW(),
  3,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_21_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────
-- Module: m_21_3
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_21_m_21_3_30',
  'A small startup is developing a new mobile application that requires a relational database backend. The application is expected to have highly unpredictable and intermittent traffic patterns, with long periods of inactivity followed by sudden bursts of activity. The startup needs a database solution that can scale automatically to handle these fluctuations without requiring manual capacity planning, and they want to pay only for the database resources consumed, avoiding costs for idle capacity. The primary constraint is to achieve the MOST cost-effective database solution.',
  'mcq',
  '["Provision a standard Amazon RDS MySQL instance with a Multi-AZ deployment.","Utilize Amazon Aurora Serverless v2 for the relational database backend.","Deploy a self-managed MySQL database on an Amazon EC2 instance.","Configure Amazon DynamoDB with provisioned capacity for the application''s data."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B, utilizing Amazon Aurora Serverless v2, is the most cost-effective solution for highly unpredictable and intermittent relational database workloads. Aurora Serverless v2 scales capacity in fine-grained increments and can scale down to minimal Aurora Capacity Units (ACUs) during idle periods, ensuring that the startup pays only for the database resources consumed and avoids paying for unused provisioned capacity. This directly addresses the anti-pattern ([ap-4.3-003]) of not using Aurora Serverless v2 for intermittent database workloads. Option A, provisioning a standard Amazon RDS MySQL instance with Multi-AZ, would incur costs for provisioned capacity 24/7, even during idle periods, and Multi-AZ doubles the cost for high availability, which might be overkill for a startup''s initial phase or non-critical development. Option C, deploying a self-managed MySQL database on an Amazon EC2 instance, would involve significant operational overhead for patching, backups, and scaling, and would still incur costs for the EC2 instance even when idle. Option D, configuring Amazon DynamoDB with provisioned capacity, is incorrect because DynamoDB is a NoSQL database, and the scenario specifies a relational database. Furthermore, provisioned capacity for DynamoDB is suboptimal for unpredictable traffic patterns, as it risks over-provisioning or throttling, which is an anti-pattern ([ap-4.3-002]).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain21_4',
  NOW(),
  4,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_21_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_21_m_21_3_31',
  'A gaming company is launching a new mobile game that stores player profiles and game state in a NoSQL database. The game is expected to experience highly variable and unpredictable traffic, with sudden spikes during new feature releases or promotional events. The company needs a database solution that can automatically scale to handle these fluctuations without manual intervention or capacity planning, ensuring consistent performance while minimizing costs. The solution must be fully managed and pay-per-request.',
  'mcq',
  '["Configure Amazon DynamoDB with provisioned capacity for reads and writes.","Utilize Amazon DynamoDB in on-demand capacity mode.","Deploy an Amazon RDS PostgreSQL instance with read replicas.","Set up an Amazon ElastiCache for Redis cluster to cache player data."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B, utilizing Amazon DynamoDB in on-demand capacity mode, is the most appropriate and cost-effective solution for highly variable and unpredictable NoSQL workloads. On-demand mode automatically scales read and write capacity based on actual usage, and customers pay per request, eliminating the need for capacity planning and avoiding costs for over-provisioned capacity. This directly addresses the anti-pattern ([ap-4.3-002]) of using provisioned capacity for DynamoDB with unpredictable traffic. Option A, configuring Amazon DynamoDB with provisioned capacity, would require upfront capacity planning and risks over-provisioning (leading to unnecessary costs) or under-provisioning (leading to throttling and performance issues) for unpredictable workloads. Option C, deploying an Amazon RDS PostgreSQL instance, is a relational database solution, which is not suitable for a NoSQL requirement. Option D, setting up an Amazon ElastiCache for Redis cluster, is a caching solution, not a primary database. While caching can reduce database load, it does not replace the primary database and would add complexity without solving the core database scalability and cost challenge for unpredictable traffic.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain21_4',
  NOW(),
  5,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_21_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_21_m_21_3_32',
  'A software development company uses Amazon RDS for its various environments. For production applications, high availability is critical, so they use Multi-AZ deployments. However, for their non-critical development and testing databases, which are only used during business hours and do not require immediate failover, they are also using Multi-AZ. The company wants to reduce costs for these non-production environments without impacting the production setup. The goal is to achieve the MOST cost-optimized database solution for development and testing.',
  'mcq',
  '["Migrate all development and testing databases to Amazon DynamoDB.","Keep the existing Amazon RDS Multi-AZ deployments for development and testing for consistency.","Convert the Amazon RDS Multi-AZ deployments for development and testing to Single-AZ deployments.","Implement Amazon ElastiCache for Redis in front of the development databases to reduce load."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C, converting the Amazon RDS Multi-AZ deployments for development and testing to Single-AZ deployments, is the most cost-effective solution. Amazon RDS Multi-AZ doubles the cost by maintaining a synchronous standby replica for high availability. For non-critical development and testing databases that do not require high availability or immediate failover, a Single-AZ deployment is sufficient and significantly cheaper. This directly addresses the anti-pattern ([ap-4.3-001]) of running RDS Multi-AZ for non-critical development databases. Option A, migrating to Amazon DynamoDB, might be a valid solution for some use cases but introduces a different database type and potential migration effort, which is not the most direct or cost-effective optimization for an existing relational database in a non-critical environment. Option B, keeping Multi-AZ deployments, fails the cost optimization constraint for non-critical environments. Option D, implementing Amazon ElastiCache for Redis, is a caching solution that reduces database load but does not address the underlying cost of the Multi-AZ deployment for the primary database itself.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain21_4',
  NOW(),
  6,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_21_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_21_m_21_3_33',
  'A popular online news portal uses Amazon RDS for its main content database. The portal experiences a high volume of read traffic, especially during breaking news events, which sometimes impacts the performance of the primary database and increases its operational costs due to scaling requirements. The company wants to offload read-heavy queries from the primary database to improve performance and reduce the cost associated with scaling the primary instance, without changing the application''s write patterns. The solution must be highly available and integrated with Amazon RDS.',
  'mcq',
  '["Create Amazon RDS Read Replicas and direct read traffic to them.","Migrate the entire database to Amazon DynamoDB for better read scalability.","Upgrade the primary Amazon RDS instance to a larger, more powerful instance type.","Implement Amazon ElastiCache for Memcached to cache all database queries."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A, creating Amazon RDS Read Replicas and directing read traffic to them, is the most effective and cost-optimized solution for offloading read-heavy queries. Read Replicas allow you to scale out your database''s read capacity independently of the primary instance, reducing the load on the primary and potentially allowing it to run on a smaller, less expensive instance type. This improves performance and reduces costs. Option B, migrating to Amazon DynamoDB, is a NoSQL solution and would require significant application changes for a relational database, which is not the most direct or cost-effective approach for this scenario. Option C, upgrading the primary Amazon RDS instance, is vertical scaling and would increase costs significantly without addressing the fundamental issue of separating read and write workloads, and it still has scaling limits. Option D, implementing Amazon ElastiCache for Memcached, is a caching solution that can reduce database load, but it requires application-level changes to implement caching logic and might not be suitable for all types of read queries or data freshness requirements. Read Replicas provide a direct, integrated solution for scaling read capacity.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain21_4',
  NOW(),
  7,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_21_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_21_m_21_3_34',
  'An analytics application frequently queries a large Amazon RDS PostgreSQL database, leading to high database CPU utilization and increased costs for scaling the primary instance. Many of these queries are for frequently accessed, static reference data that changes infrequently. The development team wants to reduce the load on the database and improve query response times for this specific type of data, thereby optimizing overall database costs. The solution should minimize changes to the application logic and be highly available.',
  'mcq',
  '["Implement an Amazon ElastiCache for Redis cluster to cache the reference data.","Upgrade the Amazon RDS PostgreSQL instance to a larger, more powerful instance class.","Create an Amazon RDS Read Replica and direct all queries for reference data to it.","Migrate the static reference data to an Amazon S3 bucket and access it directly."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A, implementing an Amazon ElastiCache for Redis cluster, is the most effective solution for caching frequently accessed, static reference data. Caching this data in ElastiCache significantly reduces the load on the Amazon RDS database, improves query response times, and can lead to cost savings by allowing the RDS instance to potentially run on a smaller, less expensive instance type. Redis offers high performance and availability. Option B, upgrading the Amazon RDS instance, is a vertical scaling approach that increases costs and does not address the root cause of repetitive queries for static data. Option C, creating an Amazon RDS Read Replica, would offload read traffic but still involves querying a database instance, which is less efficient for static, frequently accessed data compared to an in-memory cache. Option D, migrating static reference data to an Amazon S3 bucket, is a valid approach for static content but would require changes to the application to retrieve data from S3 instead of the database, and might introduce higher latency compared to an in-memory cache for frequent lookups.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain21_4',
  NOW(),
  8,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_21_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────
-- Module: m_21_4
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_21_m_21_4_31',
  'A company hosts several internal applications on Amazon EC2 instances within a private subnet. These applications need to securely access data stored in Amazon S3 and Amazon DynamoDB without traversing the public internet. The current architecture routes all outbound traffic, including to AWS services, through a NAT Gateway, which is incurring significant data processing charges. The company wants to reduce network data transfer costs while maintaining secure, private connectivity to these AWS services.',
  'mcq',
  '["Configure a VPC endpoint for Amazon S3 and a VPC endpoint for Amazon DynamoDB.","Route all traffic through an AWS Direct Connect connection to on-premises data centers.","Implement a VPN connection from the private subnet to the AWS service endpoints.","Create a new public subnet and move the Amazon EC2 instances there to access services directly."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A, configuring a VPC endpoint for Amazon S3 and a VPC endpoint for Amazon DynamoDB, is the most cost-effective and secure solution. Gateway VPC endpoints for Amazon S3 and Amazon DynamoDB are free and allow instances in private subnets to access these services directly over the AWS network, without requiring a NAT Gateway or an internet gateway. This eliminates NAT Gateway data processing charges for traffic to these services. This directly addresses the anti-pattern ([ap-4.4-001]) of using NAT Gateway for traffic that could go through VPC endpoints. Option B, routing traffic through AWS Direct Connect, is for hybrid cloud connectivity to on-premises networks, not for optimizing intra-AWS service communication. Option C, implementing a VPN connection, is typically used for secure connections between a VPC and an on-premises network or another VPC, and would still incur data transfer costs and potentially VPN connection charges. Option D, moving instances to a public subnet, would expose them to the public internet, compromising security and not necessarily reducing data transfer costs for internal AWS service access.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain21_4',
  NOW(),
  9,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_21_4',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_21_m_21_4_32',
  'A small startup has a web application running on Amazon EC2 instances in a private subnet. These instances need to initiate outbound connections to retrieve third-party APIs and perform software updates. Currently, the startup uses a NAT instance running on an Amazon EC2 t3.micro instance in a public subnet to facilitate this outbound internet access. The company is looking for a more robust, highly available, and operationally simpler solution that also offers better cost efficiency for its outbound internet traffic.',
  'mcq',
  '["Continue using the NAT instance and scale it up to a larger instance type.","Replace the NAT instance with a NAT Gateway.","Configure an Internet Gateway and move the Amazon EC2 instances to a public subnet.","Implement AWS PrivateLink to connect to all third-party APIs."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B, replacing the NAT instance with a NAT Gateway, is the most appropriate solution. A NAT Gateway is a fully managed AWS service that provides high availability, higher bandwidth, and requires no administrative effort for patching or scaling, unlike a self-managed NAT instance. While NAT Gateway has an hourly charge and data processing fees, it is generally more cost-effective and operationally simpler than managing a NAT instance, especially considering the operational overhead and potential single point of failure of a NAT instance. Option A, continuing with a NAT instance, retains the operational burden and single point of failure, and scaling it up increases costs without addressing the management overhead. Option C, moving instances to a public subnet, would expose the application instances directly to the internet, which is a security risk and generally not recommended for application servers. Option D, implementing AWS PrivateLink, is used for private connectivity to specific AWS services or services hosted by other AWS customers, not for general outbound internet access to arbitrary third-party APIs.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain21_4',
  NOW(),
  10,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_21_4',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_21_m_21_4_33',
  'A global enterprise has applications deployed in multiple AWS Regions. These applications frequently exchange large volumes of data between Regions, primarily for data synchronization and analytics. The current setup routes inter-Region traffic over the public internet, which results in inconsistent performance, higher latency, and escalating data egress costs from the source Region. The company needs a secure, reliable, and cost-effective way to transfer data privately between its VPCs in different AWS Regions.',
  'mcq',
  '["Establish VPC peering connections between the VPCs in different Regions.","Continue routing traffic over the public internet and use S3 Transfer Acceleration.","Implement AWS Direct Connect connections between each Region and on-premises data centers.","Deploy a VPN connection over the public internet between the VPCs in different Regions."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A, establishing VPC peering connections between the VPCs in different Regions, is the most cost-effective and direct solution for private inter-Region traffic. VPC peering allows private IP address communication between VPCs, leveraging the AWS global backbone network, which offers better reliability, lower latency, and is often more cost-effective than routing traffic through the public internet. This directly addresses the anti-pattern ([ap-4.4-002]) of routing inter-Region traffic through the public internet. Option B, continuing to use the public internet, fails to address the cost, performance, and security concerns, and S3 Transfer Acceleration is specific to S3 uploads/downloads, not general inter-Region VPC traffic. Option C, implementing AWS Direct Connect, is for connecting on-premises networks to AWS, not for inter-Region VPC communication within AWS. Option D, deploying a VPN connection over the public internet, provides encryption but still uses the public internet, which can suffer from inconsistent performance and higher latency compared to AWS''s private backbone, and it incurs VPN connection costs.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain21_4',
  NOW(),
  11,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_21_4',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_21_m_21_4_34',
  'A global content delivery network company frequently uploads large video files from various geographically dispersed client locations to an Amazon S3 bucket in a single AWS Region for processing. Clients often report slow upload speeds, especially those far from the S3 bucket''s Region. The company needs to improve the upload performance for these distant clients and reduce the time it takes to ingest content, without significantly altering their existing application architecture. The solution should be cost-effective and leverage AWS services.',
  'mcq',
  '["Enable S3 Transfer Acceleration on the Amazon S3 bucket.","Create an Amazon S3 bucket in each geographic Region where clients are located.","Use Amazon CloudFront to cache the S3 bucket content for faster downloads.","Implement AWS Direct Connect from each client location to the AWS Region."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A, enabling S3 Transfer Acceleration on the Amazon S3 bucket, is the most appropriate and cost-effective solution. S3 Transfer Acceleration uses Amazon CloudFront''s globally distributed edge locations to route uploads over the optimized AWS backbone network, significantly improving transfer speeds for clients located far from the S3 bucket''s Region. This directly addresses the anti-pattern ([ap-4.4-003]) of not using S3 Transfer Acceleration when uploading from distant locations. Option B, creating S3 buckets in multiple Regions, would require complex data synchronization and management across buckets, increasing operational overhead and potentially costs. Option C, using Amazon CloudFront, is primarily for accelerating content delivery (downloads) to users, not for accelerating uploads to S3. Option D, implementing AWS Direct Connect, is for establishing a dedicated network connection between an on-premises data center and AWS, which is not practical or cost-effective for individual client locations and does not address general internet-based upload performance.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain21_4',
  NOW(),
  12,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_21_4',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────
-- Module: m_21_1
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_21_m_21_1_29',
  'A research institution stores petabytes of scientific data in Amazon S3. This data is frequently accessed for analysis during the first 30 days after upload, but after that period, access frequency drops significantly, becoming very infrequent for the next year. After one year, the data is rarely accessed but must be retained for compliance purposes for an additional five years. The institution wants to optimize storage costs by automatically moving data to cheaper storage tiers based on its access patterns. The goal is the MOST cost-effective storage solution.',
  'mcq',
  '["Keep all data in Amazon S3 Standard storage class indefinitely.","Configure an S3 Lifecycle policy to transition objects to S3 Standard-IA after 30 days and to S3 Glacier Deep Archive after 1 year.","Enable Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering for the bucket to automatically manage storage tiers.","Manually move data to Amazon S3 Glacier after 30 days and then to S3 Glacier Deep Archive after 1 year."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B, configuring an S3 Lifecycle policy, is the most cost-effective solution for data with known and predictable access patterns over time. The policy can automatically transition objects to Amazon S3 Standard-Infrequent Access (S3 Standard-IA) after 30 days (for infrequent access) and then to Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive after 1 year (for long-term archival with the lowest storage cost). This directly addresses the anti-pattern ([ap-4.1-001]) of keeping all S3 data in Standard tier without lifecycle policies. Option A, keeping all data in Amazon S3 Standard, is not cost-optimized as it pays for the highest-cost tier for infrequently accessed and archived data. Option C, enabling Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering, is ideal for data with unknown or changing access patterns, but for clearly defined patterns, a lifecycle policy offers more precise control and potentially greater cost savings by directly moving to the most appropriate tier. Option D, manually moving data, is operationally inefficient, prone to error, and not scalable for petabytes of data.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain21_4',
  NOW(),
  13,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_21_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_21_m_21_1_30',
  'A database administrator is provisioning a new Amazon EBS volume for a production database that requires a consistent performance of 5,000 IOPS and 200 MiB/s throughput. The current plan is to use a Provisioned IOPS SSD (io2) volume. However, the team is looking to optimize costs without compromising the required performance. The administrator needs to select the MOST cost-effective EBS volume type that meets the specified IOPS and throughput requirements.',
  'mcq',
  '["Provision an Amazon EBS Provisioned IOPS SSD (io2) volume with the required IOPS.","Utilize an Amazon EBS General Purpose SSD (gp3) volume and provision additional IOPS and throughput.","Use an Amazon EBS General Purpose SSD (gp2) volume and rely on burst performance.","Select an Amazon EBS Throughput Optimized HDD (st1) volume for cost savings."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B, utilizing an Amazon EBS General Purpose SSD (gp3) volume and provisioning additional IOPS and throughput, is the most cost-effective solution. gp3 volumes provide a baseline of 3,000 IOPS and 125 MiB/s throughput at no additional cost. You can provision additional IOPS (up to 16,000) and throughput (up to 1,000 MiB/s) independently for a small extra charge. For 5,000 IOPS and 200 MiB/s, gp3 can easily meet these requirements at a lower cost than io2. This directly addresses the anti-pattern ([ap-4.1-002]) of using Provisioned IOPS (io2) EBS when gp3 meets the IOPS requirement. Option A, provisioning an Amazon EBS Provisioned IOPS SSD (io2) volume, would meet the requirements but is significantly more expensive than gp3 for the specified performance levels. Option C, using an Amazon EBS General Purpose SSD (gp2) volume, relies on burst performance and might not consistently deliver 5,000 IOPS, making it unsuitable for a production database with consistent performance requirements. Option D, selecting an Amazon EBS Throughput Optimized HDD (st1) volume, is designed for large sequential workloads (like big data or log processing) and is not suitable for transactional databases requiring high random IOPS.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain21_4',
  NOW(),
  14,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_21_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_21_m_21_1_31',
  'A digital media company has a large archive of historical video content that is rarely accessed, perhaps once every few years, but must be preserved indefinitely. The company wants to store this content in the most cost-effective way possible, accepting retrieval times that could range from several hours to a day. The solution must provide extreme durability and be highly scalable to accommodate future growth of the archive. The primary constraint is to achieve the absolute lowest storage cost.',
  'mcq',
  '["Store the video content in Amazon S3 Standard storage class.","Utilize Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering for the video archive.","Archive the video content directly into Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive.","Keep the video content on Amazon EFS with a lifecycle policy to Infrequent Access."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C, archiving the video content directly into Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive, is the most cost-effective solution for data that is rarely accessed (once every few years) and can tolerate retrieval times of several hours to a day. Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive offers the lowest storage cost in the cloud, specifically designed for long-term archival. Option A, storing in Amazon S3 Standard, is significantly more expensive and not suitable for rarely accessed archives. Option B, utilizing Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering, is designed for unpredictable access patterns and would eventually move data to archive tiers, but directly placing it in S3 Glacier Deep Archive is more cost-effective for data known to be rarely accessed. Option D, keeping content on Amazon EFS, is a file system service for shared access, which is generally much more expensive than S3 Glacier for archival purposes and not designed for this use case.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain21_4',
  NOW(),
  15,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_21_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

