INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_21_m_21_2_33',
  'A company operates a data processing application that runs for several hours daily, but the exact start and end times are variable and unpredictable. The application is designed to be fault-tolerant and can gracefully handle interruptions, resuming its work from the last checkpoint. The operations team needs to execute this workload in the MOST cost-effective manner possible, without compromising the ability to complete the processing within a flexible daily window.',
  'mcq',
  '["Launch the application on Amazon EC2 Spot Instances, leveraging their lower cost for fault-tolerant workloads.","Utilize Amazon EC2 Reserved Instances with a 1-year commitment to secure a discounted rate for the daily operation.","Deploy the application on Amazon EC2 On-Demand Instances, scaling them up and down manually as needed.","Migrate the data processing logic to an AWS Lambda function, triggered by an Amazon EventBridge schedule."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A is the most cost-effective solution for this scenario. Amazon EC2 Spot Instances offer significant discounts compared to On-Demand pricing, making them ideal for fault-tolerant, flexible workloads that can tolerate interruptions. Since the application can resume from checkpoints, it is well-suited for Spot Instances. Option B (Reserved Instances) is not suitable for variable or unpredictable workloads (ap-4.2-001) as it requires a steady-state commitment. Option C (On-Demand Instances) would be more expensive than Spot Instances for this type of workload. Option D (AWS Lambda) is generally for shorter, event-driven tasks and might not be suitable for ''several hours daily'' continuous processing without complex orchestration, and could become more expensive than Spot for long-running batch jobs.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain21_4',
  NOW(),
  1,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_21_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_21_m_21_2_34',
  'A development team maintains several Amazon EC2 instances for their staging and testing environments. These environments are typically only utilized during standard business hours, Monday through Friday, but are currently running 24/7. The team''s primary goal is to significantly reduce the operational costs associated with these non-production instances while ensuring they are available when developers need them during working hours.',
  'mcq',
  '["Purchase Amazon EC2 Reserved Instances for all development instances to achieve a discounted hourly rate.","Implement an automated solution using AWS Lambda and Amazon EventBridge to stop instances outside business hours and start them before the next workday.","Manually stop and start the Amazon EC2 instances at the beginning and end of each workday.","Downsize the Amazon EC2 instances to smaller types (e.g., t3.micro) and run them continuously 24/7."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is the most cost-effective and operationally efficient solution. By using AWS Lambda and Amazon EventBridge, the instances can be automatically stopped when not in use (outside business hours) and started when needed, eliminating idle costs (ap-4.2-002) with minimal manual intervention. Option A (Reserved Instances) would still incur costs for 24/7 usage, which is not optimal for environments used only during business hours. Option C (Manual stop/start) is prone to human error and adds operational overhead. Option D (Downsizing) reduces costs but still incurs 24/7 charges for idle periods.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain21_4',
  NOW(),
  2,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_21_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_21_m_21_2_35',
  'An e-commerce company experiences predictable traffic spikes during major sales events and holidays, requiring substantial compute capacity for its web application. During off-peak times, the traffic is moderate. The current architecture relies on a single, very large Amazon EC2 instance, which is often underutilized outside of peak periods. The company aims to optimize compute costs while ensuring the application can seamlessly scale to meet demand.',
  'mcq',
  '["Upgrade the single large Amazon EC2 instance to an even more powerful instance type to handle all potential peak loads.","Purchase a 3-year Amazon EC2 Reserved Instance for the existing large instance to reduce its hourly cost.","Implement an Auto Scaling group with multiple smaller Amazon EC2 instances, configured to scale based on demand.","Migrate the application to AWS Fargate, running a single large container to handle all traffic."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C is the most cost-effective and scalable solution. Using an Auto Scaling group with multiple smaller Amazon EC2 instances allows the application to scale horizontally, adding or removing instances based on actual demand. This avoids over-provisioning during off-peak times, which is a common anti-pattern (ap-4.2-003). Option A (Upgrading to a larger instance) exacerbates the over-provisioning issue. Option B (Reserved Instance for a large instance) locks in the cost for an often underutilized resource. Option D (AWS Fargate with a single large container) still suffers from the single point of failure and potential over-provisioning if not configured to scale horizontally.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain21_4',
  NOW(),
  3,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_21_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_21_m_21_2_36',
  'A technology company is modernizing its backend microservices, which are containerized and run on Linux. The application is CPU-intensive but has no specific hardware dependencies that would prevent it from running on different processor architectures. The company is seeking to achieve the best possible price/performance ratio for its compute resources.',
  'mcq',
  '["Continue using x86-based Amazon EC2 instances and purchase 3-year Savings Plans for maximum discounts.","Migrate the containerized microservices to AWS Fargate to leverage a serverless compute model.","Right-size the existing x86-based Amazon EC2 instances to the smallest possible size that meets performance requirements.","Re-platform the microservices to run on AWS Graviton (ARM-based) Amazon EC2 instances."]'::json,
  '{3}',
  'Option D is the most effective way to achieve the best price/performance ratio for compatible Linux-based workloads. AWS Graviton instances (ARM-based) offer up to 40% better price/performance over comparable x86 instances (ap-4.2-004) for many workloads, including containerized microservices. Option A (Savings Plans for x86) provides discounts but doesn''t address the underlying price/performance advantage of Graviton. Option B (AWS Fargate) is a serverless option, but Graviton instances specifically target the price/performance aspect of the underlying compute. Option C (Right-sizing x86 instances) is a good practice but doesn''t leverage the architectural benefits of Graviton.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain21_4',
  NOW(),
  4,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_21_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_21_m_21_2_37',
  'A financial institution operates a critical core banking application that requires a stable and predictable compute capacity, running continuously 24/7 for the foreseeable future (at least the next three years). The workload characteristics are well-understood, and the specific Amazon EC2 instance types required are unlikely to change. The institution''s primary objective is to minimize the long-term operational cost for this continuous, steady-state workload.',
  'mcq',
  '["Purchase 3-year Amazon EC2 Reserved Instances for the required instance types with an All Upfront payment option.","Utilize Amazon EC2 Spot Instances for the application to take advantage of significant cost savings.","Run the application on Amazon EC2 On-Demand Instances and rely on AWS Cost Explorer to identify optimization opportunities.","Implement an Auto Scaling group with On-Demand Instances to dynamically adjust capacity based on real-time load."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A is the most cost-effective solution for a stable, predictable, and continuous 24/7 workload over a multi-year period. Amazon EC2 Reserved Instances offer substantial discounts compared to On-Demand pricing, with the largest discounts typically available for 3-year commitments and All Upfront payment. Option B (Spot Instances) is not suitable for critical, continuous workloads that cannot tolerate interruptions. Option C (On-Demand Instances) is the most expensive option for a predictable 24/7 workload. Option D (Auto Scaling with On-Demand) is designed for variable workloads, not for a steady-state 24/7 requirement, and would be more expensive than Reserved Instances for this use case.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain21_4',
  NOW(),
  5,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_21_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_21_m_21_2_38',
  'A company operates a data processing application that runs for several hours daily, but the exact start and end times are variable and unpredictable. The application is designed to be fault-tolerant and can gracefully handle interruptions, resuming its work from the last checkpoint. The operations team needs to execute this workload in the MOST cost-effective manner possible, without compromising the ability to complete the processing within a flexible daily window.',
  'mcq',
  '["Launch the application on Amazon EC2 Spot Instances, leveraging their lower cost for fault-tolerant workloads.","Utilize Amazon EC2 Reserved Instances with a 1-year commitment to secure a discounted rate for the daily operation.","Deploy the application on Amazon EC2 On-Demand Instances, scaling them up and down manually as needed.","Migrate the data processing logic to an AWS Lambda function, triggered by an Amazon EventBridge scheduled rule."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A is the most cost-effective solution for this scenario. Amazon EC2 Spot Instances offer significant discounts compared to On-Demand pricing, making them ideal for fault-tolerant, flexible workloads that can tolerate interruptions. Since the application can resume from checkpoints, it is well-suited for Spot Instances. Option B (Reserved Instances) is not suitable for variable or unpredictable workloads (ap-4.2-001) as it requires a steady-state commitment. Option C (On-Demand Instances) would be more expensive than Spot Instances for this type of workload. Option D (AWS Lambda) is generally for shorter, event-driven tasks and might not be suitable for ''several hours daily'' continuous processing without complex orchestration, and could become more expensive than Spot for long-running batch jobs.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain21_4',
  NOW(),
  6,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_21_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────
-- Module: m_21_3
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_21_m_21_3_35',
  'A mobile gaming company is launching a new game expected to attract a highly variable user base, with unpredictable traffic spikes during promotional events and quiet periods overnight. The game''s backend uses Amazon DynamoDB for storing player data and leaderboards. The company needs to ensure the database can handle extreme fluctuations in traffic without throttling, while also minimizing costs during low-traffic periods.',
  'mcq',
  '["Configure Amazon DynamoDB with Provisioned Capacity, setting high read/write capacity units to handle peak loads.","Switch the Amazon DynamoDB table to On-Demand capacity mode to automatically scale and pay per request.","Implement Amazon DynamoDB Global Tables to distribute traffic across multiple AWS Regions.","Migrate the player data and leaderboards to an Amazon RDS for MySQL database with Auto Scaling."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is the most cost-effective and scalable solution for highly variable and unpredictable workloads on Amazon DynamoDB. On-Demand capacity mode automatically scales capacity up and down based on actual traffic, and you only pay for the read and write requests your application performs, eliminating the need for capacity planning and avoiding over-provisioning (ap-4.3-002). Option A (Provisioned Capacity) risks over-provisioning during low traffic or throttling during unexpected spikes. Option C (Global Tables) is for multi-Region replication, not primarily for cost optimization of a single table''s variable traffic. Option D (Amazon RDS) is a relational database and not typically chosen for the extreme scalability and unpredictable traffic patterns of a gaming leaderboard, especially when DynamoDB is already in use.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain21_4',
  NOW(),
  7,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_21_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_21_m_21_3_36',
  'A small startup is developing an internal analytics dashboard that queries a relational database only a few times a day for short, bursty periods of activity. The database needs to be highly available for these intermittent operations but should not incur significant costs during the long idle periods. The team prefers a MySQL-compatible database engine.',
  'mcq',
  '["Provision an Amazon RDS for MySQL Multi-AZ instance to ensure high availability.","Deploy a MySQL database on an Amazon EC2 instance and manually stop/start it as needed.","Utilize Amazon Aurora Serverless v2 for MySQL, which scales capacity in fine-grained increments and down to minimal ACUs during idle periods.","Use Amazon RDS for MySQL Single-AZ and implement a scheduled task to stop and start the instance."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C is the most cost-effective solution for intermittent relational database workloads. Amazon Aurora Serverless v2 scales capacity almost instantly and in fine-grained increments, scaling down to a minimal amount of Aurora Capacity Units (ACUs) during idle periods, thus avoiding paying for unused provisioned capacity (ap-4.3-003). Option A (RDS Multi-AZ) doubles the cost by maintaining a synchronous standby, which is unnecessary for a non-critical, intermittent development database (ap-4.3-001). Option B (EC2 with MySQL) requires significant operational overhead for database management. Option D (RDS Single-AZ with scheduled stop/start) is a viable option but Aurora Serverless v2 offers more seamless scaling and less management overhead for intermittent workloads.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain21_4',
  NOW(),
  8,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_21_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_21_m_21_3_37',
  'A social media application frequently retrieves user profile data and trending topics from its Amazon DynamoDB database. These data points are relatively static for short periods but are accessed by millions of users, leading to high read capacity unit consumption and increased costs. The company aims to reduce the read load on DynamoDB and improve response times for these frequently accessed, unchanging data.',
  'mcq',
  '["Increase the provisioned read capacity units for the Amazon DynamoDB table.","Implement an Amazon ElastiCache cluster (e.g., Redis) to cache frequently accessed data.","Migrate the frequently accessed data to an Amazon RDS database for better read performance.","Enable Amazon DynamoDB Global Tables to distribute read traffic across multiple AWS Regions."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is the most cost-effective solution for reducing read load on Amazon DynamoDB for frequently accessed, relatively static data. Amazon ElastiCache (e.g., Redis or Memcached) can store copies of data, serving read requests from the cache instead of DynamoDB, which significantly reduces DynamoDB read capacity unit consumption and improves application response times. Option A (Increasing provisioned capacity) would directly increase costs without addressing the efficiency of data access. Option C (Migrating to Amazon RDS) is a different database type and would require re-architecture. Option D (DynamoDB Global Tables) is for multi-Region replication and high availability, not primarily for reducing read costs for a single-Region, read-heavy workload.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain21_4',
  NOW(),
  9,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_21_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────
-- Module: m_21_4
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_21_m_21_4_35',
  'An application running on Amazon EC2 instances within a private subnet needs to securely and cost-effectively interact with Amazon S3 buckets and Amazon DynamoDB tables located in the same AWS Region. The current setup routes all outbound traffic through a NAT Gateway, which incurs unnecessary data processing charges for these specific AWS service communications. The company wants to eliminate these charges.',
  'mcq',
  '["Configure an AWS Transit Gateway to route traffic directly to Amazon S3 and Amazon DynamoDB.","Deploy an Amazon EC2 NAT instance in a public subnet to replace the NAT Gateway.","Create Gateway VPC Endpoints for Amazon S3 and Amazon DynamoDB in the VPC.","Enable S3 Transfer Acceleration for the Amazon S3 buckets to optimize data transfer."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C is the most cost-effective and secure solution. Gateway VPC Endpoints for Amazon S3 and Amazon DynamoDB allow instances in private subnets to access these services without requiring a NAT Gateway or an Internet Gateway. This keeps traffic within the AWS network and eliminates NAT Gateway data processing charges (ap-4.4-001), as Gateway Endpoints are free. Option A (AWS Transit Gateway) is for connecting multiple VPCs or on-premises networks, not for direct service access within a single VPC. Option B (NAT instance) would still incur data processing charges and requires more management than a NAT Gateway. Option D (S3 Transfer Acceleration) is for optimizing uploads from distant locations, not for internal VPC access.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain21_4',
  NOW(),
  10,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_21_4',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_21_m_21_4_36',
  'A global enterprise has two separate VPCs, one in `us-east-1` and another in `eu-west-1`, each hosting different microservices that frequently exchange data. Currently, the microservices communicate over the public internet, which results in high data transfer costs and introduces variable latency. The company needs a more cost-effective and secure method for inter-Region communication between these VPCs.',
  'mcq',
  '["Configure an Internet Gateway in each VPC and route traffic between them over the public internet.","Establish AWS Direct Connect connections between each VPC and the on-premises data centers.","Set up an AWS PrivateLink connection between the two VPCs for secure service access.","Implement AWS Transit Gateway to connect the two VPCs across different AWS Regions."]'::json,
  '{3}',
  'Option D is the most cost-effective and scalable solution for connecting multiple VPCs across different AWS Regions. AWS Transit Gateway allows you to centralize routing and establish private, low-latency connections between VPCs in different Regions, avoiding the public internet (ap-4.4-002) and reducing data transfer costs compared to public egress. Option A (Internet Gateway) is the current problematic approach. Option B (AWS Direct Connect) is for connecting on-premises networks to AWS, not for inter-Region VPC-to-VPC communication. Option C (AWS PrivateLink) is for exposing a service privately to consumers in other VPCs, not for general VPC-to-VPC routing.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain21_4',
  NOW(),
  11,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_21_4',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_21_m_21_4_37',
  'A global media company frequently receives large video files uploaded by contributors located all over the world into a central Amazon S3 bucket in a single AWS Region. Contributors often report slow and unreliable upload speeds due to their geographical distance from the S3 bucket. The company wants to significantly improve the upload performance and reliability for these geographically dispersed users.',
  'mcq',
  '["Enable S3 Transfer Acceleration on the Amazon S3 bucket to utilize Amazon CloudFront''s edge locations.","Configure S3 Cross-Region Replication to copy objects to S3 buckets in multiple AWS Regions.","Instruct contributors to use AWS Direct Connect to upload their files directly to the S3 bucket.","Use Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering to automatically move uploaded objects to different storage classes."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A is the most effective solution for improving upload performance and reliability for geographically distant users. S3 Transfer Acceleration uses Amazon CloudFront''s globally distributed edge locations to route uploads over the optimized AWS backbone network, significantly reducing latency and improving speeds (ap-4.4-003). Option B (Cross-Region Replication) is for copying data between S3 buckets for redundancy or local access, not for improving initial upload performance. Option C (AWS Direct Connect) is for private connectivity from on-premises to AWS, not for individual contributors globally. Option D (S3 Intelligent-Tiering) is for optimizing storage costs based on access patterns, not for upload performance.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain21_4',
  NOW(),
  12,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_21_4',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_21_m_21_4_38',
  'A small development team has several Amazon EC2 instances in private subnets that require occasional outbound internet access for patching operating systems and making calls to external third-party APIs. The team is currently using a NAT Gateway, but they are looking for a more cost-effective solution for their low-volume, non-critical outbound traffic, even if it requires a bit more manual management.',
  'mcq',
  '["Provision an additional NAT Gateway in a different Availability Zone for redundancy and load balancing.","Deploy an Amazon EC2 NAT instance in a public subnet and configure routing through it.","Utilize Gateway VPC Endpoints for all outbound internet traffic.","Establish an AWS Direct Connect connection to route all outbound traffic through an on-premises firewall."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is a more cost-effective solution for low-volume, non-critical outbound internet traffic when compared to a NAT Gateway, especially if the team is willing to manage the instance. While a NAT Gateway offers higher availability and less operational overhead, a NAT instance can be cheaper for minimal usage. Option A (Additional NAT Gateway) would increase costs. Option C (Gateway VPC Endpoints) are only for specific AWS services like S3 and DynamoDB, not for general internet access. Option D (AWS Direct Connect) is for connecting on-premises networks to AWS, not for outbound internet access from VPC instances.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain21_4',
  NOW(),
  13,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_21_4',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_21_m_21_4_39',
  'A Software-as-a-Service (SaaS) provider offers a critical service running in their AWS VPC. They need to allow multiple customers, each with their own AWS VPCs, to securely and privately access this service without exposing traffic to the public internet or managing complex network configurations like VPC peering for each customer. The solution must be scalable to accommodate a growing number of customers.',
  'mcq',
  '["Configure an Internet Gateway in the service provider''s VPC and allow customer VPCs to connect over the public internet.","Set up VPC peering connections between the service provider''s VPC and each customer''s VPC.","Implement AWS PrivateLink, allowing customers to create interface VPC endpoints in their VPCs to connect to the service.","Establish AWS Direct Connect connections from each customer''s on-premises network to the service provider''s VPC."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C is the most secure, scalable, and cost-effective solution for this scenario. AWS PrivateLink allows the SaaS provider to expose their service privately to multiple customers. Customers can create interface VPC endpoints in their own VPCs, making the service appear as if it''s hosted directly within their network, without traversing the public internet or requiring complex VPC peering relationships. Option A (Internet Gateway) exposes the service to the public internet, which is not secure or private. Option B (VPC peering) becomes unmanageable and complex for a large number of customers due to CIDR block overlap concerns and the need for individual peering connections. Option D (AWS Direct Connect) is for connecting on-premises networks to AWS, not for VPC-to-VPC service access.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain21_4',
  NOW(),
  14,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_21_4',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────
-- Module: m_21_1
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_21_m_21_1_32',
  'A company stores vast amounts of historical log files in an Amazon S3 bucket. These logs are frequently accessed for the first 30 days for operational monitoring and troubleshooting. After this initial period, access becomes very infrequent, but the logs must be retained for compliance reasons for a total of 7 years. The company wants to minimize storage costs while ensuring data availability according to access patterns and retention policies.',
  'mcq',
  '["Keep all log files in Amazon S3 Standard storage for the entire 7-year retention period.","Enable Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering to automatically move objects between access tiers.","Store all log files in Amazon S3 Glacier Instant Retrieval from the moment they are uploaded.","Implement an S3 Lifecycle policy to transition objects to S3 Standard-IA after 30 days, then to S3 Glacier Deep Archive after 90 days, and finally delete after 7 years."]'::json,
  '{3}',
  'Option D is the most cost-effective solution for this scenario. An S3 Lifecycle policy allows for automated transitions of objects between different storage classes based on their age and access patterns. Transitioning to S3 Standard-IA after 30 days and then to S3 Glacier Deep Archive (or another Glacier tier like S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval) for long-term, infrequent access significantly reduces storage costs compared to keeping data in S3 Standard (ap-4.1-001). Option A (S3 Standard) is expensive for infrequently accessed data. Option B (S3 Intelligent-Tiering) is ideal for unpredictable access patterns but less optimal for a fixed, known lifecycle. Option C (S3 Glacier Instant Retrieval from upload) would incur retrieval costs if accessed frequently in the first 30 days and is not the most cost-effective for the entire 7-year period.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain21_4',
  NOW(),
  15,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_21_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_21_m_21_1_33',
  'A data analytics team ingests large volumes of raw data into an Amazon S3 bucket for various processing tasks. The access patterns for this data are highly unpredictable: some objects might be accessed frequently for a short period, then become infrequently accessed, and potentially become frequently accessed again later. The team needs to optimize storage costs without manual intervention or performance impact due to tiering.',
  'mcq',
  '["Enable Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering on the bucket to automatically move objects between frequent and infrequent access tiers.","Configure an S3 Lifecycle policy to transition objects to S3 Standard-IA after 30 days.","Keep all data in Amazon S3 Standard storage to ensure consistent high performance.","Store all raw data in Amazon S3 Glacier Instant Retrieval to minimize storage costs."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A is the most effective solution for unpredictable access patterns. Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering automatically moves objects between two access tiers (frequent and infrequent) based on changing access patterns, with no retrieval fees and minimal performance impact. This eliminates the need for manual lifecycle management and ensures cost optimization for unknown or changing access patterns (ap-4.1-003). Option B (S3 Lifecycle to S3 Standard-IA) is suitable for known, predictable access patterns, but not for unpredictable ones. Option C (S3 Standard) is more expensive for data that becomes infrequently accessed. Option D (S3 Glacier Instant Retrieval) is designed for data accessed rarely, not for unpredictable patterns that might involve frequent access.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain21_4',
  NOW(),
  16,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_21_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_21_m_21_1_34',
  'A company runs a critical database application on an Amazon EC2 instance that requires a block storage volume with consistent performance of 3,500 IOPS and 130 MiB/s throughput. The current volume type is `gp2`, and the company is looking to optimize costs while ensuring these specific performance requirements are met. The application is sensitive to latency.',
  'mcq',
  '["Migrate the volume to an `io2` Provisioned IOPS SSD volume type to guarantee performance.","Migrate the volume to a `gp3` General Purpose SSD volume type and provision the required IOPS and throughput.","Switch to an `st1` Throughput Optimized HDD volume type for better cost-effectiveness.","Use an `sc1` Cold HDD volume type for the lowest storage cost."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is the most cost-effective solution that meets the specified performance requirements. `gp3` volumes offer a baseline of 3,000 IOPS and 125 MiB/s throughput at no additional cost and can be provisioned up to 16,000 IOPS and 1,000 MiB/s throughput independently of storage size. Provisioning `gp3` to 3,500 IOPS and 130 MiB/s will be significantly more cost-effective than `io2`. Option A (`io2`) is significantly more expensive and should only be used when requirements exceed `gp3` limits (ap-4.1-002), which is not the case here. Option C (`st1`) and Option D (`sc1`) are HDD-based and do not meet the high IOPS and low-latency requirements of a critical database application.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain21_4',
  NOW(),
  17,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_21_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_21_m_21_1_35',
  'A company has a large archive of historical compliance documents that are rarely accessed, perhaps once or twice a year, but must be retrieved quickly when needed. These documents are currently stored on an Amazon EBS `gp2` volume attached to an Amazon EC2 instance that runs 24/7. The company wants to significantly reduce the storage costs for these archives, as they do not require block storage or POSIX file system semantics.',
  'mcq',
  '["Keep the documents on the Amazon EBS volume but switch to an `sc1` Cold HDD volume type.","Migrate the documents to an Amazon EFS file system for shared access.","Migrate the documents to Amazon S3 Glacier Instant Retrieval.","Store the documents in an Amazon S3 Standard bucket."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C is the most cost-effective solution for rarely accessed data that needs quick retrieval. Amazon S3 Glacier Instant Retrieval offers low-cost archival storage with retrieval times in milliseconds, which is ideal for data accessed once or twice a year. Storing infrequently accessed data on Amazon EBS (ap-4.1-004) is expensive because EBS charges per provisioned GB regardless of usage. Option A (EBS `sc1`) is still EBS and not as cost-effective as S3 Glacier for archival. Option B (Amazon EFS) is a file system for shared access, not optimized for cold archival. Option D (Amazon S3 Standard) is more expensive than Glacier Instant Retrieval for rarely accessed data.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain21_4',
  NOW(),
  18,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_21_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_21_m_21_1_36',
  'A research institution hosts a large public dataset in an Amazon S3 bucket that is frequently accessed by external researchers from around the world. The institution wants to make the dataset readily available but needs to ensure that the costs associated with data transfer and requests are borne by the external users who access the data, rather than by the institution itself.',
  'mcq',
  '["Configure the Amazon S3 bucket as a public bucket, allowing anonymous access.","Set up Amazon S3 Cross-Region Replication to distribute the dataset globally.","Distribute the dataset using Amazon CloudFront, configuring it to serve content from the S3 bucket.","Enable S3 Requester Pays on the Amazon S3 bucket."]'::json,
  '{3}',
  'Option D is the correct solution. Enabling S3 Requester Pays on an Amazon S3 bucket ensures that the requester, not the bucket owner, pays for the request and data transfer costs when accessing objects. This directly addresses the institution''s requirement to shift costs to external users. Option A (Public bucket) would mean the institution pays for all egress and request costs. Option B (Cross-Region Replication) is for data redundancy and local access, not for cost shifting. Option C (Amazon CloudFront) would still incur costs for the institution for CloudFront data transfer out to the internet, even if it improves performance.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain21_4',
  NOW(),
  19,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_21_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

