INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_21_m_21_1_37',
  'A data analytics company stores petabytes of historical log data in Amazon S3. This data is frequently accessed for the first 30 days for immediate analysis, then rarely accessed for the next 90 days for compliance audits, and finally needs to be archived for 7 years with infrequent, long-term retrieval requirements. The company wants to implement a solution that automatically transitions data between storage tiers to achieve the MOST cost-effective storage over the data''s entire lifecycle. Which S3 feature should be configured?',
  'mcq',
  '["Enable Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering for the bucket.","Apply an S3 Lifecycle policy to transition objects from S3 Standard to S3 Standard-IA, then to S3 Glacier Deep Archive.","Keep all data in Amazon S3 Standard storage class and manually move older data to S3 Glacier.","Use Amazon S3 One Zone-IA for all data to reduce costs from the start."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is correct. Applying an S3 Lifecycle policy to transition objects from S3 Standard to S3 Standard-IA, then to S3 Glacier Deep Archive, directly addresses the requirement for a known, tiered access pattern over time. This is the most cost-effective approach for data with predictable access changes. Option A (S3 Intelligent-Tiering) is ideal for unpredictable or changing access patterns (anti-pattern ap-4.1-003), but for known patterns, explicit lifecycle policies can be more cost-effective. Option C (keeping all data in S3 Standard and manual moves) is an anti-pattern (ap-4.1-001) as it''s not cost-optimized and requires manual effort. Option D (S3 One Zone-IA) is cheaper but offers lower durability and availability, and it doesn''t address the multi-stage lifecycle requirement.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain21_4',
  NOW(),
  11,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_21_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_21_m_21_1_38',
  'A company stores a large volume of customer-uploaded images in Amazon S3. The access patterns for these images are highly unpredictable; some images might be accessed frequently for a period, then rarely, and then become popular again. The company wants to minimize storage costs without impacting availability or requiring manual intervention to move data between storage classes. Which Amazon S3 storage class is MOST appropriate for this scenario?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon S3 Standard, as it provides high durability and availability for frequently accessed data.","Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering, which automatically moves objects between access tiers based on changing patterns.","Amazon S3 Standard-IA, designed for data that is accessed less frequently but requires rapid access when needed.","Amazon S3 Glacier Instant Retrieval, for long-lived, rarely accessed data with millisecond retrieval."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is correct. Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering is specifically designed for data with unknown or changing access patterns. It automatically moves objects between frequent access, infrequent access, and archive instant access tiers, optimizing storage costs without performance impact or retrieval fees. This directly addresses anti-pattern ap-4.1-003. Option A (S3 Standard) is more expensive for data that becomes infrequently accessed. Option C (S3 Standard-IA) is suitable for known infrequent access, but not for unpredictable changes. Option D (S3 Glacier Instant Retrieval) is for rarely accessed data with a higher retrieval cost compared to Intelligent-Tiering''s infrequent access tiers, and it''s not designed for automatically adapting to frequent access.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain21_4',
  NOW(),
  12,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_21_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_21_m_21_1_39',
  'A company needs to store critical application backups that are rarely accessed but must be retained for 10 years due to regulatory compliance. When retrieval is necessary, it can tolerate a few hours for the data to become available. The primary objective is to achieve the LOWEST possible storage cost for this long-term archival data. Which Amazon S3 storage class should be chosen?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon S3 Standard, due to its high durability and immediate access.","Amazon S3 Glacier Instant Retrieval, offering millisecond retrieval for archived data.","Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive, providing the lowest cost for long-term data archival.","Amazon S3 Standard-IA, for infrequently accessed data requiring rapid access."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C is correct. Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive is specifically designed for long-term data archival (7-10 years or more) at the lowest storage cost, with retrieval times typically within hours. This perfectly matches the requirement for 10-year retention with tolerance for multi-hour retrieval. Option A (S3 Standard) is significantly more expensive for long-term archival, relating to anti-pattern ap-4.1-001. Option B (S3 Glacier Instant Retrieval) offers millisecond retrieval but is more expensive than Deep Archive. Option D (S3 Standard-IA) is for infrequently accessed data that needs rapid access, but it''s not as cost-effective as Glacier Deep Archive for very long-term, rarely accessed data.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain21_4',
  NOW(),
  13,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_21_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_21_m_21_1_40',
  'A data processing application running on an EC2 instance requires a block storage volume for its temporary working files. These files are frequently read and written, but the application does not demand extremely high IOPS, with typical requirements around 2,500 IOPS and 100 MiB/s throughput. The company wants to select the MOST cost-effective EBS volume type that meets these performance needs. Which EBS volume type should be chosen?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon EBS Provisioned IOPS SSD (io2) to guarantee consistent high performance.","Amazon EBS General Purpose SSD (gp3) with default settings.","Amazon EBS Throughput Optimized HDD (st1) for its high throughput capabilities.","Amazon EBS Cold HDD (sc1) for its lowest cost per GB."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B is correct. Amazon EBS General Purpose SSD (gp3) with default settings provides a baseline of 3,000 IOPS and 125 MiB/s throughput at no additional cost. This easily meets the application''s requirements of 2,500 IOPS and 100 MiB/s, making it the most cost-effective choice. Option A (Provisioned IOPS SSD io2) is significantly more expensive and is only necessary when requirements exceed gp3 limits (up to 16,000 IOPS), representing anti-pattern ap-4.1-002. Option C (Throughput Optimized HDD st1) is designed for large, sequential workloads and is not optimized for the IOPS requirements of this application. Option D (Cold HDD sc1) is for very infrequently accessed data and would not meet the performance requirements for frequently read/written temporary files.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain21_4',
  NOW(),
  14,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_21_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

